        <p>-- The goodly addition the Government has already liiade to the peerage, amounting to a dozen, suggeas that it has ample faith in the stabi- I,tY of the privileged order. I may mention that ?ere is no intention on the port of the Cabinet to al with the constitution of the Upper House, in approaching session at least. If private peers introduce a Bill, Lord Salisbury will probably give the reception he gave Lord Rosebery's. Seven Years of liormonious co-operation between the two liaases will deaden the edge of the controversy ich has arisen, by which time also the electorate 'ill have undergone enormous modification by the geath of the old electors and the addition of a Y°.4riger generation to the roll. But the question id revive with every Liberal majprity in the gul R of Commons; and some day, when that sin ar pendulum of public impuls,A swings so .as return as big a Liberal majority as the Unionist 'lie of to-day, it will come up for settlement.</p>
        <p>Lois a licentiate of the Royal College of Physicians, y4,131:1011, or is he not, entitled to call himself a _e4Yeician ? This is a question which has been .411 ch discussed of late by medical men. The ' tatter, however, has now apparently been settled tt o°r Rood in the affirmative. It has just been pointed t that when the class of licentiates was estab- 'sits/led on its present basis the College consulted the atalstaing counsel to the College—Sir Richard Web- the present Attorney General—on this very reetion. He gave it as his opinion that those- on a horn the College conferred its license were in con- legally conc.tituted physicians. This does or, of course affect the other greatly debated point 4 the title of "doctor." A licentiate, although a hiPhYsician," is not allowed by the College to call inself"doctor," inasmuch as this latter title really 1111131 belongs to those who possess the degree of 13. of some University. id The announcement that the eldest daughter of JllStiCe Mathew is about to become the bride of t. John Oillon, M.P., is pre to be received by those Unionists who so bitterly attacked the °tried judge in connection with the Land Coin- kiss' lon as largely confirming the indecent t accusa- -1111 of bias and partiality. Ot course it is absurd- 1t then political prejudice often makes men for, tight injunction to abstain from setting down :got in malice. Lord Peel did not become tainted with Home Rule because his daughter married a d.°rne Ruler, and there is no reason why a judge's Cellßliter's choice of a husband should have any aring on her father's inflexible uprightness.</p>
