        <p>--- tilt'? well-informed Liberal circles it is believed h, When Mr. John Morley visits Newcastle next -4:l!3Utla he will take formal farewell of his old con- tUents. It is understood that he does not pro- P°Bls to again offer himself as a candidate for New- tie.</p>
        <p>Ity4lllona Mr. Barnato's particular friends in the c considerable amusement has been created by Bolernn announcement of a leading Conservative I,7llrnal that "Barney" was to be a Unionist candi- date for the East Torteth Division of Liverpool. b" fact is that Mr. Barnato is a strong Badical,and e"aa been all his life. Moreover, it is quite on the tilrcla that he will stand as a Radical candidate at lam election.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>alt Would seem that the policy of the Russian itovernment to acquire a considerable holding in Suez Canal Company is being quietly but 1,754i1Y carried Out. It is stated that when he p.as recently in France M de Witte, the Russian Iti•illtnee Minister, discussed the matter with M. 41bot and with several prominent Russo-French nild other banking houses, and that as a conse- rr„ence nearly £200,000 worth of stock has been idebased in their names. It will be transferred, ci•Rether with further purchases which may be or- (.1, into the name of the Russian Government, bug It is computed that by the close of the year the :`llBsians will have acquired shares to the amount of vtleast half a million sterling.</p>
        <p>t Seldotn has a literary man of any eminence en- „ered the House of Commons without losing his restige and standing, and sometimes even his liter- and philosophic influence. Members who are ,sill enough can still recall the derisive laughter and arguments with which the Tories greeted some John Stuart Mill's speeches, though these em- tried the most carefully thought-out conclusions t that great political thinker. It has not been edi- eing to see a man like Mr. John Morley engaged in kil.seted warfare with his Tory opponents, or to hear ?re taunted with his studies in the French Revolu- tilin• It is with misgiving, therefore that I hear cuatlfr. Lecky is allowing himself to be induced t 3 4111 e forward as a candidate for Dublin University. 4' Present his works are quoted in the House with e,eP respect, as containing the opinfbns of a great „ptical and historical authority. His speeches in :le Rouse, though -they would be marked by the ability would be turned upside down by every LbCal scio'list. At at the same time, men of his uteoilliancy and gifts are too rare in the House of tilleons. He would add a new lustre to its great tel?ates, and be a fit successor to one of the most 41,4hed orators—whose voice, however, was too Or heard—which the House possessed. Our</p>
