        <p>0,11„t 18 becoming very clear indeed as the time groaches when the Government will have to fll9)Ple with the question of the Voluntary schools wet they are in a position of extreme difficulty. ,views of Mr. George Dixon--whose past labours to course of education give him a special right et, heard upon the question—are known to be , 60,11. ed by a number of the Liberal Unionists and by th`me,.at least, of the Conservatives. At all events, ete 18 a feeling among a section of the Conserva- tmteB that the Government will be making a mis- etoLat the beginning of their career to bring a 4,t-o about their ears upon a question of this kind. to IS Pretty certain that the Vice President of the 44111161, who will have to take the responsibility, been throughout alive to the difficulties in the ; and, according to rumour, Sir 3. Gorst's I;.6endance at the Church Congress when the matter 444,8,diecussed has not strengthened his hope of 'lng a modus vivendi which will be fully accept- t o all Parties on the Unionist benches. Indeed, ,11e. Government were left to themselves it is q° able that the concessions to the Voluntary would be of a very moderate character. 41lt they have the Church party to contend with, the Church party have made it quite clear that t41:41 this matter, as on some others, they intend to eti,a an independent course. They will have the e°iect before them at their first meeting when the lo„11se assembles. It will not be surprising if before i),'R the Church party proves somewhat of a thorn the aide of the Government. The dominant ele- -11:,!1:t in the Government is not " Churchy " ; and. Islo„ung regard to the feelings of their influential lat.lonformist followers among the Liberal Union- IS evident that Ministers have special reasons t,e,ePing all disputed questions with regard to o ptt" urch very much in abeyance. But the Church teitdt3' i 8 numerous and well organised ; it has a for- -I,le able programme of its own, and it has not only it :eased its numbers in the present Parliament,but Vlifttrilrally flushed with the successful campaign tee:" 18 carried on against the Welsh Disestablish- Bill. Members of the type of Lord Cranborne, tiob Btance, are not likely to have much considera- lets; far the Nonconformist conscience as repre- v4od on the Treasury bench. The Church party itt all events, afford the House some lively edneaday afternoons in the coming session.</p>
