      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>HINT TO TELTOTALERS.</head>
        <p>A HINT TO TELTOTALERS.</p>
        <p>" I at last consented to try brandy," said a teetotal lady to a " Wilts County Mirror" interviewer ; " but I upset my temperance ideas for nothing." It is very often so. In the supposed interests of health, people will break through the habits of a life- time. Those accustomed to take a moderate stimulant deprive themselves of it ; strict teetstalers suffer them- selves to take as a medicine what they would rather leave untouched. The wiser plan is to adhere to the habits of a lifetime.</p>
        <p>The lady interviewed, Miss E. B. Burden, of Tisbury, Wiltshire,°continued : " For quite five years I suffered acutely from neuralgia and nervousness. Pain was constantly with me, and my friends could see that my agony was intense. Sleep was out of the question. I used to toss about in bed, longing for the morning, and I must confess that I sometimes felt it would be a happi- ness to be dead. My appetite was completely gone ; I always had a languid and giddy feeling; and lifeseemed hardly worth living. I tried all sorts of remedies ; and although I have always been a strong teetotaler, I at last consented to try taking brandy ; but I upset my tem- perance ideas all for nothing."</p>
        <p>Miss Burden then went into the Great Northern Hospital in London, but to use her own expression, " couldn't get well."</p>
        <p>Eighteen months ago a friend said to her, " Why not try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People ?. They have done me a wonderful amount of good." Miss Bur- den consented. " And," said she to the reporter, with a face beaming with gratitude, " I had been but a very little time taking the Pills before I felt better. When I took one box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I felt I was cured, 'and when I had taken the second box I was sure of it. My friends will tell you I was red aced to a shadow, and like a walking ghost. Since 'taking Dr. Williams' Pills I feel strong and cheerful,take a pleasure in life, have a good appetite, [sleep well, and can now do work that a couple of years ago would have killed me."</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cure Neuralgia, rheumatism, locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, and indigestion ; diseases of the blood, such as scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and restore pale and sallow com- plexions to the glow of health. They are a specific for all tho troubles peculiar to the female sox, and in men cure all cases arising from worry, overwork,or excesses. Sold by all chemists, and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9cl. Genuine only with full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ENGLISH CRICKETERS IN AUSTRALIA</head>
        <p>ENGLISH CRICKETERS IN AUSTRALIA</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the members of the Melbourne Cricket Club was held on September 14,when Mr. Frank Smith, the president, submitted the report of the com- mittee, which stated that as a result of the visit of Mr. Stoddart's team of Edglish cricketers the club had bene- fited to the extent of £3,349, after allowing for all ex, penses, and a contribution of £250 to the Melbourne charities. The committee also stated that the visit of Mr. Stoddart's eleven possessed none but the most grati- fying recollections. They would ask the members to unite in passing a hearty vote of thanks in appreciation of the valued services rendered by that combination to the advancement of cricket in Victoria,and to the other colonies in which the eleven played,and they would also ask the meeting to elect the amateur members of that eleven who were not already honorary life members f the Melbourne Cricket Club to that distinction. These gentlemen were Messrs. F. G. J. Ford, L. H. Gay, A. C. Maclaren, and H. Philipson. and anticipating that will- ing recognition would be accorded to them,the commit- tee had already presented them with gold badces of honorary life membership. In speaking of the report, the Chairman said that the terms of high appreciation in which the English eleven were alluded to were well deserved, and opportune corroboration of the fact was shown by Mr. Stoddart's eleven having just succeeded in defeating the strongest combination in England. The Melborne Cricket Club had not been slow to recognize this, and a cable message had already been despatched to Mr. Stoddart congratulating him upon his victory. The report and balance sheet were adopted, and motions were carried to the effect that ,the committee should be empowered to invite another English team to visit Australia in the near future, and also that the action of the committee in making the amateur members of the last eleven life members of the club be ratified.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>SALES AND CURES PROVE THE MERIT OF HOOD'S SARSAPARILLA.</head>
        <p>SALES AND CURES PROVE THE MERIT OF HOOD'S SARSAPARILLA.</p>
        <p>The enormous sales of Hood's Sarsaparilla and its wonderful cures prove indisputably even more than has ever been claimed for the medicine, and give it a clear right to the title of the one true blood puri- fier prominently in the public eye to-day. We chal- lenge any medicine to show an appreciation at home like that which ha been accorded Hood's Sarsa- parilla. Such results as have attended the use of Hood's Sarsaparilla could come only from absolute merit, and the steadily increasing demand unmis- takably proves that the people appreciate Hood's Sarsaparilla. GIVES US STRENGTH.</p>
        <p>WEAKENED SYSTEM GREATLY BENEFITED BY HOOD'S SARSAPARILLA. "Gentlemen,—My husband has taken Hood's Vegetable Pills, and finds they do him a lot of good, and he strongly recommends them to those suffering from headache and tired feeling. Seeing the names of the-well-known herbs used in Hood's Sarsaparilla in one of your circulars, I was induced to try Hood's Sarsaparilla. and found it relieved the dreadful nervous pains I was suffering from,pro- needing from a diseased spine and nerves. After I had taken it a few days I gave it to my husband who was suffering from a ruptured spine bone. After he had taken it two days he began to feel much better, and we are still taki4g it. It helps us daily through our daily work." Mrs. M. A. MOORE, 4, Garfield Place, Hailsham, Sussex.</p>
        <p>MADE A PERFECT CURE.</p>
        <p>"I recommend Hood's Olive. Ointment and Hood's</p>
        <p>Sarsaparilla for sores. I had a sore on my nose for nearly three months. I tried everything,but nothing did me any good. It nearly covered one side. One night in the middle of August a man called at our house with books and papers, and I read one, which was all about Hood's Sarsaparilla. I sent for a bottle of the medicine and a box of Hood's Olive Ointment, and after I had used it I was completely cured." ANDREW BAILEY, Sefter Farm, Foghorn, North Bognor, Sussex. HOOD'S SARSAPARILLA.</p>
        <p>Sold by all chemists, or sent post paid, 2s. 9d. or 4s. 6d., by C. I. Hood and Co., 34, Snow Hill, Loaden, E.C. HOOD'S PILLS do not cause pain or gripe, but act gently and thoroughly. Is. LK (No. 4).</p>
        <p>The autumnal meetings of the Peace Society were held at Birmingham on Monday. A resolution affirming that the present aspect of Europe in relation to the burden of its vast armaments, to the serious moral evils in- volved by its huge standing armies, and to the awfully destructive nature of modern warfare should be regarded as constituting a strong appeal to maintain an itureas- ing delity to the principles of peace, and calling upon all Governments and all nations to make every effort to promote justice and goodwill in all their relations to one another, was passed. Another resolution congratulated the friends of the peace on the growing frequency with which the principle of arbitration has been applied to the settlement of international difficulties.</p>
