      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>EXAMINER. SATUR I UY, OCTOBEE 26 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I think you asked me to tell you what shape people are wearing in hats and other headgear, so I will hold forth. In felt hats, then, one of the nicest and most generally becoming shapes has a rather low crown and a wide _brim slightly turned up all round, but rolled away much more decidedly above the face. This in pale grey felt with a trim- ming of bows of striped ribbon and outspread wings in front, a simple twist of the former going round the crown, is in very good taste, and quite useful too. One specimen shown me lately had no fewer than six black wings—not very small ones either—standing out in various directions amid loops of green and white striped ribbon ; but for the wear of ordinary people a single pair of wings would be less conspicuous, and consequently more desirable, or the wings may be dispensed with altogether, loops of rather wide ribbon with a handsome steel or jet buckle in the centre, together with one of the fashionable white-tipped ospreys, being sufficient trimming. Have you seen the new mushroom hats. They are made mostly in Tuscan straw, and a pretty trimming is blush roses outside and underneath</p>
        <p>the brim; a large and prominent black and white aigrette towering above the flowers. I see that large, comfortable cloaks of reversible tweed or cloth are beginning to be worn. These are most comfortable, look undeniably smart, and, as they are easily made at home with the aid of a pattern, are quite inexpensive. An excellent design is a three-quarter length cloak, with caps, and high collar. It can well be cut from three yards-of cloth. Where the material is reversible, the make is simplicity itself, for once cut and fitted, it only remains to turn up a deep hem on to the plaid lining, and when this is nicely pressed it can be finished with two or three rows of machine. stitching in tailor style. The collar is made on a firm,..but not too stiff canvas, and the outer edge is much-stretched, and that joining on to the cape is sloped..inwards, and also shrunk with a damp rag and hot iron, and this certainly requires some little skill. It has three darts on each shoulder, and these are deep and throw a great deal of fulness over the arm, while another dart in the middle of the back throws out the centre of the cape.</p>
        <p>Here is a splendid long blue cloth coat. It has bishop sleeves and big black velvet buttons, and is smartened by a black velvet collar trimmed with blue fox fur. A far handsomer carriage cloak, quite fit to do duty for the theatre, is a long black silk cloak, trimmed with blue fox down the front</p>
        <p>and round the hanging sleeves, and with a collar at the neck. It is lined throughout with squirrel.</p>
        <p>My next description is that of a rather frivolous- looking dress, a chic toilette I saw recently in Paris, and which I thought the very thing for a quiet evening dress. It was made of pink and green " mother-of-pearl " glace ; those eaft shell tints that are so indescribable, and the only trim- ming was the two strips of lace insertion at each side of the box-pleat in the rather full bodice, and the ehemisette and ruffle of white chiffon. The sleeves reach to the elbow only, and when pre- ferred, the cheinisette could, of course, be dispensed with, leaving a " demi "bodice.</p>
        <p>As I have told you before, nearly all this season's jackets are adorned freely with fur. A good material for the new well-fitting coats is West-of- England cloth, in either colours or black, though the latter, I think, looks the best. One I saw the other day in this material was lined with silk throughout, and the fur was beaver, but either mink or Persian lamb would look quite as well. The sleeves were very wide to the elbow, below which they were, of course, tight. The beaver was</p>
        <p>used for the collar and immense revers, and on the cuffs. There was no trimming of it down the front of the jacket, but this is not wanted where the revers are entirely of fur. Disposed in this fashion it looks awfully well, I think. .</p>
        <p>A great cause of the muddy and coarse skins that are seen is the too generous use of water and soap. Both of these are excellent used in modera- tion, but, like all good things, may be overdone. The face should be washed well every night, to remove the deleterious effects of powder, and to permit af a free perspiration during the night, and then in the morning a cold douche should be in- dulged in. During the day avoid washing with water. A far more effective and cleanly practice is to dab the face well with sweet oil, wiping it off thoroughly afterwards, and dusting with a good powder, such as Veloutine. You will be surprised to see how fresh and clean your complexion looks after this treatment, which also has the merit of keeping the skin soft and pliable, and prevents the dry yellow tint which is the sign of departed youthfulness.</p>
        <p>• • ,(,•104.0, gtlot , -ter _ •</p>
