      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Your loving JENNIL</head>
        <p>Your loving JENNIL</p>
        <p>The furbelsw was at first separate from the dress,and a distinct article of apparel. At the Thames Police Court, on Wednesday a man named William Stevens was committed for trial for steal- ing a horse and van—the latter containing coffee and indigo—belonging to the London and North-Western Railway Company. The property, which was valued at £6BO, was taken possession of by the prisoner in the temporary absence of the driver, in circumstances simi- lar to those of the recent silver robbery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>Miyako Yujiro, a Japime-0 journalist has discovered that ata moui Chinese poem, written several centuries azo by Liu Ting Che, bears a remarkable resemblance " The Last L-af," by Oliver Wendell Holmes. Jay Gould's estate has been apprised at about sixteen million pounds odd. As the report was made as a basis on which to collect the collateral inheritance tax, it is not likely the value of the property has been over- es imated.</p>
        <p>...... "To-day "says :—" I heard a modern dramatic cri- tic observe one thy with perfect truth, " I must be al- ways wrong to my life's end. If I conscientiously praise an actreQs, I must have kissed her. If I find fault with , her actin2, she puts it about that I wanted to kiss her, but she objected.' " Aluminium neckties have been introduced into Ger- many. They are frosted or otherwise ornamented in various shapes imitating the ordinary silk or satin arti- cle. They are fastened to the collar button, or by a band around the neck,and are particularly recommended for summer wear, since they can be easily cleaned when soiled, while they are not perceptibly heavier than cot- ton, cambric or silk. Smoothing irons were first used in France, and are supposed to have been a French invention, being intro- duced in the sixteenth century. After the introduction of starch, linens were first made smooth by pressure, being starched and placed b ..ween two boards. This being found not to give the best results,resort was next had to pressure with a cold flat-iron, and finally the iron was heated to impart the polish now considered indis- peneible. Miss Lottie Collins, saveithat her American manager, Mr. Charles Frohmann, treated her most royally during her tour in the States. "When he found what an at- traction I was proving, and how I drew in the dollars,he immediately doubled my salary, aed when we went touring and I objected to the one-night • stands,' he promptly offered me two hundred dollars a week extra to compensate me for the additional fatigue. That brought my earnings to just on two hundred pounds a week," she added, "and as I was there to make all I could, I accepted his offer. You see a young woman must look out for the ' cash ' ; it's money we all want, and if I don't get it now I never shall." A correspondent in Vallebuia, Italy, writes to Woman: —" Ouida, the authoress, leads a most retired life in this lovely neighbourhood. She never receives now, but is occasionally to be seen walking about the lanes escor- ted by her six dogs, of whom she is pansionately fond. More generally, though, she prefers to sit or walk'in her own grounds. Her villa is large and handsome,with a magnificent view from the windows. She stood at her gate the other evening dressed in an old-fashioned-look- ing full white muslin gown, trimmed with handsome lace, but alas ! the lace was badly torn by the beloved dogs. That, however, she minds not at all, and says with a smile. "They only make little triangles in it." She wore a tiny white shawl, white shoes, and black stockinge,and had on what looked like a reddish wig. She still writes, and thought of going ta England in July,but gave up the idea." Any of Mr. Hall Caine's wide circle of friends who gets hold of the New York " World " for September 26 will be amused to see the way in which. the Yankee in- terviewer dealt with him. First comes the personal description, aided by the grossest caricatures of the novelist, his wife, and son that even an American daily could perpetrate. Those who know Mr. Caine will be surprised to learn that he struck the " World's " re- porter as" a little man," but will be reassured by the additions " rather like a bundle of nerves," and " about his personality there is a strong flavour of genius." Mrs. Caine is let down as " a pretty little English- woman," and altogether "the novelist made a pretty domestic picture." When he removed his slouch hat he "showed long hair brushed back ala Pompadour." Of this hair the reporter remarks, "It has the shade of maple sugar, and is getting rather thin on the top.' Further we read, " He had an enormous collar, with a turn-down flap at least three inches deep. . . He could wear a collar at least five sizes smaller without choking. His white necktie was tied so carelessly thaw the knot part of it hung at least three inclus south of the collar button."</p>
        <p>French Wiveg.</p>
        <p>Under the heading of " A Study in Wives," the cur- rent number of the "North American Review" contains a ieries of articles by writers of various nationalities, purporting to give a true character sketch of the typical married women of the respective countries from which the writers hail. Mr. Max O'Rell gives us a charming picture of the French wife. " Her constant atm is," he tells us, "to be interesting to her husband. She multi- plies herself. In turn she is his friend, his confidant,his partner in business, his chum, and, if I may use the word in its best and most relined sense, his mistress. She is for ever changing her appearance. For instance, you will seldom see a French married woman wear her hair in the same way longer than three or four weeks. She kncws that love feeds on trifles, on illusion, on sug- gestion. She knows that, when a man loves his wife, a rose in her hair, a new frock, a bonnet differently trim- med, will rev;ve in him the very emotion that he felt when he held her in his arms for the first time. She also knows that the very best dishes may sometimes be- come insipid if always served with the same sauce. She understands to a supreme degree the poetry of matri- mony. I have heard men say that matrimony kills poetry. The fools There is no poetry outside of it, and the poetry has all the more chance to live long in French matrimonial life because our wedding ceremony is not, as in England, tke end of courtship, but only the beginning of it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MILLIONAIRE'S SON KILLED.</head>
        <p>A MILLIONAIRE'S SON KILLED.</p>
        <p>Mr. John W. Mackay, eldest son of Mr. John Mackay, the well-known Californian millionaire, was accidentally killed on Friday while riding in the neighbourhood of Paris. Mr. Mackay was only 25 years of age, and had a large circle of European acquaintances, with whom he was exceedingly popular. The accident occurred under the following circum- stances : Mr. Mackay had rented from the Duke of Grammont his chateau and grounds at Mayet, in the department of the Sarthe, and had established a race- course with hurdles and ditches in order to test the qualities of a number of horses he had lately purchased in Faris. On Friday afteraoon,Mr.Mackay,accompanied by;two friends, Messrs. Lynch and Digby, visited the ground, and Mr.Mackay,contrary to Mr. Lynch's advice, mounted a particularly restive horse which Mr. Lynch had tried the previous day. Mr. Mackay had hardly been ten minutes in the saddle when the animal bolted, left the track, and rushed into a neighbouring thicket. At first the rider successfully dodged several treeq,but at last, according to the statement of an eye-witners, the exertion proved too much for him, and, losing all control over the horse, he was seen to reel in the saddle and finally to collide with a fearful crash against a tree,strik- ing his forehead and knee. The impact was so viol.-nt that he was knocked off the animal's back. His two friends and other visitors rushed to the spot, where he lay insensible, and applied restoratives, but to no avail, and they eventually carried him home.</p>
        <p>A doctor arrived. soon afterwards, and in about three hours Mr. Mackay recovered consciousness. He presented a ter; ible appearance, as both eyes had been crushed. He had repeated collapses, and expired at half-past nine. after having bidden farewell to and pressed the hands of all his friends, whom he recognised by their voices. His last parting was with his dog. Mr. Lynch came at once to Paris to see Mr. Clarence Mackay, whom he dissuaded from going to the Castle. Mrs. Mackay left on Friday for a tour in Normandy, but she has been summoned by telegraph, and was expected back in Paris on Sunday night, when the sad news of Mr.Mackay's death would be broken to her. No member of Mr. Mackay's family was present at his death-b ed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>UNITED KINGDOM ALLIANCE.</head>
        <p>THE UNITED KINGDOM ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>The General Council of the United Kingdom Alliance held its annual meeting in Manchester on Tuesday. Sir Wilerid Lawson, who presided, endorsed the Bishop of London's view that a crisis had been reached in the history of the temperance movement, and said it was for the Alliance to rise to the height of its opportu- nity. Many people thought the Alliance was a total abstinence society. It was neither that net yet a tern- peranc3 society. It was an organisation that existed for the everthrow of the liquor traffic. Sir William Har- court brought in a Bill which substantially and generally carried but the policy they desired. There were blemishes in the measure, but no leading statesman had &amp;broader grip of the policy of local veto than Sir Wil- liam Harcourt. The party which incorporated the policy of the Alliance in a Bill was thoroughly beaten at the polls, but if it had not gone in for the local veto it would not merely have been beaten, but would have been pulverised to atoms. Even if the ,local veto had been the cause of the defeat, the Liberal party would surely never think of throwing over a just measure merely because it did not catch votes. The other sneakers included Mr. W. 8. Caine, Mr, Samuel Pope, Q.C., Mr. T. P. Whittaker. M.P., Mr. Herbert Roberts. M.P., and Mr. H. J. Wilson, M.P., by all of' whom gratification was expressed that the policy of the Alli- ance occupied so forward a position in the politics of the day. The meeting of the Council was followed in the evening by a great public meeting in the Free Trade Hall, at which addresses were delivered by Sir Wilfrid Lawson, who presided, Mr. Lloyd-George, M.P., Mr. R. Cameron. M.P., anal several of the members of Parlia- ment and others who had taken part in the meeting of the General Council.</p>
        <p>ISLE OF Max IMPROVEMENTS. Permission has been granted for the construction of an electric or mechanical tramway or lift from the bridge connecting Douglas town with Douglas Head (now in course of construction) to the commencement of the lines of the Douglas South- ern Electric Tramways, Limited, whose prospectus has been advertised in the "Examiner." This tramway or lift w ill he carried out at once,and will run in connec- tion with the Douglas Southern lines, thereby making a practically continuous line from Douglas to Port Soderick. We are also advised that a company is being formed for the purpose of supplying amusements along the tramway route.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>There is now every probability that a contest will take place in every ward in the town at the forth- coming municipal elections. Councillor Beaman during the present week has issued his address for Bewsey ward, and Councillor Greening, who was expected to retire from the Council will after all be the Conservative candidate for Latchford. Coun- cillor R. T. Fairclough will not be allowed a walk over in the Fairfield ward for the Liberals have chosen a candidate in Mr. David Duncan, of Padgate-lane, who will make a very g3od fight. Mr. Duncan is well-known in the ward, having been connected with the Wagon Works of the Cheshire Lines Railway Company. Mr. Fairclough has never yet fought the ward so that he is by no means certain of re-election. Mr. David Tinnion has gallantly come forward as the champion for the St. Austin's ward, and since his determination to the contest the seat was formed he has lost no time in commencing with his electioneering work. Al- though the seat is at present held by the Couserva- tives,Mr. Tinnion will undoubtedly:be:a formidable opponent to Mr. Gill. The Liberals as yet have not brought out a candidate for the Howley ward, but Dr. Cannel' will most probably have to fight for his seat. In the meantime, the candidates are vigor- ously prosecuting their canvass, setting their views before the ratepayers. The Conservatives appear to have some difficulty in securing a candidate for &amp;John's ward,to take the place of the retiring candidate,Mr.liill.Alr.Furness has in the meantime been prosecuting his candidature with every promise of success A meeting of the Liberal Latchford Ward Com- mittge was held in the Primitive Methodist school- room, Knutsford-road, on Friday night, under the presidency of Mr. W.Bibby. Mr. Luke Ellison,boot- maker, Latchford, was unanimously chosen as the candidate to contest the ward.</p>
        <p>THE NOMINATIONS. The following nominations have been received. ST. AUSTIN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>William Gill (G.) proposed by Thomas Starkey Smith, seconded by Arthur Browne, and assented to by David Garnett, William John Forshaw, John Walton, John Mainwaring, James Scotson, Edward Booth, Charles Twiss and Hugh Fergusson.—ln a second paper, Mr. Gill is proposed by John Edward Wright, seconded by Edward Greenall, and assented to by John Chidlaw, William Chantry, Robert Hughes Dainteth, John Skelton, Richard Dainteth, John Heath, Thomas Lane and William Rigby—and in a third paper, Mr. Gill is pro- posed ov John Percival Taylor, seconded by William Pickstone,and assented to by William Butterton, William John Bloomfield, James Massey, Thomas Howard, Ashcroft, Arthur Charles Lazanby, Arthur James Allen, John Cox and Joseph Parsons.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD. ROBERT HEATON (C), contractor, is proposed by Robert Davies. seconded by Edward Crofton Ellis Car- leton, and assented to by Alfred Woods,William Strettle, John Greeney,Joseph Shillcock,James Henry Smethurst, George Armstrong Ward, Alexander Baird, and James Brown—Also proposed by James Smethurst,seconded by Alfred John Jolley, and assented to by Ephrain Auklan-d Joseph Ryder Blease' James Wood, Alfred Thornley, George Starkey, Richard Silk, John Pugh, and John Hughes.</p>
