        <p>CONSERVATIVE MEE I'l NO.-SUGGESTED COMPROMISE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Conservative Association was held on Friday night under the presidency of Mr. John White. It was decided to contest every ward unless the chairrnan,vice-chairman,and secretary of the association should come to an agreement with the Liberals with regard to the representation of the wards. It was also suggested that Latchford, Whitecross, Bewsey, and Howley wards be uncontested.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week eliding 12th October, 1895. During the week, which was the 41st of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths Warrington 3B 31 Latchford 4</p>
        <p>Total 3B</p>
        <p>The excess of deaths over births was 3. The death rate from all causes was equal to 31.8 per 1,000, against 38-6 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 167 the corresponding week of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to 18.2 per 1,000, against 20.4 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 2.7 the corresprlnding week of 1894. The ages of mortality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 15 16 3 1 35</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from scarlet fever, 7 from measles, 12 from diarrhea, 3 from lung disease, 3 from brain disease, 1 from old age, 4.from debility.</p>
        <p>, The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Szarlet fever, 9 cases. enteric 2 cases. Total number of patients in hospital on Oct. 12th-41. METEOROLOGL—The mean temperature was 49.9 Fah., and therefore 6.6 degrees lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 61 F., was recorded on the 6th inst., and the mimimum temperature 39 F., on the 6th Oct. inst. __</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured .33 inch. The general direc- tion of the wind was W. The reading of the Anemo- meter denoting 635 miles. JOHN 11. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justin McCarthy made his first appearance in print in the pages of the "Cork Magazine," which was started by his father. "It was a love story," he tells us in the course of an interview in the "Young Man" and the scene was laid in the Blackwater. I forget the title of the story, and I forget what became of the lovers,but I remember that one of the characters in the book was 'Mr. Parnell.' Ido not know why I chose that name' and I think of it now as a singular coincidence."</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say Crltchley's " STARCH GLCEIS Is unequalled for imparting a beautiful finish to starched linen, ecc., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss." From Mrs. Bradford : " Dear sir.— I am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Po!lshes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Bold at Id., 3d., and Bd., to most starch sellers. If not in stock will procure it. T ORITCHLET. Blackburn. Ptoprietor .—Adet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DEATH OF A WELL-KNOWN</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A WELL-KNOWN</p>
        <p>WA RRINGTONIAN.</p>
        <p>---- 1 A well-known resident in the neighbourhood of Warrington passed away on Friday afternnon at 1.15, in the person of Mr. William Ro,- 11, of Darlington Lodge, adjoining the Twiggeiy Farm, Manchester-road. The deceased gentleman was la his usual health on Thursday night, when lie played at bagatelle, and also indulged in sipigiug. At about a quarter to six on Friday morning he was seized with a stroke, and though Dr. Rich- mond was called in, he never recovered conscious- ness, and passed quietly away at the time above stated. Mr. Robinson was born in a village in Flintshire in 1820, and was therefore 75 years of age. When he attained the years of maturity, he set up in business in Crewe as a miller, and was so successful in business that in 1869 he retired, and came to live at Darlington Lodge, Warrington, where he has resided ever since. He has been married twice,and leaves a widow but no children, two children having died young. Mr. Robinson was well-known as a militant atheist, and the window and walls of his house bear record of his attempts to convert the passers-by to his views. The funeral will take place at Warburton old church, probably on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MARRYING ON TEN SHILLINGS A WEEK.</head>
        <p>MARRYING ON TEN SHILLINGS A WEEK.</p>
        <p>At the Crewe Bankruptcy Court on Wednesday, befere Mr. Registrar Speakman, Francis William Bellringer, grocer and general dealer, of Crowton, Northwich, at- tended for his public examination. Mr. T. Bullock (Official Receiver) examined the bankrupt,who admitted that he had,, been insolvent for some time. In addition to his business he held the position of sub-postmaster, for which he received £5 a year. Asked as to his turn- over, be said he had taken as much as £2 or £3 a week, but at the start his turnover was oily about £1 a week. He devoted his whole time to the business. His profit ' was about 25 per cent, of his turnover.—The Official Receiver : How did you manage to live on ss. or 103, a week ? The bankrupt replied that he managed to live somehow. He looked after the house himself and cnoked his own meals. Then he married. He had only about 10s. a week, and 4s. of that went for house rent.—The Official Receiver : Practically you could not live on 10s.a week yourself, to say nothing of keeping your wife. The bankrupt said they had lived somehow.—Mr. Bullock said they had evidently been living on tkeir creditors.— The examination was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SHIPBUILDERS' DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>THE SHIPBUILDERS' DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>The representatives of the Clyde and Belfast ship- builders and of the operative engineers employed by them, met in conference at Carlisle on Wednesday to discuss the terms proposed for the settlement of the existing dis- pute. The meeting which was a private one, lasted seven hours, and when it terminated a statement was made to the effect that the Glasgow engineers had ac- cepted the offer of a farthing an hour to all under seven- pence,but that they were unable to accept the condition by which the offer was accompanied, v;z , that there should be no change in the wages of the men for six months without notice. A resolutioa had been passed confirming the offer subject to the mutual adjustment of the condition previously stipulated. With regard to the position at Bel "ast, a resolution had been adopted ex- pressing . the opinion that any advance at Belfast was entirely unwarranted in the present state of trade, but that the Joint Committee ware prepared to recommend that, provided the men now on strike returned to work within a week, an advance of a farthing an hour should be conceded in Match next, if the state of trade then warranted it, on condition that the change shall remain in force six months. The operative delegates will at , once proceed to lay these proposals before the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>MORE ABOUT STOKES'S MURDERER.</head>
        <p>MORE ABOUT STOKES'S MURDERER.</p>
        <p>A STARTLING STORY.</p>
        <p>Thomas Bradley, a fireman, recently returned from West Africa, bearing a letter from Mr. Alan B. Stokes, the brother of the murdered African trader, relates in the columns of the " Greenock Telegrap'l" a story of his adventures in the Congo district. In reply to whether he had met the unfortunate Mr. Stokes, he said " Yes I came in contact several times with him, I heard of Lothaire's jealousy of Stokes and I heard from Bishop Taylor, the American missionary at Stanley Pool, that Lothaire had eften oxhibited jealousy towards Stokes on account of his superior abilities in dealing with the natives. Stokes was at one time a missionary, and was conversant with nearly all the native tongues. I heard him once give a sermon in the Swahili language in Bis- hop Taylor's church. I also heard Stokes declare to Bishop Taylor that Lethaire was jealous of him because the natives traded more with him than they did with Lothaire, and because he would not bribe Lothaire in the same way as that practised by other traders. Stokes was the means of diverting much trade from the Belgian to the English factories, and thus the jealousy of Loth- sire would be accounted for. Br both the black and white people Stokes was much liked, while Lothaire bore a bad character, even among his own people. For nay comrades in the work of overseeing the black navvies I had two Norwegians and an American, all of , us being in the employment of the Belgian Government. Becoming dissatisfied with the treatment we were receiv- ing from the Belgian Government,and more particularly being displeased at the poor quality of the food given , us—which was nearly as wretched as that supplied to 11 slaves—we made a ri.quest for our wages, and were told 1 by Captain LothairP, who was commander of the sta- tion, that our payments would be made at Boma. Ws then proceeded down the river for Boma en board the Government steam r.iinch, Captain Lothair accompany- ing us, and on reaching Boma we once more made an application for payment. From the natives, the two Norwegians, the Arn, rican, knd myself procured a hut, and this we converted into our residence. We bad not the slightest suspicion of foul play, and were greatly alarmed about four o'clock one morning on having our slumbers disturbed, and awakening to find our frail habitation in flames. But for the warning given by one of the Norwegians we would all have suffered a frightful death, and this dastardly act was the york of Lothaire ' and four of his black policemen. Picking up what articles of clothing we could lay hands on, we quickly made our escape from the burnine. tent. Outside we encountered Lothaire, and the American accosting hire said "Is this all our thanks? Is this all the pay we are to get for our work ?" Without a moment's hesitation Lothaire drew his sword, and with the flat of it struck him on the back, and ordered his native policemen to lay hands on hjm. The poor fellow was immediately seized and fastened by the arms round a tree by little leather thongs used for handcuffs. This accomplished, Lothaire himself commenced slashing the bound man with his sword, cutting the clotheefrom (If his back and wound- ing him terribly. The Norwegians and I were specta- tors of this frightful erueltr." " Why did you not in- terfere ?"—lnterfere ! Any interference on our part would at once have been resented by the native police under the orders of Lothaire, about a couple of hundred of whom were present. At the earliint opportunity we cut down our bruised companion and carried him to the outskirts of the town. Here, under the shelter of the tall grass, we did, tinder the circumstances, what we I could for him, and endeavoured to alleviate his agony. His power of speech was gone, end he was apparently in great pain. After lingering for two days. he died. With our hands we scooped out a grave in a cassava plantation, into which we placed his remaias. The desire for revenge was strong within WI, and not one of us would have hesitated to put a bullet into Loth3ire,but so well guarded was he at all times that no suitable opportunity presented itself. At Boma there was no authority to whom we eould appeal, so we left it with the intention of reporting the matter whenever the chance occurred. In the darkness of the night we (that is, I and the two Norwegians) stole one of the Govern- ment canoes, which had wheels worked by the feet, and proceeded about forty miles down the river to Banana Creek, and from there to Black point (Puntariegra) on foot. I returned to Sette C mama, and subsequently got to Loango. From here I WaS sent on by steamor,with a letter from the French Consul to the British Government agent at Old Calabar. Here I met Major Macdonald [Sir Claude Macdonald), and informed him of the treat- ment of the American by Lot haire. and also related to him the murder of the two Norwegians. but he refused to accept reeponsibility,as the outrages bad not occurred within his territory. From Old Calabar I got a passage home to Liverpool, went to my home in county Meath for a few weeks, and now I am in Greenock seeking a ship."</p>
        <p>"WHOSE VERY DEIST IS EISTOR</p>
        <p>Macedonia is covered with the ruins of historic monu- ments, acme of whim may yet be made to tell the rale of that land of lost gods and godlike men. Near the pic- turesque lake Yenidshe,in the Payik Planina, once stood Pella, the seat of government of Philipi of Macedon and the birthplace of Alexander the Great, later .7.. n one of the most important stations on the Roman Via Ematia, and to-day a pestiferous marsh wherein are bred deadl fsvers felt as far away as Salonika. If the fields and roads, the hills and valleys of this ancient land, whose very dust is historic,could be made to tell the chequered story of the noble and base deeds done or conceived by the various races of men who lived and worked here since Perdikkas in the seventh century, 8.c., founded the Macedonian kingdom and the dynasty of the Augeads, since Philip consolidated his empire wad I Alexander conquered the world, through the Greek and Persian wars, the Roman occupation, the Slav invasion, the West Bulgarian Empire, and the agonies of Byzance down to the present titre-, when lust-, murder, and all manner of uncleanness stalk shamelessly through the land, and the shrieks of violated women and the groans of tortured men are swallowed up by the death-like silence which the Turks have established and called peace, what a thrilling narrative would be prese,ted to the world!But history and Macedonia are incompatible terms. It is well-nigh impossible to learn with an approach to accuracy what is going on there to-day, much less what has taken place since the Bulgars or Volgars, having quitted their settlements on the banks of the Volga, entered the country in the last quarter of the seventh century, A.D., adopted the language of the Slav people, and gave them in return their name. The de- scendants of these two races, the Bulgars and the Slays, constitute the predominent element of the popu- lation of contemporary Macedonia ; and the sad story of their sad sufferings is writ as large in their physical, psychical, and political condition as that of their coun- try is in the dust of its palaces, the broken pillars of its temples, the crumbling walls of its fortrgsses, and the utter neglect and decay of everything created by art or nature which depends for existence upon the fostering care of man. The Macedonians are the wreck of a once great and warlike people. All we know about their lives and sufferings, their hopes, fears and aspira- tions, comes to Lis from outside, coloured and distorted by the very worst of mediums—party politicians, ambitious patriots, and paid advocates. Bulgaria, Servia, and Greece, the half-fledged States of the Balkan Peninsula, hunger already for slices of Macedonia, which Turk, Albanian, and Arnaut, painfully conscious; that their time is BhGrt and their tenure precarious, ruthlessly plunder and oppress ; while the wretched Macedenian,toiling and moiling from morning till night, from January to December, hungers for the bread and meat with which he so liberally supplies his thankless- masters—and often dies for want of them!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>OCEAN SPEED,</head>
        <p>OCEAN SPEED,</p>
        <p>WHERE WILL IT STOP? Mr. Alfred Yarrow, the chief of the well-known firm. which recently built the Russian torpedo catcher Sokol, the fastest vessel at present afloat, has been interviewed by a representative of " Black and White" on the ques- tion of ocean speed. "As regards the ultimate result of this increase of speed and of finality," said Mr. Yarrow, " a auestion I have often been asked, the only possible reply is that the man who talks of finality in improve- ments must indeed be bold. Even with present know- ledge it is evident that vastly-increased speed it, possible by the use of lighter materials in construction—alumi- nium, for example, instead of steel. The last access of high speed has been mainly brought about by the introduction of water-tube boilers—that is to say,boilers in which the water is inside the tubes and the fire outside. The great difference between our bailers and those made by other firms is that our tubes, being all straight, are easily accessible for cleaning and for examination. Eighteen years ago our highest speed was twenty-one and a half knots—that was in the case of a torpedo-boat built for the British Admiralty. That vessel was half the length of our latest torpedo-boat destroyer. Since that date we have been steadily increasing our speed by means of triple 'compound engines and higher boiler pressures, with the result that we have to-day a vessel doing over thirty knots per hour." "Is another such rapid increase of speed to be- expected in the future?"—" This last jump. dua to the employment of water-tube boilers, will probably carry us to about thirty-five knots. Any further increase of speed will be obtained through some w.;ssible but unfore- seen invention which, so far as I know, is not in sight of engineers at the present time. It is not rash to prophesy an increase of five knots within 'the next decade." "Which navy can now be said to possess the fastest war vessels afloat ?''—" As matters stand now both Russia and Francs have vessels to the class I have been referring to,superior in speed to those of the British Navy."</p>
