      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ORTH LIVING</head>
        <p>ORTH LIVING</p>
        <p>IN HELL?</p>
        <p>Such was the subject of a celebrated sermon preached in Manchester. but the more practical problem of the slay is how to make life worth living on earth. One of the important tri- butaries of the river of happiness is the possession of a com- fortable home. A home made bright and cheerful not only by the kind and loving dispositions of those living there, but made attractive by the good taste displayed in the furnishing. Furnishing is a very important factor in the matter of con- tinued self-satisfaction. So much jerry new London furniture Is being now retailed by drapers that one needs to pause be- fore rushing impetuously into the first draper's because of a cheap red ticket. High-class makers, such as Lamb,Hillows, and Gamett,still exist, to whom the wealthy can still go with a long puree but to most people this would be a too expensive experiment. Again, the average man nowadays has a strong liking for a bargain; anything obtainable at about half or quarter the cost has a strong fascination f T him. Is it possi- ble to obtain such an advantage in furnishing? Yes, and more than possible, it is an absolute fact. We cannot do better than recommend a visit to MR. WORSLETS FUR- FISHING- PANTHRON, GAYTHORN. MANCHRSTIiii.</p>
        <p>?FEB Successful Establishment of any of the large ware- A. houses of Manchester is. always of interest to the citizens. One of the most brilliant examples of the value of perseverance, enterptise, and tact is the now busy hive of industry so well known at The Pantheon, which occupies such a prominent position In Gaythorn, near the Central Station, Manchester. The building is s. massive pile of modern design, 150 feet long, six storeys high, with show- room accommodation covering an area of over one million square feet. The plate-glass windows are each 12 feet square. each square if broken costing nearly £3O to replace. 'len of these immense elbow windows form a portion of the frontage. This will give a faint idea of "The Pantheon" in outward appearance, but ah I—here pen fails altogether in attempting to do justice to anything like an adequate description of the interior. "The Pantheon" was specbilly built to give the largest and most convenient floor space .for showrooms, and cost £28,000, the floors specially constructe,l tocarry enormous weight. To-day it stands as one of the smartest furnishing houses in England, containing by far the largest and most comprehensive stock of any two houses In this large city From basement to roof, from floor toceiling, "The Pantheon" is filled to overflowing with thousands of well bought, care- fully selected bargains, to attempt description of which would be as futile as Liverpool attempting to crush - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MA- HONEST-RE iHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE MA- HONEST-RE iHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The destinies of" The Pantheon" are cmatrolled and pre- sided over by its sole absolute proprietor, Mr. W. H. Worsley, whose sole study is an ever •increasing desire to satisfy and please the thousands of cuetomers who daily patronise and support his efforts. We invite the reader to accompany us in an imaginory visit to "The Pantheon' to see what it con- tains and hew it Is managed. On arrival we are at once struait with the magnificent</p>
        <p>STARTLING APIGIARANOB of "The Great Pantheon." the immense 'Ong line of stupen.- dons show windows displaying for sale capital specimens of higb-olass Second-hand Furniture uy high-class makers, the sale of which has made " The Pftritheou ' famous.</p>
        <p>UPON ENTBRING TRH BUILDING , we see at once that as far as stock is concerned there is sufficient furniture for nearly the whole of Manchester. We see at once visitors walking round inspecting from Oldham, Bolton Bury, Burley, Bfickburn,Bacup. Rochdale, Preston, Warrington and all parts of Lancashire and Yorkshire, Cheshire, the Potteries, Wales, &amp;c. It is the cardinal rule of "The Pantheon " to give the fullest facilities for inspec- tion without feeling compelled to buy. The ground floor is filled with the large sptcitnens of bedroom furniture suitable for Urge rooms; we are told the smaller suites are on the top floor, to which we can travel by means of the hoist, or walk. Bedroom suites on the ground floor we notice by Lamb, original cost £l5O, price £35 ; by Goodall, cost £lBO, for £5O ; by Henry Ogden. cost £2OO, for £65. These are among the best suites, but one that strikes us with special attention Is a fine handsome Bedroom Suite by Behan, in solid American walnut, heavily carved In bas relief, with large wardrobe fitted with brilliant crystal bevel mirrors ; dressing table unique with neatly fitted jewel drawers, sur- mounted by large swing dressing glass ; washstand fitted with statuary marble and shaving mirror. three chairs, pedestal table de nuit, and towel horse ; what originally cost £6O you can obtain for £25. Over 200 bedroom Suites are in sto.A, on show, from 355. to £lOO, forming a display so impressive that wears almost STRUCK DUMB WITH ADIAZSIONT.</p>
        <p>We now go up stairs to the Dining-room Showroom. What a sight, long avenues of Sideboards is the first impression; hundreds of Sideboards, with hundreds of Suites, also Dining Tables, Overmantels, splendid collection of Bronzes, Ormolu Clocks, Vases. dm. In this department we find magnificent Sideboards, original cost from £lOO to £2OO. at prices simply astounding, Ell) to £2O. The next floor above is the Drawing- room Furniture Showroom, which is simply a show of DAZZLING BBAUTY dissolving one's sensitive appreciation into impressive loveli- ness. No department is so interesting to la,ries as the draw- ing-room showroom; a keen delight is experienced in selecting weird, and fantastic furniture for the drawing-room. 21Isthetio. Moorish, and Bgyptian Cabinets. peculiar Green and Bronzed Tables Occasional Chairs of charming variety. Cosy Corners, Comfortable Lounging Settees and chairs equal to the highest class firms in the Kingdom at quarter the price. The largest showroom for Drawing-room Furniture in Manchester, containing over 100 Suites, 100 Cabinets, 200 Overmantels, 500 Tables. Suites from £3 10s. tn. to £5O, Cabinets from 335. to £45. Marvellous value, worth in most cases i hree or four times the money charged. The next department adjoining is cases thee or four times the money ch inged. The next de- partment is the Bedstea.l idni De iiimg Department, which contains an enormous stock of Bedsteads. The brilbant- burnished brass displayed here is so immense as to present almost a</p>
        <p>SBA OF GOLD. Magnificent specimens of the brassworkers' art is here die played to perfection. Brass Bedsteads may be purchased solid and complete for as low a price as 355. to the beet at £35. A clear saving of 50 per cent. is effected in bedsteads and bedding. Brass and Iron Bedsteads, strong and durable, maybe secured from 61. 6d. to 30s. The bedding is all new, manufactured on the premises; everything is new in this department, the work here giving employment to over 50 hands. Nothing is allowed to be used here except it has pre- viously been washed and purified. All bedding sold at Wor- riers may be guaranteed as of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SPOTLIISS PURITY.</head>
        <p>SPOTLIISS PURITY.</p>
        <p>_ Wool Beds complete, guaranteed all wool, from Es. fd., Hair Mattresses from 18s. 6d., Feather Beds from 225. 6d. The next place of interest to visit is the Bedroom Furniture Department, which is displayed in a showroom, 68 yards by 25 yards. larger than most of the combined showrooms of most furnishing establishments. One is bound to admit that The Pantheon is certainly far ahead of any rival establish- ments in point of size and convenience. The showroom visited so far are lofty, well lighted, and of immense area. The Bedroom F-rniture Showroom is magnificent; 300 Bedroom Suites are displayed in avenues. tke finest collec- tion extant, at prices simply incrediabie, astonishing every one, pulverising and exterminating all competition. Com- plete Bedroom Suites, solidthroughout, at Si 3 108. up to £3O, , magnificent in style aud finiso. Enamelled Suites from 305. to £7. The next department is for Kitchen Furniture, the ! variedatock of which is quite sufficient to satisfy the most exacting. Thousands of chairs, hundreds of tables and dressers, every requisite for the kitchen at half the usual ! prices charged. A department is also provided for Iron- mongery. But we must move on, our examination and in- , spectim is taking 00 long and occupying teo much spice. I We now are shown into the Carpet and Oilcloth Department, ! which contains thousands of new rolls of carpets, Brussels from is. Did., Tapestry from 10Id., Axminster from 2s. 11.10 and so on. Then we see hundreds of Second-hand Carpets. If you want a carpet to beat creation, buy one of Worsley's secondhand carpets ; they knock the competition of Jew and Gentile into smithereens. You can get a second-hand Brussels carpet, no, tapestry ; no. real, genuine Brussels. from 4 fob yards by 3to 4 yards, for under a sovereign. itugs of im- minim variety, lorely Skin Bugs, Axminster, in facto! every conceivable design, shade, and colour, forming a gorgeous display.</p>
        <p>RIVALLING. TRH RAINBOW. Olic'oths and Linoleum. are here cut to pieces; thousands of yards are here daily slashed and cut into all shapes to tit the various houses they are intended for. The prices are also cut, in fact, cut 50 tine that you would require a magnifying glass to discover any profit made out of this department. We saw Oilcloths from lid. per yard and Linoleum. trona 6d.per yard. The best Scotch Oilcloths and Linme,me are displayed from Is. 3d. per yard to 2s. lid. We should like to speak of all the wonderful things displayed for sale, but we must be pre- paring to ie sve The Pantheon in order to catch our train. We, therelore, hu.ry throug.t the Office Furniture Department, the Brass Vender and k. irebrass Department, the Miscellane- ous Department quickly on to the latest addition, the Music Department. Hark I What do we bear?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>MUSIC, HRA.VB!ILY AND SUBLIIID.</head>
        <p>MUSIC, HRA.VB!ILY AND SUBLIIID.</p>
        <p>The '• Hallelujah Chorus," Hasidol's masterpiece. is being rolled along in glorious h,trmony byagentleman who later we discovered to be •' The Proprietor." American organs are here displayed in great variety, mostly second-hand ; for instance, a Mason and Hamelin organ can be purchased for 10 guineas which cost £4O. The Pianofortes are in strong evidence, and a great credit to Mr. Woreley, who buys everytting for "The Pantheon" himself, Pianos by Brard. Hteinway, Broad. wood. Bechstein. Justin Browne, Collard, Brinsmead, and all the best makers are displayed at prices simply amazing. Brard pianos, the king of all instruments are priced at £l5, Ss2o, ad £2B. Marvellous value. Who would buy a cheap German ; iano W 131911 in 12 months would be worse than a Jaw's harp, when you Can obtain a floe second-hand piano by one of the best makers for a fourth of the original price? New pianos ty all the best makers are here sold for a profit f 10 per cent ; Worsley's will procure any instruments, by any maker, for a simple 10 per cent prat, and pav carriage. Neumever pianos, quite new, splendid Vanos,tor 26i guineas, list price 50 guineas ; Klikman's steel upright pianos £33, list £6O: B. insmead's £3O, collard's £3O, Hopkinson's £25. and so on, showing a marvellous saving on the oriilnary musiciellers' price. Magnificent second-band pianos at £3, £4, £5 up to £lO. wonderful bargains.</p>
        <p>IN CONCLUSION, we find that" The Pantheon" is undoubtedly three times the size of any other furnishing hones in Manchester • we have visited every rival establishment, but none can hold a candle to "The Pantheon." The universal courtesy extended to every visitor, whether a buyer or simply inspecting, is a strong recommendation to all to pay a visitor to "The Pan- theon " before purch‘sing furniture, And although a terrific 'eash trade is daily in evidence, yet Ms. Worsley is such a strong opponent to the iniquitous hire system that to sombat these evils, which mean a serious loss to those starting in life (see last weeks " Tit Bits." October 12th). Worsley's now give et edit to all respectable buyers at exsctly the same price Charged for cash, but make a direct charge of 10 per cent. above the invoice price. This is a boon,preventing the extor- tionate prices Usually charged on the hire system. Now our visit is ended, and after thnuking Os, salesman for his courtesy in showing us round, we cannot help but marvel as we wend our way to the station, upon the marvellous skill and ability displayed at "The Pantheon"in the building up of what pro- phetically promises to become one of the largest concerns in England, DON'T BUY FURNITURE _ .</p>
        <p>anywhere until you have visited '6 The Pantheon," Gaythorni Manchester, near the Central Station. HOURS OF BUSINESS EVERY DAY 9 TO 7.</p>
        <p>PRESS OPINIONS ABOUT J. GERSHON, THE UNIVERSAL FURNISHING ARCADE, 44. OLDHAM-STREEr, MANG` iIE3TER, (Next to the Arlolphl Bank).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>READ WIIAT THEY SAY:-</head>
        <p>READ WIIAT THEY SAY:-</p>
        <p>_ One of those modern monstre establishments, whose re- sources are so boundless, and whose stocks are so comprehen- sive, that it can, with equal care and despatch, furnish throughout a cottage or a mansion, is to be found in the famous house of J. GIiaISHON, fulnishing contractor of 44, Oldham-street, Manchester. This colossal business was commenced in 1870, and being conducted with remarkable ability, energy, and foresight, and on sound commercial principles, it rapidly grew in public favour and patronage until at the present time it is the largest and most represen- tative house of its kind in Manchester. Mr. Gershon at- tends to every customer personally, and does not leave cus- tomers in the hands of salesmen. Operations are carried on In a magnificent block of four-storey buildings. occupying a prominent and attractive position, and having a splendid frontage. They comprise shops, offices, and capacious show- rooms on the ground floor, and further suites of extensive showrooms on the first, second, and third floors, togethei with showrooms at the rear of vast capacity, and filled to re- pletion with an immense collection of valuable goods. Mr. Gershon is a thorough exponent of the general house furnishing, arm stocks held are unrivalled in Mane</p>
        <p>either in magnitude, variety, or worth ; if —wi-d^e—ex—perien e and far-seeing policy are absolutely requisite in props selecting and getting together such a stupendous conglom ation of miscellaneous goods, it, is equally true that a gref, amount of ability of no common order is necessary to arrange and group them with such admirable effect, and to maintain at the same t ime such regard for the comfort and convenience of visitors. Where everything that can possibly be considered to belong to this branch of business is fully represented, It is an endless task to attempt anything like an enumeration in detail, it will be sufficient to Indicate the leading lines with which the house i occupied. Superb and comprehen- sive stocks are held in Drawing, Dining, and Breakfast Room Suites, made of all the beet woods, artistically elegant in design, and magnificently and gorgeously upholstered in all materials of various colours, shades, and tints. Bedroom Suites made of the most dainty materials, and of the choicest patterns. And substantial, solid-looking Library. Study, and Hall Furniture, together with inex- haustible supplies of splendid couches, ottomans, and so as, I duchesse stands, sidebo,rds, book-cases,cheffoniers, cabinets, Davenports, easy, occasional, and other chairs, dining tables, dressing, work, card, 100, a.d occasional tables, whatnots, overmantels, chests of drawers, as well as a large assortment of bedsteads of every description. p ttern, size and price ; bedding, mattresses, curtains, fenders, fireirons and carpets, I rugs, and mats of every pattern and design, from all the most celebrated makers. Briefly, the stock embraces every article of whatever kind that can possibly be required in furnishing any or every kind of residence, from the cottage to the mansion of the noble; and not only is each in- dividual article to be found in these marvellous stores, hut each article is represented by a large collection, often unique In its kind. anst including a wide selection in style, quality, and prices This enterprising firm have also manufacturing premises of consider.ble extent, and thoroughly equipped with plant and machinery of the newest and best kind and a large number of skilled workmen are constantly employed 'in the nup.ufacture of cabinet work of all oescriptions. Repairs, too, are executed on the premises, a little army of assistants is employed in the control of this stupendous establishment, and competent men are sent all over the kingdom to tit up private residences. hotels, offices, public buildings, and mansions, in conjunction wi h their business. The connection of this representative house is far-reaching and most valuable, its goods imve acquired the highest repu- tation in Lancashire and the neighbouring counties, and the name of "Gershon "is an ample guarantee for everything that is superior to his line of business. The proprietor is a man of extensive experience and ot great and varied know- ledge in the various branches of the business in which he is engaged. His able and energetic supervision is given to the whole of the concern, and the utmost vigilance is used that nothing shall emanate from his establishment which shall tend to detract from the high and well-merited reputation the firm has achieved. Mr. Gershon is held in great esteem in private life for his ability, public spirit, and commercial Integrity. We strongly advise those about furnishing to call at Mr. GHRSHON'S Universal Furnishing Arcade, 44, OLDHAM - STREET, MANCMSSTEIS. (Next door to the Adelphi Bank).</p>
        <p>LETTERS ARRIVB BY BVE!IY POST expressing thanks at the tmanner orders are cArried out, and the following are published with the consent o+ the writers. _ _ HEAD WHAT THEY SAY. _</p>
        <p>L. Samson, Esq.. the Managing Director of the Civil Service Dental Company. Limited, writes : " 150, Brompton-road, South Kensington, S.W., "August 30, 1895. " Mr. J. Gershon, Manchester.</p>
        <p>" Dear sir,—l have returned your packing materials, and consider it only just to say that everything arrived in perfect condition. 1 am very pleased with the goods. Should I require more, you shall have my order, and 1 shall be please] to recommend you.—Yours truly, L. SAMSON.</p>
        <p>READ ON. Fred Bentley, Req., writes : ' Victoria Hotel, Manchester, September 25, 1895. "Dear sir.—l expected to be able to call on you to-dav on passing through, but time prevented my doing so. Every- thing has arrived in a satisfactory manner, and my wife and self are perfectly sAtisfied with the various suites you furnished me with, and now the work is completed the house looks exceedingly nice. I enclose cheque in settlement. Please acknowledge to Southport, and oblige yours faith- fully, FRED. BENTLEY. Mr. J. Gershon, Oldham-street. Manchester. D BAD ON. W. Broadbent. sq , writes :-.- " Eeynard Cottage. Penkridge. September 27, 1895. " Dear sir,—All the goods arrived safely, and give the greatest satisfaction. If they turn out equal to the goods I bad from you 14 years ago, when I lived at Blackpool, I shall have no need to complain. Cases and racking returned. —Yours truly, "W. BROADHURST. "J. Gershon, 44, Oldham-street. Manchester."</p>
        <p>MAR VLL OUS BARGAINS. IN Consequence of Financial Difficulties and a Death in the Family, I have had placed in my hands for immediate dis- posal the contents of a handsomely furnished Villa. late the property of a solicitor, who, through the death of his wife and unfortunate speculation, has come to grief. In addition to the dining, drawing, bed rooms, and library furniture, and a quantity of miscellaneous articles. I have a number of Law Books, particulars of which will be given below. Only a brief description of the goods can be given. Intending purchasers are cordially invited, and upon examination will find tire goods high class and in spotless condition, they having been made to special order less than 18 months ago. The hall is furnished with a most uncom- mon combination Sall stand, which forms a seat, ber,ch, ' bracket, fitted with receptacles for sticks, umbrellas, brushes, gloves, Ac., and the back is finished with brass pegs. and a mas.ive bevelled-edge glass mirror measuring about 3ft. long: Price £5 15s. A pair hall chairs to match, 21s. The Dining-room is well worthy the attention of thase about furnishing. It consists of a noble sideboard, which is guaranteed solid walnutwood through; it measure's 6ft. long and about 91t. high. The lower portion is fitted with every convenience, plate drawers, wine bin, and cupboards, with splendid carved panels, and a truly magnificent back, with a massive shield shape centre bevelled glass and four smaller bevelled glasses. between which are brackets, supported by massive carved pillars. Next comes the solid walnut extend- . ing dining table, with two extra leaves opening out to a large size, and resting on finely carved fluted legs of rare thickness. To match are the half-dozen standard upholstered chairs, two large easy chairs and couch, all Studded with brass studs, and I upholster-4 in a rich claret-colour leather of undoubted I quality. To complete this remarkably handsome let, I have the pair of real Japanese vases, standing about 2ft. high. Now for the price. Some houses would ask £9O for them. I am in a positidn to pack and deliver the lot for 38 guineas. The sideboard alone is worth the money. . . .</p>
        <p>The Drawing-room really baffles description, it is so unique, and cannot be described in the space of an advertieement. The four small occasional chairs, two conversational chairs, 1 pair of Eugene easy chairs, and the gondola double-ended shaped settee, are most profusely inlaid with choice and rare woods; the upholstery is moat uncommon, being a real broc .telle of chocolate and silver, which is handsomely 1 brocaded with flowers and sprays, trimmed with suitable gimp and highly finished. Neat comes the magnificent inlaid cabinet, measuring about Ct. 91n. wide and nearly 131 t. high, fitted in a most artistic manner with bevelled glasses, sma 1 cupboards enclosed with inlaid doors. It is simply a work of art, being complete In construction, design, and finish. The overmantle to match is very unique, most beaVfully inlaid, finished with bevelled glass and side brackets to match the Cabinet. There are two of the handsomest inlaid tables ever seen, octagonal shape, on curious shaped legs. with wine tray fitted between the legs. Now for the price. If .272 was , quoted it would not be anything like the cost. lam able to offer the complete fittings for a stylish drawing-room at the low price of 38 guineas. The Library is fitted with 10 solid oak chairs, one large easy chair, and very fine lounge. All are Doveston's make. hey are covered in leather, which Is somewhat worn. When new they cost £5O. Can now be had for £.lO the lot. There is a very handsome carved oak bookcase, enclosed with glass door' with carved pillars running up the sides. The lower portlon is carved with curious hearts. faces, Ac. Price, £lO. Very fine carved oak corner cabinet. 5 guineas. Superior black oak carved dinner waggon or side table, and several other oak pieces. Useful mahogany bookcase, with folding glass doors, mov,Ve shelves, lower portion enclosed with cupboards and drawer.. A great bargain ; for £4 158. A fine old Spanish mahouany dining table. No better table ever made, on bold fluted legs, five extra leaves, opening out to 20ft., also the leaf case. A dead bargain; for £l2. There are several Bedroom Suites. Ihe best room is in solid American walnutwood, and cost £2O. price 50 guineas , The second room is in solid rosewood, which cost £8.,. for 40 guineas. The third room, whieh is very handsome, consists of a solid American walnut suite ; the wardrob. is 61t. wide, fitted with two bevelled-glass doors, reaching f. om plinth to cornice; between the doors are lock-up drawers and cup- boards ; pedestal dressing table, fitted with drawers in the pedestals. large bevelled glass attached; the double wash- stands, with cupboards in the pedestals, dark marble slab,tiks back ; bevelled shaving mirror, towel miser, beside cupboard, and three chairs, upholstered in plush ; a £5O suite for 25 guineas. Several smaller suites, complete with wardrobes ; 13 guineas. Solid ash suite, with wardrobe, dressing table, washstand, bedside cupboard, chairs and towel airer, for £7 10s. Several odd wardrobes, dressing tables, and wash- stands.</p>
        <p>Kitchen oddments. Dinner set complete, 54 pieces. 18s. td. two corner cupboards. nearly new ; wicker invaltd carriage. on spider wheels and rubber tyres; coat four months ago, £8; price 555. A number of law books, among is hich are the following : William's Law of Real Property. Leading Convey- ancing and Equity Cases, " Inderman's ;" Leading Common Law Cases, " Inderrnan's ;" Law of Companies T. Hustsce Smith; the •Conveyanciog Acts, 1881 and 1874, with notes. forms, and precedents y H. P. Wolstenholme and H. O. Turner ; Introduction to Conveyancing, H. W. Rip- htnetone ; 4 vols. Stephens's Commentaries on the Laws of England. &amp;c.; a fine registered writing desk, pedestal writing desk, both nearly new ; number of bordered carpet squares, velvets, Brussels, and others. Some pleasing oil paintings and engravings, musical hog, two pianos, quantity of china and glass. Several very fine brass bedsteads. Solid 0.. k case of cutlery, neves been used, a wedding present. The whole have been removed, and are on view at J. GERSHON'S Showrooms. 44, Oldham-street (neat to Ad el phi Bank). Can be warehoused till requmed, and delivered re Note address; J. GERSHON, next to Adelphi Bank, 44, Oldham-street, Manchester.</p>
        <p>,alts by Rtution.</p>
        <p>lATHMS, Planing, Shaping, Slotting. Radial, and Pillar 1-4 Drilling Machines ; ac.—A.xnaF.w HAWORTH, Tool Maker, Sowerby Bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 26th, 1895.</p>
        <p>THE speeches which Lord ROSE. Lordarbor Roseber vat BgE.Y made at Scarborough at the Scough. end of last week were not framed in a militant vein. The late Premier out- lined no programme and sounded no" call to arms." He loyally accepts the verdict of the country at the recent elections and he has resigned himself to the prospect of fivo or six years of Tory administration. He points out that in the meantime the chief business of the Liberal party will be one of education. Some years ago, a party leader summed up his programme in the words " Register, register, register " ; Lord ROSE - HERY'sprograrame for at all events the immediate future is, "Educate, educate, educate." Ho urges that the apathy which has been evident in the Liberal ranks in various parts of the coun- try should be dispelled, and that a vigorous pro- paganda of Liberal primiples should be entered upon. The causes of the electoral reverses should be inquired into and studied, so that useful les- sons for future guidance may be drawn from them. Although Lord ROSHBERY was extremely frank in recognising and dealing with the facts of the poli- tical situation, thero was not the slightest note of despor.dency in his speech. He does not think that Liberalism will ultimately suffer in the estim- ation of the country by the perfornaances of the the late government. The history of that govern- ,</p>
        <p>rnent has, he contends, still to be written with more candour and knowledge than has yet been available. "It left behind it a mighty surplus, a mighty fleet, and a reviving trade for which its opponents were now endeavouring to take the credit. All that it did was done with very inadequate means and with difficulties which, so far from being known,were scarcely suspected" As for the future, Lord ROSEBERY entertains no doubt whatever that Liberalism will again play a dominating part in the history of this country. The differences between Liberal administration and Tory administration will, it may be confidently expected, be very clearly impressed upon the elec- torate before the present Parliament has reached its limit, and the need for a change will be gener- ally admitted. The majority of one hundred and fifty which supports Lord SALISBURY was to a largo extent secured by the making of promises which will never, and can never, be fulfilled by a Tory government. Already the breaking of pledges has commended in the most open fashion. The re- markable Tory poll in Lancashire was largely due to the promises that were made in regard to the Indian cotton duties, yet these promises were flagrantly violated within a few weeks of their be- ing made. The manner in which the Puke of DEVONSHIRE has within the last few weeks thrown cold water upon old-age pensions and other schemes of social reform, which did so much to re- cruit one section of the Government's majority, is well known to every student of politics; and mae of the latest and most startling examples of this wholesale repudiation of pledges is the denial by another member of the Government, Mr. LONG, that any promises were ever made by the winning party that they would introduce measures to relieve agricultural depression. When all this can take place within a few months of the gene- ral election it is easy to foresee that numerous sections of voters who were drawn into the Unionist net by astute but unscrupulous elsetioneerers are doomed to bitter disappointment during the next five or six years. Then will take place that reaction which Lord ROSEBERY foretells, and which will again carry the Liberals into power with a mandate to add still fiirther to the long list of reforms with which the party has been identified.</p>
        <p>THalthough Ui fortyii d y The United tt Unite di ia s bornefn i nAllin K weakness edm t h A i labour, between crepitude. Neither at the 'of the General Council held on Tuesday afternoon at Manchester nor at the public meeting in the evening was there any sign of the hopeless des- aoeu. shows no signs n annual meeting afiaofktn ss yearsl oir stress a -f; Kingdom active pair which, we are told, has seized upon leading temperance reformers. The President of the Alliance, Sir WILFRID LAWSON, was as genial and witty as ever, and in spite of the fact that the political party which has identified itself with a great measure of liquor traffic reforms has been severely beaten at the polls, neither he nor any of the other speakers was disposed to take a gloanls:, view of the prospect of temperance reform. indeed, it would have been strange if they ha" done so, for the mere introduction into Parlia" ment of the Local Veto Bill by the responsible Government of the day was, in its way and aPert from'7all after results, a crowning victory for the Alliance. It is only in quite recent years that the Alliance has been able to command this attention from politicians of any party. As Sir WILFRID LewsoN put it with equal force and brevity, in the early days of the movement they were rich" culed, reviled, misunderstood, reproached, repulsed, despised, denounced." It has been only through long years of persistent agita" tion that the question of temperance re i form has been brought within the sphere ° practical politics. That a great and radical measure of reform has not been accepted at the first time of asking is a matter of comparativeli minor importance. The great thing from the point of view of the members of the United ging- dom Alliance is that the question is now finallY • adopted by one of the great parths in the State. In two respects the members of the Alliance wh° were assembled at Manchester showed undoubted wisdom. In the first place, there was a complete tolerance of other suggested methods of reforming the liquor traffic than those which the Alliance most strenuously advocates. Any reform, even if it is proposed by the present Government will receive not only a fair consideration but even a hearty support from the Alliance, so long as it 15_ a real and honest attempt to reform the evils of the liquor traffic and not an insidious device for sub: sidising the brewers and for placing obstacles in the way of more complete improvements later on. All attempts for this latter kind will, of course,he ho jealously watched and earnestly opposed by tthe Alliance. And, again, wisdom was shown further passing of the resolution in favour of" still the educating the nation in the principles °-f ~bi., Alliance, and in the history and results of Pr°"l. tory liquor legislation." There can be no clitestl° that a great deal of harm was done to the Bibe.ra cause at tlie last election by the misrepresentatTlebe of the provisions of the Local Veto Billy the exposure of these misrepresentations and :popularising of the principle of local control of tn liquor trade is a work which if thoroughly ender taken is certain to produce good results. TRH FATAL ACCIDRNT AT CRNTRAL STATIOtDrien Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at the ,Pach, of Arms Hotel, on Monday morning on the James Horst, 70 years of age, out porter, v791°.„, killed on Friday evening at the Central Ststi" E;ed Catherine (forst, the widow, said that the deces n for could hear very well. He was a wonderful taa"t the his years.—John Hudson, a porter emploYed a.Ad at station, deposed that be was with the &amp;near lat. five minutes to six on Friday evening, and We, toe ter assisted him to take some luggage out Liverpool train. The old man then went avrthsed witness did not see him alive again. The &amp;cello° was an outdoor porter, but was privileged to, on to the platform tolook after she luggage °`:-„jelt mercial gentlemen. The deceased was verY at hearing.—Thomas Henry Hardie, carriage. " Spector, said he found the deceased on Friday at twe minutes past six on the lines leading 'the the shed. A light engine had just gone over spot. In answer to a juror witness said that „117 e. deceased was obliged to take his truck over the Li! os oi There was no other way by which be could the line.—The Coroner pointed out that thed it, ceased was not a servant of the companY, "wa- cora d was his duty to look after the luggage d cial travellers. If the old man was not all°We work at the station on account of his advalL.- e.. years a petition would no doubt bare ber..„riot seated to the company by the public '-oi,ne- him to be taken on again.—Mr. Simpson said times commercial travellers wrote to certaidn " their door porters asking them to meet them aa with luggage. They would threaten the compsnYo loss or damage if the porters were not there t them.—The Coroner said that if she deceashede not been allowed to put his hand-cart into t eVery he would have had to take it to Bank Qundeeral evening.—The jury returned a verdict Ot !leL there death, and exonerated all from blame, but there said they would like to recommend to the "mPa were several business men on the jury, wily that, ld be to prevent accidents in the future, it %TO__ was good thing if an outlet from the plattceto_"l„, made through Crown-st., so that perscns to the platform should not have to cross the inau</p>
