      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>i</p>
        <p>Is. It is now certain that there will, on the First of evernber, be elections in all, or nearly all, of the till° Wards into which Warrington is divided for lialaipal purposes. Seeing that there was a etteral Plection a few months ago and that the itn.wa is not agitated at the present time by any €‘'t ge question of municipal policy, it might seem thrasqe that there should be a wide-spread contest at t e Present time. But, after all,the reason is not far n° eeek. It is to be f3und in the fact that there is :general dissatisfaction in the borough with the Itijora3ent Town Council. The Council has been atiTinated for many years by the onse atives teot- aQcl ai ts 1 ioisnsalmost invariably the case where similar prevail, the result is unsatisfactory. The Acillaeil has gradually deteriorated as a governing 'to administrative body. The efforts of. the more 14"ghtened members, who recognise that progress Aft larinus directions should be tEe order of the 1:1 l' in Warrington, are to a large extent frost rated Alta eheer dead-weight of mediocrity and sto lid re- 440traace• The ratepayers will, therefore, not be ,40,2' that an opportunity is given them to infuse , '''e ne w blood into the Council. .</p>
        <p>! tah'e;.et InsLion' .13rEmidi '4Bl ra i .444 the %44eil 4 11l- 8 tie r”.</p>
        <p>Wan, live Ington is unfortunate in not having a "real r.l(ltd '' living within its borders, or even within 48°nable distance of.them, as the Corporation is MY prevented from following the prevailing -of having a member of the aristocracy to le over its deliberations. Many Corporations to have been seized at the present time with a 414. , mania for lordly Mayors. Lord Ripon is o.4latest acquisition of the peer-hunting Town „tleila as he has been invited and has consented to 'gaYor of Ripon for the coming year. There is Ito be said for the selection in this case—Ripon it easily get a worse Mayor than Lord Ripon. 'Corporation are stnitten:with the prevailing and the chosen victim in their case is Lord e• But there is a slight impediment in the. Lord Crewe is not a ratepayer. This diffi- Co4' however, not insuperable, as the Town tborolleil can make Lord Crewe a free-man of the kot4Rb as well as a Mayor. In this case, too, it is hie ctbie that Lord Crewe .would make an admir- tro,,thief magistrate for any town, but on general %II; de this running after peers is .not to be en- -141,40 Red• A town does not merely want in its t a more or leas ornamental figurehead, but a -.lwh igenti 0, even if he be only a plain "Mister," is Ret„ ned in feeling and in interests with the 441 body of inhabitants.</p>
