      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>VICAR OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE VICAR OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Chester has appointed the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, M.A., rA Bur:, to he one of his examining eh', ..us in the place of the ti E. A. P. Gray, M.A.. r,c,or of Wallasey, who has resigneu ri con- sequence of i fl-health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>lIE BOARD OF GUARDIANS VACANCY.</head>
        <p>lIE BOARD OF GUARDIANS VACANCY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J din Ashton, the returning officer has fixed Tue. • day n. xt, the 29th inst., as the last date for the receipt of nominations of candidates to fill the vacancy on the Hal of Guardians caused by the death of Mr. Thomas Ha,..iley, J.P., who represented Heath ward. The ele tion will take place on Saturday, the 9th November.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>NELSON-STREET SUBSCRIPTION BAND.</head>
        <p>NELSON-STREET SUBSCRIPTION BAND.</p>
        <p>Saturday saw the first turn-out of the above band in UT. form under the leadership of Mr. H. Clays. It pro- ceeded to Sutton Weaver returning home via Halton at nine o'clock p.m. Everyone was much pleased with the nu • ng and the bandmaster was frequently compli- mei 'ed on the smart appearance and training of the lads Their object is to give help to any denomination in cor e.erts, &amp;a. The band and concert party now com- prise 40 members, who have several engagements for the winter in-mths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH OF A CHILD AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF A CHILD AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The infant son of George Henry and Edith Spencer, of 13, Taylor's-row, Halton-road, Runcorn, died somewhat suddenly on Thursday m,rning,. Te baby was born on Monday morning and appea.ta to be in good health up to Wednesday night. At three o'clock the following morning, it became ill and medical assistance was sent for but before a doctor arrived the child had ex- pired. The facts have been communicated to the Coroner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>... P.S.E. AT THE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>... P.S.E. AT THE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Pleasant Sunday evenings were commenced at Bethesda Congregation Church on Sunday, and there was an encouraging attendance. An excellent address on "What do they call you ?" was delivered by the Rev. J. Simpson, pastor of the church. Special musical arrangements had been made. The Misses Heathering- ton very nicely rendered as a duet "Rock of Ages ;" and Mr. Charles Atherton exceedingly well sang " Nazareth." A euphonium solo was contributed by Mr. Outhwaite, conductor of the Runcorn Volunteer Band. Mr. C. C. le Couteur presided at the organ with his usual ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>130ATING CLUB SOIREE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>130ATING CLUB SOIREE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The first soiree of the season in connection with the Runcprn Boating Club was held in the Volunteer Hall on Wednesday evening, and was from every point of view a complete success. 'rho hall had been most tastefully decorated for the occasion by Messrs. flitchen and Squire of Liverpool. With the aid of Japanese lanterns, ban- nerettes, shields, fans, and bunting a most pleasing dis- play was made. , Leaning against the walls were a number of oars, and close to the platform, where Mr. Powell's orchestral band discoursed sweet music, a canoe was displayed. Near and around many plants were to be seen, and these had been lent for the occasion by Mrs. Hazlehurst of the Grange. Nearly 200 persons mere present, and the masters of ceremonies were Messrs. Sherratt (Widnes), A. Abel, and G. Buxton. About a score of dances were engaged hsv and these were interspersed with songs re idered by Mr- Ambrose Shingler, Mr. Arnold Pover, and Mr. Jones. Refreshments were supplied by Mrs. Broady, of the Drill Hall. The arrangements,which were entirely satis- factory,were in the hands of the following gentlemen : Messrs. W. Millinger, J. Smith, West, A. Abet, F. Abel, T. Miller, E. Speakman, J. H. Sherratt, and E. Rhodes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SOCIAL GATHERING AT THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL GATHERING AT THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Asocial gathering of students attending the Techni- cal Institute was held on Saturday 'evening. Mr. G. H. Kirkham occupied the chair in the absence of Mr. F. Wiswall. The programme for the first part of the even- ing was of a miscellaneous description. The soloists were Mr. B. Crosby, Mr. Henry Branch,and Mr. Brown, and their efforts were highly appreciated. A violin solo was well rendered by Miss Lily Temperley, and Miss Tyrie recited. Messrs. William Cooke and Rowley gave an instrumental duet. A lantern lecture comprised the second part of the programme. The slides illustrated the Valley of the Wye and the Thames. Mr. Wilding de- livered the descriptive lecture, and the lantern wa manipulated by Mr. B. Crosby. Altogether a most en-5 joyable evening was spent. THE SERVICES IN THE WESLEYAN CHAPELS.</p>
        <p>Services specially devoted to the spiritual welfare of the yoang were held in the Wesleyan Chapels on Sunday. The services were particularly addressed to the youth- ful members of the several congregations. Preaching at St. Paul's Chapel in the evening, the Rev. C. de Putron Gliddon said they wanted in society young men who were physical athletes. It was their duty to develop, maintain, and guard all the splendid powers of the body. Tbere was something beside muscular strength, namely, intellectual vigour. The next century would put a pre- mium on brains, and not HO much on blue blood. They should, therefore, train their intellect. Then there was the moral and spiritual athlete, and the source of this strength was Christ, in wham he urged them to place their trust, so that they might overcome the temptations and trials of the evil one. MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</p>
        <p>The programme of the first concert of the season in connection with the above-named mission was provided by members of the Parish Church choir, assisted by Miss Taylor and Miss Simkin. The concert, which was held in the mission-room on Tuesday evening, was presided over by Mr. Shaw, missionary. There was a good attendance. The programme was an excellent one, and was given in a most creditable manner. The choir,under the conductorship of Mr. James Littler, sang a number of glees, and the following contributed songs :—Mr. Branch, "The Tin Gee Gee" ; Master Lane, "Sweet Marie"; Master Bennett, "The Sea is England's Glory"; Mr. Littler, " Mary " ; Miss Simkin. " Uncle Tom Master Lane, " The Farmer's Boy " ; Mr. Wilkinson, " The White Squall " ; Mr. Branch, " Stranded " ; and Miss Taylor, " Dolly's Revenge." Duets were rendered by Miss Simkin and Miss Taylor ; Messrs. Littler and Branch ; and Messrs. Wilkinson and Littler respectively. Owing to the length of the programme no encores were permitted. At the close a hearty vote of thanks was tendered those who had taken part,on the proposition of the Chairman.</p>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING CONCERT BY THE MUSICAL SOCIETY. As will be seen from a glance at our advertisement columns, the members of the Runcorn Musical Society are to give their first grand concert of the seam on Tu.-Iday evening in the Runcorn Public Hall. Mr. F. H. Crossley will again be the cinductor,and the programme to be given will be of a miscellaneous character, includ- ing overture, Magic Flute, Mozart ; March and Chorus (Taniahausgr), Wagner ; violin solos, orchestral selec- tions, part songs, and ballads. The violin solos should be a most interesting part of the concert, inasmuch as Miss Ethel Barns will be the violinist. Of this talented artiste,who is but 21 years of age, it may be stated that she commenced at an early age to study the piano and violin, and made her first appearance on the concert platform at eight years of age. When she attained the age of twelve, she played the violin before Joachim, and afterwards entered the Royal Academy of Music, and studied there under Sainton and Sauret, where she re- ceived eight medals, two certificates, the Potter Exhibi- tion prize, and the Hine gift for composition. The other artistes include Miss Jennie Rankin and Mr. Alfred Jordan. There will be a full orchestra and chorus of 120 performers. DEATH OF A CHESTER YOUTH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the Runcorn Police Court on Saturday morning touching the death of Thomas Jones, 15 years of age, son of Frank Jones, of 90, Christleton- read, Chester, who was found dead in bed at 4, Surrey- st.,on Thursday in,rning last. His mother gave evidence of identification,and stated that he was a vanman in the employ of Messrs. Peek,Frean, and Co. He left home on Tuesday. He had been in good health, only tor a week or two he had suffered from whooping cough.—Henry Smallshaw, vanman,Liverpool, said that on Wednesday they drove from Northwich to Runcorn. He did not notice anything amiss with him.—Ellen Gleave,4,Surrey- st., deposed to Jones having stopped with her when in Runcorn for 10 months. On Wednesday night he com- plained of toothache,and retired at 10.30. He slept with Arthur Pye. The following morning on entering the bed room she found him dead.—Arthur Pye spoke to deceased having suffered from a cough. About 11.30 he was awoke by his couching, when he shook him, struck a light, and assisted him, wiping froth from his mouth. He then apparently went to sleep, and witness did not notiee his condition on the following morning.—Dr.Mac- Dougall said he had made a post mortem examination. There were no marks of violence. Internally the left lung was very much congested, the right less ao, the re- mains of the evening meal in the stornach,tbe heart very large, it being the largest he remembered having seen, and the valves of the blood vessels lea ling from the heart very much diseased, the middle one almost entirely obli- terated. The cavities on both sides of the heart were full of blood. In his opinion death was due to heart disease, accelerated by a paroxysm of whooping cough. —A verdict was recorded in accordance with the medical evidence. Sr. EDWARD'S CATHOLIC CHURCH, RUNCORN. At this chinch on Sundae there was celebrated the feast of St. Edward, the harvest thanksgiving services, Pnd the annual sermons on behalf of the schools. The church had been most tastefully decorated for the occa- sion. The Rev. Father Crecan, of Northwich. was the preacher, morning and evening. In the morning he based his remarks on " Toe Providence of God and His goodness toward men." He stated that as God had been so good to them they shiuld be kind and considerate to their fellow creatures. One of the ways in which they tould demonstrate this wan to educate the children, so that then- would be excellent citizens of the Common. wealth. To give children a good soisni education was the MoSt efficacious way they could render help to those around them. He alluded to the absolute necessity of supporting denominational schools, which to-day were doing admirable work, and which up to the passing of the Education Act bore the full brunt of supplying the educational wants of the country. It wowd be an evil day when religion was divorced from education. The two should go hand in hand, like twin sisters. Unless children were grounded in the duties they owed to God, as their creator, they could not expect them to be good conscientious citizens. In the evening the rev, gentle- man spoke on the life of St. Edward the Confessor, and traced bis history.</p>
        <p>ELLESMERE-ST. CHAPEL, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held in the schoolroom on Tuesday even- ing, the balance sheet in connection with the recent "Gentlemen's at Home," was submitted. The Rev. J. Stafford occupied the chair. The financial statement read by Mr. Gandy showed that a profit of £lB 3i.llid. had been realised,which after clearing off the debt on the chapel funds,left a small balance in hand. This was considered highly satisfactory. TUB MARINERS' CHURCH, RUNCORN. •</p>
        <p>A second series of people'i. entertainments was com- menced on Monday evening in the Mariners' Church Mr. D. Smith occupied the chair, and lie ,•ciefly explained the object in view in promoting the atherings. An interesting programme was then sub- Initeci and was taken part in by the Misses Temple, Mr. ( harles Temple, Miss Hazlehurst, Mr. Richard Henry Bennett. The organist was Mr. Thomas Foster. There was a large attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>FAA MINER SAT UR AY, OCTOBER 26, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE FAA MINER SAT UR AY, OCTOBER 26, 1895.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BRANCH OF THE ST. JOHN AMBULANCE ASSOCIATION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN CONTINUATION CLASSES.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN CONTINUATION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>In connection with these classes, the second of a series of illustrated historical lectures was given on Thursday evening in Greenway-road Board Schools, by Mr. Moulton,head teacher,and Mr. Cragg. A chanter of English history was taken and copiously illustrated by means of slides shown on the screen by the aid of an oxy-hydrov.,,en lantern, which was manipulated by Mr. Thomas Bowyer. There was a large attendance of students who were highly pleased with the lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ANNIVERSARY SERVICES AT ST. JOHN'S CHURCII.</head>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY SERVICES AT ST. JOHN'S CHURCII.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. D. Evans, of Towyn preached the annual sermons on behalf of the St. John's Presbyterian S.mday School on Sunday. The musical psrtion of the service included a bass solo by Mr. C. Leathwood, and duets by Mrs. Temperlcy and Mrs. C. Leathwood, and Mrs. Warburton and Miss S.P. Hayes respectively. The con- ductor of the choir was Mr. J. E. Warburton, and the organist, Mr. J. Richardson. The members and friends of St. John's Presbyterian Church are actively engaged preparing for a sale of work to be held on Wednesday and Thursday, November 27th and 28th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>• • BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS.</head>
        <p>• • BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS.</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of this class on Wednesday evening, Mr. J. W. Hogg read an admirable paper on "Life in Damascus." As the lecturer had for some time been working as a missionary in the neighbourhood of Damascus he was able to give many interesting remini- scenes. He described the ancient city, and referred to the character of the people. He said he was obliged to leave the country in consequence of ill-health, but he expressed his intention of returning to it again. A num- ber of pictures and photographs, which were handed round, added greatly to the interest of the lezture. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Hogg on the motion of Captain Millinger, seconded by Mr. G. H. Kirkham. The president of the class, Mr. Simister, occu- pied the chair. • SoNs OF TEMPERANCE, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of the above order, on Saturday night, in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom under the chairmanship of Mr. Thomas Hudson, an ad- dress was given by Mr. Malcolm Jones, in which he chiefly dwelt on the instincts of animals for self-preser- vation. He gave a number of instances in which he showed what wonderful instincts they had when in dan- ger. He then pointed out the example they were to man, the higher animal, when he is surrounded by so many moral dangers. He especially referred to drink and the temptations which beset men. The address gave rise to a discussion in which the following officers and members took part :—Messrs. B. Key, Thomas Cooke, John Jones, S. M. Temperley, Thomas Joseph Holt, and Daniel Bisbrown. Afterwards a vote of thanks was ac- corded to Mr. Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>THINGS HEARD – AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD - AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The triennial election of members on the Run- corn School Board is now drawing nigh, and the ratepayers will soon be called upon to record their votes. The six members at present on the Board retire, and from all appearances they " intend to offer themselves for re-election. A vacancy occurs in consequence of the lamented death of Mr. Thomas Handley, LP., who,as is well known, was a staunch Nonconformist and strong supporter of Board schools. As far as can he ascertained no steps have yet been taken by the Nonconformists of the town to select a candidate to till the vacancy. This is much to be regretted The present is a most crucial time in the history of elementary schools. The Church party are fighting harder than ever for denominational schools, and the result of this year's School Board elections, will have important consequences especially if the proposed extraordin- ary increase of Government grant to Voluntary schools be brought forward. The deciding vote on the Runcorn Board has been held by the representa- tive of the Roman Catholics in consequence of the number of Church and Nonconformist members being equal. If rumour be correct, a great effort is to be made by the Church party in Runcorn to capture another seat on the Board, and thus be in full control. It is time the Nonconformists were up and doing unless they are content to sleep in their tents while their opponents steal a march upon them. Considering the healthy state of Noncon- formity in Runcorn, they should have long since been in possession of the majority of the seats on the Board.</p>
        <p>But apart altogether from the religious stand- point, there are several matters of the greatest im- portance to Runcorn people which need deciding. First of all there is the proposed enlargement of the Runcorn Board Schools which promises to be a knotty question to deal with. In the plans already prepared, provision is made for the construction of baths in the basement of the building, and Dr. Mc- Dougall who has taken a foremost part in pushing this matter forward is intent on carrying out his project. To the bath scheme a number of the pre- sent members are opposed, and it will be well for the public to express an opinion upon it. There certainly can be no question as to the desirability of having baths in a maritime town such as Run- corn, but as to the advisability of carrying out the project at the present juncture there may be a dif- ference of opinion.</p>
        <p>Another point which should be ventilated at the forthcoming election is that of the Board charging school fees in some of their schools and not in others. In the annual balance sheet submitted a few week ago there appeared an item of a little over £3O for school fees. That, presumably, was for a quarter's fees taken at what were formerly the Wesleyan schools, but which are now in the hands of the Board, Already complaints have been made by parents at the distinction made by the Board, and if £l2O is going to be the sum total exacted in fees from one particular set of children in the dis- trict of what were formerly the Wesleyan hools, the game hardly seems worth the candle.</p>
        <p>The breaking of the heavy clouds of trade depres- sion which have hung around Runcorn for some time now seems perceptible. There is just a little bit of blue, if it may be so termed, in the brighten- ing prospects of extended works at Runcorn. The United Alkali Company's venture at the Old Quay works seems to be proceeding satisfactorily, and almost immediately the Salt Union are to follow suit with another large warehouse at Weston Point which will probably mean an increased output at their Weston Point works. Then there is talk of a glass industry being started in our midst, and also of the erection of alkali works by the Aluminium Company or what will in all likelihood be known as "The Castner-Kellner Alkali Company," who have not yet decided against us.</p>
        <p>There is still hope, and if the company do decide upon Weston Point it will be no mean addition to the industries of the district. The estimates of the cost of erecting the works are put down at the formidable figure of £130,000, and the turnover, it is computed could be 18i tons of pure caustic soda, and 40 tons of bleaching powder daily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>PENiETFI.</head>
        <p>PENiETFI.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF MR. T. CHAMPRESS.—On Sunday the Rev. T. Champness, of "Joyful News" fame, visited Penketh and conducted two services in the Wesleyan Chapel. There were crowded attendances at both services, many friends from Widnes and Warrington availing themselves of the opportunity to hear the popular preacher. In the afternoon Mr. Champoess attended at the Sunday school, and conducted the young men's class, and afterwards delivered a short and instructive address to the school. POLICE COURT AND PRISON GATE MISSION.—OR Friday a lecture in connection with the above mission was delivered at the Wesleyan school by Mr. R. Taylor (Police Court Missionary), Mr. R. Garnett, occupied the chair. Mr. Taylor gave a very interesting address on the noble work they had in hand, and several photo- graphs from life, were shown by the aid of a lime-light lantern. Mr. Garnett also made SOMA interesting remarks. There was a good attendance,and a collection was taken in aid of the Mission.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCH ES -WATtRINOTON. ST. LUKE'S CHURCH. _</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services wereoonntined a- the above church on Sunday. when the pulpit was oc cupied in the morning by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton And in the evening by the Rev. J. Forbes, M.A. In the after- noon a scholars' service was held, when an address WAS given by the Rev. H. R. Wild. The church was beauti- fully decorated, and there was a large congregation at leach service. Collections were made throughout the day I to the amount of about £B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>DISTRIBUTION OF CERTIFICATES.</head>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION OF CERTIFICATES.</p>
        <p>The annual tea and concert in connection with the</p>
        <p>Runcorn branch of the St. John Ambulance Association was held in Greenway-road Primitive Methodist school- room, on Wednesday evening. The president of the Runcorn branch, Mr. J. Brundrit, J.P., occupied the chair, and there were also present :—Miss Silver (Kingsley), Miss Barclay, Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P., Dr. McDougall, Messrs. Edward Newton, %V. Bankes, J. H. Clare, G. H. Walton, J. Bosworth, J. D. Smith, D. Smith, CV. Branch and J. Littlemore. The proceedings were made additionally interesting by the presentation of certificates gained by the students of the classes during the year. There was a large attendance. The tea tables were presided over by the following ladies :—Mesdames S. D. Matthews, Bosworth, D. Smith, Misses B. Smith, Falkner, McNorton, Rigby, Tonkes and Ada Smith. The programme opened with the singing of a madrigal entitled " Brightly dawns our Wedding Day," given by Misses M. Clare and Simkin, Messrs. J. Littler and H. Branch. Miss May Clare followed with a capital inter- pretation of the song'' Waiting," and later in the even- ing she was still more successful in her singing of " The Devoted Apple." Mr. Wilson Blackledge, of St. Helens, the popular humorist,fulfilled his duties very successfully and that he pleased his audience may be judged by the fact that he was twice encored. At the outset he gave an inimitable caricature of a panoramic entertainment, " Round the World in Two Hours," which provoked the audience to roars of laughter. His other contributions were "The Baby " and " Our street at 8 a.m." " The Old Soldier " was the title of a song given by Mr. C. Atherton who bids fair to become a singer far above the average attained by ainateurs. In responAe to loud applause he complied with an encore. Miss Simkin's contribution was" Why are you standing, alone ? " apd she also sang in a duet with Miss Taylor, entitled " When the wind blows in from the sea." The latter vocalist at a later stage sang " Another life." Mr. H. Branch sang well in the so'o " I'm King of the F.,rest Glade," and Mr. E. Worrall contributed the hst item of the programme by once again singing " Ts floly City." The duties of accompanist were satisfactorily fulfilled by Mr. W. B Kay. During an interval the certificates were presented. . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he must express the pleasure he felt as president of the Runcorn branch of the Associa tion at seeing so numerous an attendance. He trusted it was an augury that the association was not waning in public opinion, but rather that it was on the increase. Be thought when they considered the circumstances of the case there was no reason why it should wane. There was nothing controversi:+ I in connection with it, either religious or political. It was tor the help of one an ther whether they were Caristian, Hebrew, or of no religion. So long as there was suffering humanity, the successful candidates of the classes were ever ready and willing to give efficient au i until such time as a doctor could he called in. There was no reason why it should not be the most popular of the institutions he was connected with, and he was very proud to be there and support its claims. (Applause.) It was eo new institution. It was founded in the year 1050 at Jerusalem, but it was not introduced into this country until 1876. Thus in less that 20 years they had societies established through- out the country, which showed the popularity it had gained amongst the community at large. The Prince of Wales was the Grand Prior of the Order,and since its in- ception in this country over 300,000 persons had passed and obtained the first aid certificate. (Applause.) Ile thought that was sufficient evidence to show that the Association was acknowledged by high and low alike. The Association had not only civilians but peers of the Realm, and barons going the course of study, and find- ing it most useful and interesting. In that little town ofitheir's (Runcorn) six classeslhad been taught by their respected friend, Dr. McDougall. (Applause.) The re- sult was that over 300 persons in Runcorn had gained certificates. These included abcut 60 ladies. There were also about 100 constables who had passed the ex- aminations during that short time. (Applause.) He was sorry that at the last moment Colonel Hamersley was prevented from coming, but from a conversation he had with him he believed tha:. out of 500 constables in the county something like 400 had gained first aid certificates. Colonel Ham, rsley was most anxious that every momber of the force should be able to render first aid. Of course they had complaints of policemen not being there when they were wanted, but they were always looking and walking about and thus would be ready and willing, as every member of those classes was, to render any aid when needed. It was net worth his while to enumerate the cases which had been bealt with in Runcorn during the past year, but he mignt say that first aid was rendered in between twenty and thirty severe cases—cases where medical aid was actually necessary, and where if first aid had not been rendered before a doctor had been called in, it might not only have been serious, but dangerous. That night they had twenty candidates who had passed and obtained their first certificates, nine who had passed in the second course, and one who was entitled to a medal for having passed all that was required. New elegies were about to be started shortly and he thought they would be pleased to tear that already there were 20 of their local volunteers who had given in their names as desirous of attending the classes. Dr. McDougall had kindly consented to hold two classes in consequence of those volunteers being desirous of gaining that in- formation, and if anyone wished to jAn the classes he trusted they would do so without delay. They had been fortunate in securing the services of Miss Silver, who he might say was the niece of their respected townsman Mr. Chadwick. (Applause.) They thanked her for her Icindnees in coming forward on the present occasion. (Applause.) Miss Silver then distributed the certificates to the following recipients :—Measrs. John Atherton, Thomas' Butler, Thomas Bazley, George Bancrott, H. J. Brad- shaw, George Dale, Joseph Ellis, E. Gregory, Thomas ' Haddocks, E. C. Hicks, W. Leathwood, J. Littlen3ore, A. E. Manning, S. D. Matthews, W. Quayle, P. Rawlin- son, W. Wylie, G. H. Walton, M. Williams, and A. Wright. Second stage : J. Bosworth, P. Booth, J. Griffiths, P. Hammersley, R. Lee, A. Morris,J. Ridgway, J. Richardson, and W. Williams. Medal : D. Smith. Red cross. Private H. J. Bradshaw of the 2nd Volunteer Cheshire Regiment. Mr Norman said it was sometimes charged as a re- proach against Runcorn that it did n,ot support continu- oesly the various institutions started in the town. He was not going to contest that statement for a moment, art fin contended that it was a reproach which could be used with equal force against any small town. The fact:of the matter was that if they were to be kept going they needed at their back some strong individuality. They met under the auspices of a successful associa- tion, and he had little need to point out that that strong individuality was to be found in their friend, Dr. McDougall. (Applause.) The whole town was laid under the greatest obligation to him for his work to that association and the students under a special obligation, and that they thoroughly appreciated the doctor's services was shown by the fact that they were presenting to him a geld medallion. It contained the following inscription " Presented to Dr. F. McDougall from students, Runcorn, 1895." He felt sure Dr. McDougall would highly value it, especially considering the graceful channel through which it woold reach him. His business was to request Miss Silver to present the medallion to Dr. McDougall. (Applause.) The medallion whioh had on the obverse side, the em- blem of the St. John Ambulance Association, was then handed_ te Dr. McDougall by Miss Silver. . _</p>
        <p>In acknowledging the gift, Dr. McDougall said he had had to appear before an appreciative audience in con- nection with the Ruucorn branch of the St. John Ambulance Association for the last six or seven year.. It was ono of the institutions of the town which had become thoroughly grafted. It was very gratifying to himself and the president, and every memb-r of the association, that their work had been appreciated 1.. the public. He thought the total number of students during the past year was not so large as usual, but they were equally successful. One hundred per cent., he believed, of those who attended the classes passed the examination. (Applause.) The Order, as they had been told, was established in the year 1050 at Jerusalem by a number of Italian merchants owing to the terrible cruelties of the very people who were doing such terrible havoc in Armenia at the present time. The Turks showed the same bloodthirstiness at that time as they had shown ever since. From Jerusalem the Order of St. John was driven about until they arrived at Malta, and very early in the last century they were driven from Malta by Napoleon. Since then the Order had been dispr rsed in the land. The Duke )f Manchester Introduced the order into England and became the first Prior. At his death, the Prince of Wales was appointed the Grand Prior, and the movement had now spread in all directions. An order of that description should have no enemies. Every member was at the service of every- body,and it should be rendered without let or hindrance. (Applause.) The class that was about to be established would be a very large one. There would be a number of volunteers, who were represented that night by the friend who distinguished himself at the late encampment at Blackpool, and succeeded in winning the first badge of the Red Cross Society. (Ap- plauge.) That society was adopted in Germany during the Franco-Prussian war, ad the late Empress Augusta was at the head of the Order. It had done useful work throughout the world. Those who carried the badge of the Red Cross Society were privileged from ordinary soldiers and were received in the camp of the enemy. The class was, he mentioned, going to be housed on thi; occasion in the Technical Institute. (Applause.) Referring to the present, he thanked the members of the class for vivirg him such a distinction by presenting him with that medallion. ETO assured them that he valued it very higl,lv, and he hoped that their stioces- scars would be equally successful in obtaining a 100 per cent. of passes. (Apolause.) In conclusion he prop-eted a vote of thanks to Miss Silver who had come such a I long distance to present the certificates with her usual good grace. He hoped she would come often amongst them and identify herself with them at Runcorn a little more. She had, as he knew, a love for old Runcorn. I (Applause.) 11 Mr. Branch seconded the proposition. He was sure I hey all felt it a very great honour to have her present</p>
        <p>to distribute the certificates, and he hoped it would be an inducement for the ladies to form another class. (Applause.) 1 he Chairman responded on behalf of Miss Silver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>REPORTED SINKINU OF A RUNCORN FLAT.</head>
        <p>REPORTED SINKINU OF A RUNCORN FLAT.</p>
        <p>THREE RUNCORN MEN SUPPOSED TO BE DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Some sensation was caused in Runcorn on Wed- nesday when the news arrived that the Runcorn flat" William Henry "was supposed to be lost with all hands. Sad fatalities at sea to Runcorn men have been very frequent of late, and the present from all accounts, appears to be but too true. From inquiries made it transpires that the flat in question belongs to Messrs. Phillip Speakman and Sons, of Runcorn, and when it left here on Friday night last for Liandulas, her crew consisted of Captain James lioxworth, of the Holloway, Run- corn, William Hox worth, 21 years of age, who had lived with his parents at 39, Lowlands-road, Run- corn, and John Inglesfield, 17 years of age, of 29 Salisbury-st., also of Runcorn. The flat which is. ' an exceptionally good one, completed her outward journey in safety, and having been laden, the voy- age home was commenced on Monday. She was last sighted about six o'clock on the evening of that day by the captain of the steam flat "Zillah " of Widnes, just off Rhyl. It is supposed that she struck a sandbank near this point. Darkness was coming on at the time she was seen by those on the Zillah, and there was every appearance of what is called a " dirty" night. It is thought that the captain of the Runcorn flat intended to get into the Dee, but owing to the vusts of wind accompanied by heavy rains which prevailed, all lights had been obscured and he has run on a treacherous sandbank. Messrs. Speakman and Sons received a telegram on Thursday stating that a small boat with the name Swallow, which was carried with the flat in ques- tion, had been washed ashore at Llandulas en Tuesday, bat nothing had been heard of the vessel or crew since Monday evening. This seems to show that the vessel has been lost. The Captain was a married man with three children. William Hoxworth, his brother, was a single man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>INDUSIRIES OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE INDUSIRIES OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that a short time age we referred to the prospect of the Salt Union Limited, extending their warehouses at Weston Point, with the object of being able to more expeditiously load vessels at the Ship Canal wharf, a short distance away. This, it may be stated, has now been defi- nitely decided upon, and tenders have been invited for the erection of a warehouse at Weston Point, 160 ft. long, 62ft. wide, rind 16ft. in height. With this extended storage accommodation it is very probable that there will be a considerable increase in the output at the Weston Point works, and the decision of the Salt Union seems to point to the fact that some satisfactory arrangement has been come to with the Ship Canal Company with regard to tolls. This development should be the means of finding employment for a considerable number of hands. With regard to the proposed alkali works by the Salt Union, which if decided upon, it is an- ticipated will be erected at Weston Point, the mat- ter is still cccupyinv lb e attention of the directorate, but no definite decision has yet been come to.</p>
        <p>_ The projected new works by the Aluminium Corn- pany, Limited, to which reference has been made in a previous issue, have this week, so to speak, as- , sumed a diff,-rent aspect. Early in the week a pros- pectus was issued of a Comoany to be called "The Castner-Kellner Alkali C nnpany, Limited," with a capital of £300,000, and inviting subscriptions to the extent of £200,000 in shares of £1 each. A few particulars gleaned from the prospectus may be of interest. It states that an arrangement has been made bat ween toe AlumMiuna Cmspany,Limited,owners of the Castner patents, and Messrs. Solvay et Ci 3, of Brussels, ownera of the Kellner patents (hence the name Castner-Kellner) for the manufacture of caustic alkali and bleaching powder by means of electrolytic decom- position ef salt or alkaline salts. The two companies have agreed to exchange patents, and all improvements will be exchanged for a term of 12 years. Ii evi lence of the value of the patents it is stated that the processes for the manufacture of caustic soda and bleaching pow- der by electrolysis covered by these collective patents , are upon the same principle, and it is believed that many advantages that do not exist in the old methods of manufacture will be secured to this new company by the comnination decided upon in the agree- ment. It is further stated that the Castner process is being worked on a commercial scale at the works of the Aluminium Company Li rnited,at Aldbury,near Birming- ham where plant has been erected for the purpose of de- monstrating the successful production of caustic soda and chlorine. No definite site has been decided upon for the erection of the works, but the prospectus , states that suitable sites for works are under considera- tion ; when one is selected,it is intended to proceed with the erection of plant by successive electrical instilla- tions, each complete in itself, up to 4000-horse power,l the estimates of cost of which, including reasonable con- tingencies, will amount to a total outlay ot £130,000. This plant is nalculated to produce 181 tons of pure' caustic soda and 40 tons of bleaching powder daily. The process is a continuous one, and if worked for 340 days a year, the annual output would be 6300 tons of pure caustic, and 13,600 tons of bleaching powder. There is now manufactured in this country upwards of 160,000 tons of caustic soda and 150,000 tons of bleaching powder per annum. The subscription lists have just closed ,and ' if the company is floated we may soon hear whine e the" suitable sites " has been selected. Another industry at Runcorn which will be watched with interest is that of the United Alkali Com- pany at their Old Quay works, whereby it is intended to convert the tremendous quantity of purple ore, commonly known as " blue-billy," into brickettes. A number of bricklayers and others are engaged. For many weeks past men have been removing numerous tons of the material to clear the site the new plant is to occupy. The process for making the blocks is the turning of the ore into a kind of mortar, after which it will undergo somewhat similar operations to the making of ordinary bricks, namely, being cast into blocks, and then burnt in kilns. In this state the use ef the material in connection with the manufacture of iron and steel is expected to be greatly preferred. Hitherto the chief difficulty in utilising the " blue-billy " has been solidification, thus preventing the necessary draught in the furnaces ; but in the condition of blocks it will be possible to break it in sizes that may be desired, and thus obviate that obstacle. It is difficult to correctly estimate the number of men which will be found em- ployment by the new industry, but it is cousidered that at least 300 tons will be manufactured each week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Lord Warkworth was on Monday evening adopted as the Conservative candidate for South Kensington. The world's blind are computed to number about one million—about one sightless person to every 1,400 in. habitants. . .</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Dufferin and Ava has resigned the Lord War&amp; °ship of the Cinque Ports, and the Queen h-,4 conferred the post upon the Marquis of Salisbury. the River Rhine is almost dried up. The boats ef the Cologne-Dusseldorf Company have ceascd to run, the water being too shallow. Foreign traffic suffers. Such a dry, hot season has nat been known for thirty years, and even then it was less dry than now in the Rhine districts. _ _ _ _ . -</p>
        <p>At the County of Lmdon Sessions on Monday Sir Peter Edlin granted an application that the bill against Bailey, one of the men charged with complicity in the great silver robbery, should not be presented at the pre. sent sessions. The reason for the application was that the inquiry into the case against Sarti has not been completed. America has broken the record with a " mammoth " potato, which is 28 inches long, 14 inches in diameter, and weighs 861 b. 10oz. This is equivalent in weight to over a bushel of ordinary potatoes. It was grown by Mr. J. B. Swan, of Loveland, Colorado, who raised 430 bushels of potatoes on a single acre last season. The mammoth potato is to be used for seed, it being " too big to eat." Baron Hirsch has thus far sent about 4,000 Russian Hebrews to the Argentine Republic. an hopes to have a Hebrew community there of 100,000 within ten years. He sends them out in companies of fifty families each provided with a rabbi and a doctor, and he expects them to settle in the villages, giving a special tract for each company. The Turkish newspapers at Constantinople publish the Imperial decree sanctioning the reforms in Armenia. The inspection of prisons, the formation of a mixed body of police and gendarmerie in each vili.yet, the pro- tection of the Armenians against the Kurds, the settle- ment of the "nomads " on lands provided by the Government,the establishment of a board of supervision, the abolition of forced labour,and reforms in the collec- tion of the taxes are announced.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. K. Graham, of Bucharest,read a paper before the London Chamher of Commerce, on Monday on the financial and trading capabilities of Roumania as market for English manufactures. He said that corner of Europe was deserving of more attention here. In the course of the discustion the Chairman (Sir A. MIR, M.P.) asserted that this country was very inadequately reprevented abroad, generally by means of commercial attaches ; and Mr. Graham showed that the interests of German manufacturers in Roumania were represented by a large staff at the Bucharest Legation, while our Consul could not Interfere iu such matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>IVA.RRINGTON.</head>
        <p>IVA.RRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A NEW LOCAL JUSTICE OF THE PEACE</p>
        <p>At the Quarter Sessions recently held at Chester, 11rt Harrison Tinsley, of Grappenhall, qualified as a jusice of the peace for the county.</p>
        <p>THE LABOUR EXAMINATION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The annual examination of boys and girls for labour certificates will take place on Saturday, Nov- ember 2nd, at Wycliffe Day School, sometime dur- ing the forenoon. The following list shows the number of boys and girls who intend to present themselves for examination :—Standard 3, 11; stan- dard 4 77; standard 5, 427 ; standard 6,35; standara 7, 13; making a total of 563. This is a very great increase upon the number of boys and girls ex- amined last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A.</head>
        <p>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>The attendance at the above meeting was large on Sunday, when Mr. Robert G. Henshall delivered a telling address which will long be remembered. Mr. Humphries, of Chester, was the soloist, and acquitted himself well in the solos " The Toilers and "Ye that are weary." Mr. J. P. Jones, Cairo- street, occupied the chair,and expressed his pleasure at the great success of the movement. Mr. Barber and Mr. J. "e. Green also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>SUNDAY SCETOOL TBACHERS ASSOCTATION.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCETOOL TBACHERS ASSOCTATION.</p>
        <p>The usual quarterly meeting of teachers connected with the Sunday School Teachers' Asoociation held on Monday evening, in )ne of the schoolroodis of St. Paul's Church. Monday being the day of intercession for Sunday schools throughout the diocese, it was celebrated by Holy Communion in the morning at 7.30. The tea in the evening was enjoyed by all who partook of it, and the number was by no means small. Subsequently a service of intercession was held in Sr. Paul's Church, at which there was present a fairly large congregation.</p>
        <p>THE AMALGAMArED .tOCIETY OF RAILWAY MEN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning about fifty members of the , above society assembled in front of the Town Hall, at 9.30, when, after forming into processional order, they marched, headed by the Buckley-street Brass Band, through Sankey-street, Legh-street, Horse- market-street, Bridge-street and Rylands-street to Friars'-green Chapel, where a special service was held. An excellent sermon was preached by Mr. Walter Brough, of Warrington. At the conclu,ion of the service the men re-assembled and proceeded by the same route to the Town Hall, where they dispersed for their respective homes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>At the second meeting of the current session, held at the Museum, Mr. A. T. Gillenders, of High Legh, read an introductory paper on "Insect Pests." The title was used in a popular rather than in a techni- cal sense, and the lecturer included in his list some pests, e.g., the currant bud mite, which are not strictly insects. He treated, at some length, the life history and anatomy of several scale insects which inflict injury upon both fruit and forest trees, suggested means by which they might be destroyed, and concluded an interesting and in- structive paper with some brief remarks on the aphis which causes the so-called American blight. There was a large attendance, and the paper was followed by a good discussion. EIIENRZIM, CHURCH, BITTTERMARKET-ST.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a iert meeting was held in the above place of worship, being the first of a series of meetings to be held during the winter season. It is customary for the Band of Hope committee to open the session with a social tea meeting to all its members and workers, giving a pressing invitation to both the congregation and Sunday school workers. Several friends very kindly prepared the tea and had it on the tables promptly at the time appointed. After ample justice had been done to the goods things provided tables were cleared, and the evening was spent in pleasant games. At half-past nine the meeting was closed, all being well pleased with the evening's en- joyment.</p>
        <p>LEAGUR OF THE CROSS. The usual weekly meeting of the League of the Cross was held in the League Hall, Bewsey-road, on Sunday, the choir being taken by Mr. John Mullins, president. Mr. Mullins addressed the meeting, and in a speech of great earnestness,exhorted the mem- bers, to special activity during the winter season,as it should be their harvest time. He also referred to the marked solicitude of the council of the League, for the comfort, and amusement of the members. They (the members of the Council) had spared no pains or expense, in making the rooms bright and cheerful, and in every way a formidable counter-attraction to the public-house. Mr. Mullins next referred to the important fact, that next month they would celebrate the 2.7t1i anniversary of the establishment of 'their association. At the time of the inaugu-ation,there were some wise acres, who prophesied the speedy demise of their" Cold water scheme," but thank God they were stronger than even they could have anticipated had they been amongst the few working mem who floated the good teetotal ship. Mr J. C. Dalton next ad- dressed the meeting. He commented on the base- ness and ingratitude of those Catholic people who, being so singularly blessed by God, insulted Him so frequently by the commission of the deadly mortal sin of drunkenness. They must never rest, till they had eradicated the canker of intoxication out of the Catholic body. The meeting closed by the administration of the pledge to eight men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ANNUAL WESLEY GUILD MBETINGr.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL WESLEY GUILD MBETINGr.</p>
        <p>At the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, on Sunday, special services were held in connection with the Young People's S viety, when there were large con- gregations. The preacher was the Rev. G. D. Mason. The first annual Wesley Guild meeting ef the above church was held on Monday evening, in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church. The Rev, G. D. Mason presided, and appropriate addresses were given by Miss B. Dutton. and the Rev. W. G. Triggs. Miss Dutton spoke of the divine possibili- ties there were for even the poorest, smallest, and the most sinful life, and said that however stained and defiled by sin a life might be, when the li4ht of God entered it it was altogether transformed. Referring to the difficulties that might and would be met with, she said that Christianity would not be a privilege without an opportunity of showing their courage. They lived not for what they would like to do, but for what they ought to do. She urged them to put on the harness which they must never think of putting off from the time the journey commenced to the time it ended. The Rev. G. D. Mason referred to the address of Miss Dutton, and said he was sure they all felt moved to consecrate themselves more fully to God. The Rev. W. G. Triggs spoke of the necessity of greater , earnestness among Christian people. If religion was anything it should be everything. The Church needed the conversion of the worl€l, and the world needed the conversion of the church. His prayer was that God would lead them to be serious, sober- minded, and thorough in all that they did. If a thing was worth doing it was worth doing well. The meeting concluded by singing and prayer.</p>
        <p>THRLWALL PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a meeting of the above council was held in the schoolroom, Thelwall, when there were present—the Rev. T. Pym Williamson (chairman), Admiral Tones-Parry, and Messrs. W. Tinsley, Lambert, Taylor, Warburton, W. Boughton and W. B. Cook (clerk). After the reading of the minutes the Clerk said he had received no commun- ication from the Runcorn District Council, re the foul condition of the water in the Ship Canal. With regard to the nuisance caused by the discharge of shoddy at the station the Clerk read a letter from Mr. Braide, stating that he had requested the agent ' for the Railway Company to do all he could minimise the offence.—The Clerk also informed the Council that since the letter had been written there had been no discharge of shoddy at all at the station. After a short discussion on the subject, it was seconded and carried unanimously that no further notice be taken of the matter, as there were such small quantities being discharged, until other reasons arose for so doing. A cheque for .£1 was given to the Clerk, he having paid bills to that amount during his six months term of office.—Mr. Norman Taylor said that the stream was still in a very bad state. The sewers were evidently in a defective conditien, for the water flowed away and left a sediment from which issued an abominable stench. To drain it would be very expensive, and he, therefore, proposed that the s,ream be piped up altogether, and this without delay.—Mr. W. Tinsley seconded.—Admiral Jones-Parry thought the slight defect, might be remedied. He proposed an amend- ment to the effect that the motion be abandoned tor the present.—Mr. Warburton seconded, and said that if the stream could be made clear it would be an ornament to the village.—Mr Taylor said the stream was one which would be likely to cause an outbreak of fever or some other disease —Mr. , Tinsley suggested that the resolution should not be passed until all the members of the Council were i present.—A warm discussion followed, n which Mr. Taylor said that he was anxious to me.e., the inter:- eats of the Council, and be would withdraw his resolution for the present The meeting then terminated.</p>
