        <p>LECTURES FOR THE PEOPLE AT CAIRO ST. SCHOOLROOM.</p>
        <p>OPENING MEETING. The first of a series of lectures was given at the Cairo— st. Schoolroom. on Monday evening, when the chair was occupied by Mr. William Lang, J.P. Despite the we night, there was a very large attendance. The paper which was given was by the Rev. W. Fl. Drum— mond, 8.A., and was upon "Ron,' : its monuments and its ruins." The lecture was illti.,trated by some very fine lantern slides, the lantern manipulator being Councillor F. W.Monks.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, in his opening speechoilid that during the course of last week,he met Mr. Drummond at Oxford. and he then wished him to preside at the fint of his course of loctures which he had prepared for their enjoyment and instruction during the following weeks. His (the Chairman's) fint idea was that there did not need any sort of introduction of Mr. Drummond to any audience assembled in that room, or indeed in any room in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) But on second thoughts it seemed to him fitting that on the opening lecture some other than Mr. Drummond should be pre- sent—not exactly to introduce him to them—but to soy a few words upon the subjects which it was proposed to bring before them. Of course,some of those lectures had been given before at the Literary and Philosophical Society, but that did not come within the scope of what Mr. Drummond proposed. He was glad to say that one of the distinguishing characteristics of their minister more than another—and he was sure that the people of Warrington had found it out by the present time—was that he tiled to do things for the benefit of the people— (applause)—and to bring more home to the people and the general uninstructed public, those questions that it would be of advantage to hear something upon. He thought that they ought to be grateful that there were men who made these excursions like the one the lectures that night was about, and then when they came hack could retail them out for the enjoyment of others. He was sorry that it was not in his (Mr. Long's) line, or else perhaps he could be of more use than he was. Mr. Drummond would be able to bring before them some- thing for amusement and instruction alike. (Applause.) The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said that Mr. Long had already enlarged upon the course of lectures which it was proposed to hold in that room during the coming Monday evenings. He only honed that the audience on such a wet night augured well for the attendance at the other lectures. He felt that he owed an apology for giving the first lecture. He simply held his lecture to supple the vacant date and it so happened that that was the filet night. He had arranged the lectures as Mr. Long had said both for amusement and instruction and also to give a stimulus to the imagination during the comine winter weeks. If any interest was aroused in the 1- bares he would feel perfectly satisfied. Tie cons. ' ared that he had been exceedingly fortu- nate in his friends who had so readily consented to give the lectures. He did not think that in another winter it would be so easy. With regard to the subject which had been selected for that night, he had a very delightful visit to Rome last spring, and he pro- posed to speak to them upon " Rome, its movements Red its ruins." He felt that it would be hepeless t 1, the lecture without having to his help an admirable of slides. He had been reading in one of the London papers a lettee by the well .known poet and writer on socialistic an' j •eta, Mr. William Morris, upon the proposed restoration of Rouen Cathedral. He said that it would very largely destroy the value and the beauty of the Cathedral. He observed that a building was one of the most eloquent testimonies of the past we could possibly have Old books which came from the past had been printed and reprinted many times,hut the buildings when they had not been interfered with were just as they came out of the hands of the work- men, and therefore every grand historical building was a living decument to those who were. blessed with imagination. Mr. Drummond then preceeded to give a very instructing acceunt of the general appearance of sights which were to be seen in Rome. and also the early histery, etc., and illustrated it with personal re- miniscences. At the Close a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Drummond.</p>
        <p>TIM"' BY A BUILDER'S EMPLOYE. On Wednesday at the Warrington Borough Police Court before Messrs. John Fairclough (in the chair) are John Geddes, an elderly man named John Edward Brown, resiclinr! in Bowcock-street, Town's-end, was charge-tin enstody with stealing a quantity of lead, valued at 91., from the yard of his employers, Messrs. Thomas and Walter Cross, builders. of West-stmet.— Prisoner pleaded gitilty.—The Bead Canstable explained that Brown, who had for some time been in the em- ploy of Messrs Cross as a bricklayer's labourer, was met by Detectiye-S.rgeant Monaghan om the previous night. His trtson seemed to be somewhat bulky, and the officer became suspicious. He interrogated the prisoner, who, after some he.itstion, produced the lead, saying that it had been given to him by Mr. Cross, and volunteered to accompany the officer to Mr. Cross with the object of proving his assertion. Monaghan did not do thi., how- , eyer,hut took Brown into custody,and a ftsrwards visited that Mm.hi id: snr,tni fi ehda had the heei given dtha es hmel:tnagl int Btowhwi IT er.dCeniTZ. who thereunr:n admittedorhishaAvffinegncest.oalnend ToPerdis o forn eri penxi: pre.sed enntcontritionf eney.—Detectiye Sergeant Monaghan intimated that Mr. Cross wish to rp senior gave the hhtspp c risoLieer_a_r good woodacg inhaerxacptlear, ead nddid n that though the prisoner had been before the Bench On Dr viu E, nenaginns he had not been convicted of theft.—The masistrates committed prisoner to gaol for fourteen days with hard labour, the option of a fine not being given. Canada is a Indian word,—a" collection of huts." : The new first-class battleship Victorious was launched at Chatham on Saturday, in the presence of a large and riiptinm)ighed company. which included Field-Marshal Viscount Wolseley.the Earl of Darnley, Mr. Bayard (U.S. Ambassador), and the Dean of Rochester. When the vessel was shooting down the ways a labourer sn board looked out of a porthole. His bead was caught by a wire hawser, and ha died almost Immediately.</p>
