        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WORKING A LAMS Mem—CONTRADICTORY EVIDNNCIL At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and Alderman Harrison, John Had- dock was charged with workine a horse while in an un- fit state on the 9th October; William Worrell, Whitley Superior, was charged with causing the same to be worked.—lnspector Luff, R.S.P.C.A., stated that at 11.30 on the day in question he was at Market Gate, when he saw the defendant, Haddock, with a bay mare and empty brick cart. The animal was lame on the off- foreleg. Witness stopped the man and examined the mare and found it in pain. duet() an injury to the fet- lock joint. While looking here he noticed it flinch and was going to remove the ,addle when Haddock said "It has a sore there." Witness then made an examination and found two sores under the saddle about the size, respectively, of a five and two shilling piece. Both places were very tender.—Mr. Worrell denied that the marks were sores, only abrasions and were not pressed on by the saddle. Be called William Gregory Willett, veterinary surgeon, who said that he was called in to see the mare after the inspector bad seen it. All that he found wrong was an abrasion under the saddle which did not pierce the skin. The fetlock had been blistered some three months before. It had always a peculiar action in that joint since it was a colt.—ln- spector Luff then applied for an adjournment in order to procure a witness. Worrell objected. so the Inspector pointed out that Haddock admitted there was a sore on the mare's back.—The magistrates fined Haddock in the costs and Worrell 103. ALMOST MAD WITH DRINK. Before the maistrates at the Warrington Borough Court, on Thursday, John Hares, a rough-looking pri- soner, was charged with assaulting and beating his wife, Ann Hares, in Berry's yard, at 7.20 on Wednesday night.—Prisoner pleaded sot guilty.—Sergeant Taylor said that at the time in question he was called into Berry's-yard and on going to the house of the prisoner found him lying before the .fite there, almost nude, un- conscious, and bleeding from a wound in the forehead. With same difficulty witness brought him round and at- tempted to stop the flow of blood. Prisoner,irnmediately he was aroused, seized the ashnan from the fire and at- tempted to strike his wife on the head. Witness threw him on the sofa and prevented the blow, but prisoner broke loose again and picking up the fender, attempted to strike her with that. He then seized a knife aud threatened to murder her and hang for her, and because witness attempted to prevent him going out the prisoner attempted to stab himself. He was then taken to the Infirmary where the wound on his forehead was dressed. Witness added that prisoner was drunk at the time of the disturbance and almost mad. The house presented the appearance of alslaughter-house. —Annie Hares, wife of prisoner, said that on the after- noon of Wednesday prisoner came home drunk,got some beer, and because she refused him money he struck her several times. li also wanted to fight with her brother. He got the wound on his head with falling in the yard. Every time he got drunk he "laid on" her and threatened her.—Martin M'Ouire, brother of last Witness, corroborated.—Inspector Spinks said that pri- soner was so violent that a constable had to be left in charge of him all night. He tried several times to tear the wound in his head open and behaved like a madman till morning.—Prisoner said M'Guire interfered between him and his wife and when he fell over a chair in the house someone struck him on the head.—Alderman But- ton (chairman of the bench) said it was evident that when prisoner got drunk he could not control himself in any way and became perfectly mad. He committed him to gaol for two months' hard labour, and granted the wife a separation order with 63. a week for the main- tenance of her three children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</p>
        <p>FELDAT,—Before Alderman H.arrieon (airman). Amer_</p>
        <p>roan Wright and Dr. Gornall.</p>
        <p>A MAN TA as PrrtgD.-- Thomas Haynes, Barnton WAS charged with cruelly ill-treating a horse by working the same while in an unfit state, on the 2nd October.—lnspecto Luff, R.S.P C.A., said that on the above date he was in Bridge-street and saw the defendant in charge of a brown mare and spring cart loaded with vegetables. The mare was lame on both forelegs and its knees were badly broken. The near tore-leg was made worse by the pressure of a large ring bone. On the animal's back, covered with a piece of flannel, was a raw wound about two inches tang and one wide. The mare was worn out and unfit for work. Defend- ant had told witness that be had only had it six weeks, haying given £6 for it. The man had ben taken in with it as the animal would never do him any good at all. He did not press the case.—Defenciant WAS fined 10s. including costs. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman) and Alderman</p>
        <p>Ha'rris'on:</p>
        <p>A DISIIOYEST SOlf.—Edward Greenhalgh, 17. 18. Scott- street was charged with stealing from 18. Scott-street. on Saturday morning a pair of brown boots, value 4s. and 2s. in money, the goods of Christopher Greenhaigh, his father. —Sergeant Taylor said that on Saturday afternoon he WAS called to complaint's home and there received the prisoner in custody, en a charge of stealing the boots and breaking into his father's box.—Christopher Greenhsly,h, father of tke prisoner, said his son was a very ead boy and they could do no good with him. They dare not leave anything in the house. He had been to the " Clio," 3 years, but witness thought he had been worse since he came ba-k.—When asked by the magbarat.s if he had anything to say, the rrisoner replied " No, sir." The bench thought that it would be a good thing for him to go to gaol. but on his promising to reform and go to sea they dism awl him with a conviction recorded against him.—Mr ff. Taylor, P.O M. promised to find the lad a ship at Liverpool. PAsvmsta A RaoTuRR'A CoAT.—John O'Brien, 5, Platt-st.' was charged with stealing from 5. Platt-st., on Wednesday. the 16th in.t , a man's coat, value 155., the property of Thomas O'Brien. Detective-Sergeant Monaghan said de- fendant was a labourer residing in Platt-st.. with his brother. The latter had put hi best clothes away a few days before the theft but when he came to require them he found the coat in question missing. Enquiries were made and defendant Via% found to have pledged the coat at Mor- ton's shop in St. James-at. He had a good home but was continually upsetting it by drinkb.g. He was fine' 10s. and Costs. DRIITNIC AND DISORDERLY —Patrick Garrity, Great. Sankey. was fined 5s and costs for being drunk and disorderly on Sunday morning : Patrick Borke. Silver-at., was similarly dealt with for the same offence, and likewise Margaret Griffin, 23. Warhurton-st. DIRER MONTHS FOR WIRE DFSERTION.—Peter Salford, was charged on a warrant with running away and leaving his wife an four children chargeable to the Warring- ton Union, since ihe lOth July, 1894.—Mr. Shearer pr sc- ented and ssi 1 that defentiani had been sent to gaol for two months for the same offence before, and he now pressed the case against him. Fo• some time past pris-ner had been working at Ssiford and ,arning s'd an hour, but he had during the whole lime onl y gent his family 14s. He seemed determined not to stipplrl, them.—The magistrates com- mitted him for 31months' hard labour. A CAB-DRIVER FINED FOR DR ITNREICNESS. Thomas lirfinlre, cab-driver, Tfavciock-street, was charged with being drunk in charge of a horse and cab in Win wick-street, on the 13th inst.—He pleaded guilty.—Sergeant Ch,ney proved the case and mentioned that defendant bore a god character.— A tine of 2s. 6d. incluling costs was imposed. Du BADGE AGATIL—WiIIIaM Collllor. driver. 85, Knuts- ford-road was fined 2s. 6d. and cos's for not having his badge distinctly visible on the lOtts Oct ,her.—P.C. Fronk Cooper proved the offence and said that defendant did not stop when he called to him. TITESDAY.—Before Mr. B. J. Guest (in the chair), and Dr. Smith. NEGLECT er FAMIT.T.—Walter Clarke wss charged with neglecting to maintsin ts wife and two children, thereby causing them in become chargeable to the Warrington Union during the past 14 months.—Mr. Basil Shearer, relieving</p>
        <p>and inquiry officer, stated that the defendant had been in work, but he could not say where, and the woman and her children were in the Union at the present time.—Several witnesses were called whose evidence went to show that the defendant had been in work, and that this was a case of pure neglect. —Defendant, who stated that he had only done "a tit of work "and was willing to live with his wife. was sen ho prison for one month with hard labour. THIIIISDAY.—Bef ore Alderman Sutton (chairman) and Lieut.</p>
        <p>Colonel Ridgway. A RUNAWAY PARENT.—James Fairhurst, an elderly man, was charged, having been arrested on a warrant, with having since October 1893 ran away from Warrington leaving his child claargerble to the Warrington Unlon.—Mr. B. Shearer, enquiry officer, prosecuted and said that the prisoner's daugh- ter was admitted to the workhouse in September 1890 and a warrant taken out for his apprehension. He was found in the town on Wednesday. His daughter had remained at the Workhouse until she was old enough to go out in service and she had cost them some £5O or £55 for maintenance. Prisoner had twice before been prosecute.l for neglect and Mr. Shearer now pressed the case against him—Prisoner was com- mitted to gaol for two months hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
