        <p>TRANSFERS —The temporary transfers of the following licenses were granted :—Waterloo Hotel. High-street, from Robert J. H. Dyer (deceased) to Bllen Dyer, his widow; Shamrock Inn, Regent-street. from David Shaw to John Pye; Nelson Hotel, High-street, from Thomas Blease to Henry Arnold; Volunteer Inn, Granville-street, from George Waugh to John Sadler. lINSIIIEELED DOGS.—Annie Warner, 4. Irwell-lane, was summoned for allowing a dog to be at large in Hartley-et. in ' contravention of the Rabies (luzzling of Dogs) Order of 1892.—Mr. Allen, solicitor, represented the defendant who pleaded guilty. A fine of 53. including costs was imposed.— John Newby, 39, Brook-street, Runcorn, :yid Robert Wake- field. Egerton-street were similarly de .It with for a like offence.— P.C. Wylie proved the cases. OBSTRUCTING THE FOOTWAY.—George Watson, Joseph Prescott and Christopher Inman, youths about 15 years of age, were summoned for obstructing the lootway in Egerton- street by loitering thereon to the obstruction and anpoyance of the passengers --P.C. Gregory stated that On the 28th of the previous month he saw the defendants in company with two others, loitering on the footpath in Egerton-street. They were smoking cigarettes and spitting on the path. He had cautioned them previously, and there had been many c on- plaints.—Superinten.tent Atherton said the street was very rowdy, caused by the defendants and others. He hadwart,ed several of them himself.—The Chairman in ordering tho defendants to pay the costs, severely repremanded them, and said that if they appeared before them again they would be more severely dealt with. NEW RATE I.—The magistrates affixed their signatures to the following new rates :—Halton, Is. 2d, In the E (2d. less than the preceding year) estimated to produce £351 2s. 65. ; Stoekham Is. in the 2 (reduction of 25.) estimated to realise £46 21. ; Clifton, 105. in the E to produce £l7O 171. 90.</p>
