      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RUGBY. THE NORTHERN UNION. WARRING CON v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. THE NORTHERN UNION. WARRING CON v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</p>
        <p>The meeting of these clubs on Saturday, amused great interest in Warrington, and the Wilderspool en- closure was well patronised, there being about 4,000 present. It is some years since the clubs met before, and then, Trinity were at the zenith of their fame. The formation of the Northern Union has caused the old time fixture to be renew ed, but in the meantime Wake- field have become much weakened. Since the com- mencement of the present season the committee have made vigorous efforts to put new life into the club, and have included several Welshmen in the team, including Davies, a Welsh international forward. " Dicky " Lockwood, the captain of the English team in 1833, has also thrown in his lot with them and donned their jeney for the first time this season. His figure has, however, grown so rotund that he was not of very much I use. He knew how to judge his kicks, but running was I quite out of the question. The game on Saturday was a very enjoyable one,and it is evident that the new War- rington forwards are getting more combined. Mr. Sleyin, of Wigan, was the referee at the match, this be- ing the third time this season he has " refereed "in matches in which Warrington have taken part. The I following were the players under his control : Warrington : Heesom, full back ; Barber, Cross, Bur- ton, and Broady, threequarters ; Nevins and Houghton, half backs; Burrows, Saunders, Fairhurst, Barrett, Andrews, Thornton, Hill, and Wilcock, forwards. Wakefield Trinity : Griffiths, full back ; Todd, Lock- wood, Gameson, and Wood, threequarters ; Bedford and Morgan, half bacl6s ; Walton, Davies, Eyre, Allchurch, Dawson, Thresh, Moorhouse, and Price,forwards. Barber won the toss and during the first half, War- rington defended the pavilion end. Thresh kicked off, and lioughton's return not counterbalancing it, the first scrimmage took place in the home quarters, but imme- diately upon the ball becoming loose, Cross, Burton, and Fairhurst dribbled to past half way,and the game con- tinued in the Wakefield lines almost the whole of the first half. After the above mentioned dribble, which was stopped by Gameson, Houghton kicked out of the scrim- mage and being well supported by Nevins, further ground was gained. Lcedcwerel wa.i well bowled over by Burton in attempting to break ewer, and the attack was still continued. An attempt to drop a goal by Burton was the cause of the first touch down being conceded by Trinity. From the kick out. Barber replied by a fine kick which went into touch just at the corner, and a fierce struggle took place at this point, four or five scrim- mages taking place on the line. The pressure was relieved a little but suddenly Barber was seen at top speed and dodged all his opponents with the exception of Griffiths who floored him just on the line. Gameson and Todd dribbled away from the ensuing scrimmage, but Cross. ended their career. Wakefield attempted another rush but Heesom was quite safe, Davies being brought to the ground long before the half way line was reached. Nevins now secured and passing Ito Burton, the latter doubled Lockwood and (Jameson, and finding Griffiths in his way, he passed to Broady who had, the misfortune of being knocked in touch at the corner flag. Within a minute later, Broady was again knocked in touch at the same spot, and Wakefield thus had the narrowest of escapes twice in one minute. Following these two " let L,ITA, Wakefield played up, and for the first time since the first minute of the game, crossed the centre line, Morgan,Walton and Allchurch being prominent leaders in the rush, but Cross speedily sent them back again before they had any opportunity of gettirg over. Griffiths was finely tackled by Broady before he could reply to Burton's kick, and another " hot time" ensued for Wakefield with no tangible result however. A brilliant round of passing between Todd. Lockwood, Gameson and Wood relieved somewhat, but this was completely nullifi'A by an effort in which the ball passed through the hand • , Nevins, Houghton, Broady, Burton, Cross, and Barber, the captain finishing by a kick which coat led Hill to secure the ball and the latter alter a grand run was tackled very near the line. Houghton now made his mark in front of the posts, but gurton's kick at goal was charged down and from now to half- time, play was more equal. Nothing .bad been scored up to the interval. Hill kicked off after the interval and Lockwood in attempting to return, missed his kick and the ball rolled into touch near the quarter flag. Tim home forwards rushed away with the ball from the line out, and offside play by Wakefield, caused them to be penalised right under the goal posts. Heesom, however, failed to utilise the easy place, and nothing but a minor resulted. Thresh dropped out, and Burton securing passed to Broady, and the latter running at tremendous speed, seemed likely to scare, but Griffiths just in time man- aged to rob him of the ball and regained almost all lost ground. Play continued for some minutesin the centre, when suddenly, a passing bout between Nevins, Cross and Burton, ended in Burton making a long pass to Broady, and the latter though near the touch line, and at a very difficult angle steadied himself and making a tremendous punt, a huge shout proclaimed the fact that the ball hed gone over the crossbar, and Warrington were four points ahead, and one of the best dropped goals seen for a long time on the Warrington ground had been scored. The restart almost resulted in disaster to Warrington, for Houghton mulled the kick off, and Eyre almost got over, Cross. Barber and Nevins just staving off the danger in time. The Warrington forwards quickly asserted themselves,and Barber accept- ing a pass near the quarter flag, he threaded his way through the Trinity team, and eventually scored a try, which Heesorn failed to convert. Wakefield attempted feebly to retrieve themselves near the end of the game but they could not make any headway against the watchfulness and energy of the Warrington team, and at the close had to submit to defeat by a dropped goal and a try (seven points) to nil. Warrington fairly. beat Wakefield on all points, but the game was nevertheless a very interesting one, and fought with the greatest good temper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LEIGH V. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>LEIGH V. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Played at Leigh, before 3,000 spectators. Widnes started the game. The home forwards dribbled to the Widnes quarter, where Golding stopped the rush. Good work by Drummond and Barber, followed by a rush on the part of the visiting forwards, removed play to the Leigh quarters, where the home players carried a scrim- mage, but lost the ball, and Molyoeux was called upon to save. Play up to this had been of a scrambling char- acter, but at length brilliant passing and running by Spillane and Anderton caused Widnes to defend, and at half-time there was no score. Leigh played with great dash in the second half, and pressed their opponents back upon their lines. Spillane eventually cr.ssed the line from a pass by Anderton, but the ball was called back fer some irregularity. The traria team twic- crossed the line but were called back. Johnson charged down Coon's kick, and Widnes crossed the Leigh line, a try being allowed. Barber converted. The referee ap- pointed by the Northern Union failed to put in an ap- pearance, and Leigh was so dissatisfied with the deci- sion of the gentleman who acted in his stead that they 1</p>
        <p>entered an appeal against the match being counted in the competition. Result :—Widnes 1 goal (5 points) to Leigh nil.</p>
        <p>BATLEY V. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Played on Saturday at Batley. The home team won the toss, and W. Faulkner started the game, a good return being made by Goodall. Smart passing between Myers and Warder gained some ground, but a return in reply to a free kick landed the ball back to the centre line. Runcorn reached the home 25, where a piece of smart work ended in Dunbavin, one of the visiting half backs, getting ever. The referee, however, ruled the ball forward, and a scrimmage was formed a few yards from the line. Lowrie and one or two others of the home forwards relieved, further ground being gained by Jolley mulling tile leather. Batley had now a look in, Allen picking up and making a rush,but was brought to grass. Just after this Shackleton looked like scoring, but Run- corn, by good combined play, worked the ball out of danger. The game fluctuated a good deal till at last Myers started a grand tun, and pissed to Robieson,who quickly transferred to Warder. The movement, how- ever, was unsuccessful. A minute later the same backs took part in another piece of play which sheuld have ended in a try,and Batley eventually reached the centre. At thisjuncture Abrams passed to Robinson, who threw to Warder, but being smartly Fold by Shaw, lost the leather. The ball was passed by Jimmy Naylor to his brother Joe,.who transferred to Goodall. The last- named kicked across the field, and Munro rushed up, scored a splendid try, which Fisher converted. Subse- quently play was in favour of the home side, ' and at half-time the score stood—Batley, one goal; Runcorn, nil. The second half was commenced by the ball being carried over the goal-line twice by Welsh and Goodall, but nothing came of the efforts. Both sides manifested some keenness to make headway. Batley had the best of matters, and strove hard to increase their points. Runcorn on one occasion had hard lines in not scoring. Johnson falling a yard short of the line. Ab_ams gained a try, which Dunbavin succeeded in improving,the game ended in a draw. Result :—Batley, 1 goal (5 points) ; Runcorn, 1 goal (5 points). WARRINGTON" A " v. RUNCORN "A."—The Warring- ton " A" journeyed to Runcorn on Saturday to play the Runcorn " A" team for tt e second time this season. The weather was fine and the field in good condition. The Warrington team kicked off and soon after West, with a good run, returned the ball to the Warrington 25, where it went in touch. From the line out it was passed from Pendlebury to Gayter and Lightfoot who banded it to Gregory, the latter cooling a try behind tie posts. Lightfoot took the kick and converted it, Runcorn thus scoring a goal within five minutes of the commence- ment. Lawless sent the ball down the field by a fine kick, but the Runcorn men quickly returned it to the visitors quarterswhere Gregory got possession and rushed to the line, but he was forced to kick and the ball dropped near the goal line. Carey got possession and passed to Lawless, who passed to Foster, but the ball fell short and West scored a try, being loudly cheered tsr s good play. Butterworth failed to convert. Fos- , ;er iiade an attempt to get a try but Butterworth got ! ,1 of the ball and kicked it to the other end of the titid. Lawless and Carey made several good runs with the ball but they did not gain much. VVorrall took the ball well down the field and Urmston took it up and scored another try, which Lightfoot tried to convert,but failed. Half time was called soon afterwards. During the second half the Runcorn men were not so active as be- fore and Warrington prevented any further scoring, al- though both sides indulged in some very good play, rushes for the post being frequently made, but neither side could gain any advantage. For the last ten or fif- teen minutes the play was exciting, as Warrington had to play without Foden, who had been badly kioked, but they were quite able to defend their goal. Time was ' called with the score :—Ruccorn one goal, two tries, to Warrington nil. The following were the players : Runcorn. full back, Butterworth ; threequarter backs, West, J. Lightfoot, Gayter and Gregory ; half backs, Pendlebury and Worrall ; forwards, D. Lightfoot, Urm- son, Jeacock, Jones, Clarke, Richardson, Houghton and Shaw. Warrington, full back, Evans ; three quarter backs, Carey, Lawless, Isherwood, and Forster ; half backs, Foden and Price ; forwards, Hughes, Taylor Iligham, Hassan, Monks, Royle, Savage and Schultz.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE v. CHESHIRE. The first of the county matches under the new Union took place at Stockport on Monday, in wretched wea- ther, which somewhat spoiled the interest in the event. There was practically no alteration in the teams, the Cestrians turning out as selected, whilst Doherty (St. Helens)ltook the place of Hurst (Oldham), in the Lan- cashire team. The following was the composition of the teams :—Cheshire, Saville (Stockport) back; War- der, Robinson (Rmicorn), Worsley and Bailey (Stock- port), three-quarter backs ; Abrams and J. Faulkner (Runcorn), half-hacks ; W. Faulkner, Taylor, Langley, Farmer (Runcorn), Mottershead, Morton, Gibeon, and Wrigley (Stockport), forwards. Lancashire :—Winstan- ley (Wigan),back ; Barber (Warrington) ,Lees (Oldham), Chapman (Broughton Rangers), and Doherty (St. Helens), three-quarter backs ; Vorley and A. Lee (Old- ham), half IPacks ; Bongo). (Oldham), Nevins (Warring- ton), Worthington (Tyldesley), Unsworth, Brown (Wigan). and Roberts (Tyldesley), Briars (St. Helens), and Smith (Leigh), forwards. Referee, Mr. Hutchin- son, Wakefield). There would be about 3,090 people present when Unsworth started. An infringe- ment caused the ball to be scrimmaged at centre. Cheshire soon forged ahead, and Doherty was tackled in his own quarters. Varley gave relief with a judicious kick, and some passing initiated by Abrams and .1. Faulkner was intercepted, and the Lancashire forwards went down in a body and things seems ominous for Cheshire when Saville got his kick charged down, and a free kick falling, the Lancastrians had a good chance for goal, but the ball sailed outside. Relief was forthcoming ; the Cheshire forwards, led by Faulkner, Langley and Farmer, rushed to the other end, Lee saying daringly. Kicking between Saville and Winstanley almost ended in disaster for the Red Rose representatives, when the Wigan man failed to hold the ball, but an injudicious kick sent the ball dead. Cheshire now experienced hard lines when J. Faulkner made a brilliant run through, and passing to Warder the latter was shoved in touch in goal when in the act of ground- ing, and only a minor was given. Lancashire after this played strongly, and Robinson checked a dangerous dribble by Varley ; Saville secured and ran across but Bailey failed to accept his pass and Barber racing up the Cheshire lines were assailed. Passing amongst the Lancashire backs ended in Varley trying a drop at val. Cheshire retaliated, and Bailey was given a chance but Barber proved the stumbling block. Exciting play now followed when Lancashire bore down and after some passing Chapman scored at the corner the same player kicking for goal, hot he made a miser- able attempt. The interval arriving with the score. Lancashire 1 try, Cheshire nil. W. Faulkner re-started the second moiety, and the first packl was formed near centre. Cheshire screwed the scrimmage finely, and S. Lees was temporarily disabled in stopping the rush. The ball was sent into touch, and from the line out the Ches- hire forwards rushed to the Lancashire end, and a try seemed imminent, when Winstanley saved in a dating manner. The Lancashire back a moment later got his side out of difficulty which gained a lot of ground,Bailey failing to hold the greasy ball. Excitement ran high when Abrams started a round of passing,but a miserable pass to the Stockport man lost a certain try. Bonser led the Lancashire forwards in a dribble and Saville nearly let them in by getting his kick charged down, but Warder was at hand, and got the ball to touch. Lance- shire maintained the pressure, but by. short stages the Cestriane worked out of danger and Chapman saved an- other ugly rush. From the following pack Unsworth made a sensational dribble,and kicked over the Cheshire line, but Bailey beat him in the race for possession. Worseley made a couple of fine runs, bet the proffered chances were not accepted,and Briars and Bonser cleared their lines, and the Lancashire backs indulged in a lot of passing, Robinson grassing Lees when making off at a great pace. Following a lot of kicking. the ball was kicked by Sam Lees, and Barber being onside, he beat Bailey for possession, and doubling Saville, he scored a really fine try, Winstanley ailing to add the major point. At this point Lancashire were having much the best of it, and Doherty was nearly in, when Warder got him. The concluding portion of the came was fought almost in darkness,a heavy mist falling ever the ground, and the came had to be concluded five minutes before time, the final result being a victory for Lancashile by 2 tries to nothing. - -</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MART'ti v. STICRPORT " A."—Played at Stockport on Saturday. The home team won the toss, arid St. Mary's started the game from the town and, and after the usual preliminaries the Clarets got a free kick, Cnorlton kicking to the visitors' 25. The Warrington team were hard pressed for the first ten minutes, Chorlton scoring for the home team, a goal re- sulting. The home half was now invaded, the home custodian touching down. Not to he denied the visi- tors returned to the attack,and R. Bennett scored at the corner after a grand run, the kick at goal failing. Give- and-take play follow,d in midfield, and then Kemp in- tercepted a pass, but Roughley tackled well. Half-time was now called. Chorlton re-started, and the visitors immediately asserted themselves, pressing strongly. Brown made his mark, hut gained little ground. A grand combined effort by the Saints' forwards resulted in Caul well scoring under the posts,T Bennett convert- ing. Good runs by•Warburton and Higginbottom fol- lowed, the latter being held by the full back. After some very even play Pownall threw the ball to Vernon, who scored, the place kick going wide. The scores were now even, and both sides worked hard to gain the mastery. The ball travelled from end to end with rapidity, but at length Siddall sdo-ed at the corner, a grand game resulting in a win for the Saints by one goal, two trieg, to one goal, one try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 26, 1896</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 26, 1896</p>
        <p>the attack, and only the fine play of Mahony in goal kept them from scoring. Saint's forwards now got pos- session and at once ;made tracks for the other end, Glavin scoring. From the restart Sutton bore down on Mahony's charge which had severed narrow escapes, the ball finally going ovor. The home custodian in taking the goal kick failed to rise the ball and it striking a Sut- ton player rebounded through the goal, thus equalising the score. This mistake seemed to put a damper on the Saints, and Sutton coming down scored again, half-time arriving with the score, Sutton 2, St. Mary's 2. The second half opened very fast, the visitors being in fine form. The Saints had a long spell of defending, cor- ners falling fast to Sutton from one of which they scored their third goal. The Saints livened up after this, but the fine play of the Sutton backs prevented them from coming dangerous, time arriving with the score Sut- ton 3, St. Mary's 1.</p>
        <p>BOLT TRINITY V.WARRINGTON OLYMPICS.—ThiB match was played at Howley on Saturday. The Trinitarians vigorouely attacked the visitors' defence, T. Miller scoring two goals to the visitors' nil in the first half. Resuming, the second half was a repetition of the first. The homesters missed several chances through rough shooting. They kept up the attack to the finish, and succeeded in registering five more goals, A. Pugh two, J.,Pugh two, and Seddon one. Final result :—Holy Trinity, seven goals: Warrington Olympics, nit. Pluegirrn V. H &amp;TOO= Ricszays.—This match was played on Saturday, at Penketh. The home team won the toss, and elected to kick up field. As the visitors had been delayed on their journey, it was 4.30 before they kicked cif. The game was very keen and fast throughout, both teams working hard to gain the lead. Half time was reach without either team having scored. Soon after the re-start the Penketh men got well down in goal, and Lloyd put the ball through amidst great cheering. Soon afterwards the Haydock forwards got away, and their left wing shot in well, but Bowyer cleared, and the florae forwards were beginning to again look dangerous when the whistle sounded for time, two half-hours having been played. Final result :—Penketh, 1 goal ; Haydock Reserve, nil. Mr. John Lewis officiated as referee. Esau:nom; V. HINDLEY.—These teams met to play a friendly fixture on the Mesnes ground, Earlestown, on Saturday. They are both in the Lancashire Alliance, and this fact excited a good deal of interest in the game as showing the relative strengths of the teams. Earles- town was fully represented, but Hindley were abort of a couple of their first team men. Hindley kicked off down hill, and at once assumed the offensive, keeping Earles- town penned in their own quarters for some time. Play was very fast for the first quarter of an hour, at the end of which time Hindley drew first blood. Earlestown made strenuous efforts to equalise, but it was not until close upon the interval that they succeeded in doing so. On changing ends, Earlestown having the advantage of hill took up the attack, and considerably pressed their opponents, but owing to the excellent defence of Hind- ley they were unable to get an openirg. Edmondson, the new forward, put in some splendid play, his centring from the wing being marvellous,but on several occasions he was badly supported. Eventually Earlestown wakened up, and four goals were scored in quick suc- cession, Lee being responsible for three and Topping one, I the game ending in a win for Earlestown by five goals I to one. —•</p>
        <p>-- --- RUNCORN ASSOCIATION V. STOCKTON HEATH.—These teams met for the first time at Runcorn on Saturday. The Blues were without Paton, their centre half, but Carter filled the vacancy whilst Green partnered Gone at full back. Peacock having won the toss Mr. Grounds gave the signal for hostilities to commence when the teams were seen to line up as follows :—Runcorn : goal Hough ; full backs Gorle and Green ; half backs Pye, Carter and Clarke; forwards Bloor, Walker, Bailie, Jones and Peacock. Stockton Heath : goal Rose ; full backs Baldwin and Probert ; half backs Onions, Budge and Tipping ; forwards Johnson, Podmore, Hallows, Webb, Evans. Hallows kicked off but Carter at once secured and Peacock and Jones were soon in evidence but Bald- win cleared just in time The Blues were soon back, however, and Peacock centred, Bailie receiving sent goalwards, the ball cannoning off Probert to Bloor who promptly despatched the leather through the goal. Encouraged by this early success the Blues played up well and very soon Peacock again centred and Bailie headed past Rose in prime fashion. Johnson and Pod. more now made a spurt but were easily defeated by Genie who kicked well up field and Peacock again receiv- ing gave to Bailie who once more banged past Rose, giv- ing him no chance whatever to save. The visitors now made desperate efforts to break through the home defence, but Pye and Carter proved sufficient barrier for the whole quintette and the Blues were soon in attend- ance at the other end where Rose distinguished himself greatly, clearing a very smart shot from long range by Carter. Rudge and Baldwin kicked well and relieved time after time when the Blues were pressing dangerously. The Heathens' forwards did but badly during the first half only forcing the ball over the home goal line once, Gorle and Green being almost spec- tators. No further success attended the efforts of the homesters during the first portion of the game and half time arrived with the Blues leading by 3 goals, After a short interval Bailie again commenced business, and Bloor and Walker were soon making tracks for Rose but were beaten back and Evans and Webb made off only to be pulled up by Pye. The Heathens' defence wis uove very fine and beat all efforts by the Blues forwards aid Budge kicked up aqd where Hallows secured and had a nice opening, but he tripped over the ball and lost pos- session. Webb tried hard to score, but Green cleared with a gigantic kick. Carter got possession and sent on to Bailie who again succeeded in placing it out of the reach of Rose. The Heathens now made great efforts to reduce the score but the home halves were too attentive and would give them little chance. Webb got in a fine screw shot but it was cleared. Time was now approach- ing and the Blues put in some good work, and Peaco.:k screwing back Carter received and forwarded to Bailie who ultimately passed to Walker, and the latter evading Budge and Probert in neat style sent in a regular scorcher which again heat Rose. Final result :—Run- corn 5 goals, Stockton Heath nil. RcnscoaN;Assoctariox v. STOCKTON HEATH (Reseave Taut).—At Stockton Heath on Saturday. Runcorn were very weak, but wen by ene goal to nil, Crossley being the only successful scorer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIATION LEAGUE, 2ND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIATION LEAGUE, 2ND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. SUTTON COMBINA TION.-- I This return match was played at Warrington. Sutton bringing a good team while Ht. Mary's were without E. Mather who was assisting the premier club to defeat Rt. Elphin'g. St. Mary's losing the toss started the game: but Sutton by good coinbination at once toek u</p>
        <p>t,afti,44o;nc</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>The business connected with the meeting of the Geosral Committee of the Northern Rugby Union, ad- journed at Huddersfield last week, was resumed in Man- chester on Tuesday evening. Mr. H. H. Waller (Brig- house) presided, and amongst thme present was Messrs. J. H. Yates (Leigh), C. A. Brewer (Hull), W. Carroll (Runcorn), T. Wright (flunslet), W. Hutchinson (Wake- field), D. F. Burnley (Batley),J. E. Warren (Warrington), A. Fatterini (Manningham), J. Davies (Huddersfield), J. Mitchell (Halifax), J. Underwood (Wigan), U. Rowson ( Tyldeeley), H. Sewell (Leeds), F. Hewlett (St. Helens), and J. Platt (Oldham), hon. secretary.—lt was resolved that the meetings of the Union should be held monthly alternately in Manchester and Huddersfield, those in Manchester being on the first Tuesday in the month, and those at Huddersfield on the first 'Thursday.—An Emer- gency Committee was also elected to deal with the ap- pointment of referees and all objections to the decisions of referees, and reported players and clubs. The follow- ing gentlemen were elected on this Committee :—Messrs. J. E. Warren, J. Platt. E. Gresty, J. 13. Smith, J. M. Milne, H. H. Waller, J. Nichal, A. Fattorini, J. Clifford, and H. Sswell.—Mr. Nichol withdrew the motion he had given [notice of with regard to professionalism, and the propmition that no players suspended by the English Rugby Union should be admitted into the Northern Union—The necretaq was instructed to write to the various clubs in the Union asking them to nominate re- presentatives on the General Committee. The Leigh v. Whines match played last Saturday also came under notice. The referee complained of the conduct of the spectators, whilst Leigh appealed against a decision of the referee. The matters will be dealt with at a meet- ing of the Emergency Committee to be held in Manches- ter next Tuesday, when other subjects of importance will be discussed.</p>
        <p>Those who sympathise with the afflicted will rejoice with Mrs. Priest, 4, Magadala Cottages, Hounslow, whose recovery from Bright's Disease hy the use of Warner's Safe Cure has created such extensive and favourable comment. The swelling of the body and extremities, the racking pains, the enormous quantities of albumen passed, proved she was a victim te this dreadful malady—in fact, the physicians admit the diagnosis to have been correct. Her statement explains all.—" During the early part of last year I was stricken with Bright's Disease of the Kidneys, originating from a simple cold, and after being attended•by my family doc- tor for some considerable time he informed me that he could do me no good. I then became an in-patient of the West London Hospital, and after eleven weeks' treatment there the physician in charge gave me up,and I was discharged as incurable. My family and friends —in fact all who saw me—thought my recovery was impossible—au the swelling had then extended from my ankles over the entire body. To make matters worse, after my return from the hospital I caught a fresh cold, which brought on bronchitis and pleurisy. I again called a local doctor, but derived no benefit from a month's tut thee treatment. I suffered excruciating pain in the shoulder-blades ; pains shooting through me, even when sitting and lying, and my strength seemed quite exhausted. At this period, my father (Mr. A. Brown, mace-bearer of the Corporation, Chequer, Devizes), who , can vouch for my statement, sent me a battle of War- ner's Safe Cure, and after taking the second dose I felt relief ;by the time I had finished the contents of the bottle I felt quite another woman. I continued taking it ; the relief being so marked that I eagerly looked for the time to come round for each succeeding dose. After taking fifteen bottles I am glad to say I was completely cured. It is with gratitude and heartfelt thanks that I tender m 7 testimony to the efficacy of Warner's Safe Cure and shall never fail to recommend it."</p>
        <p>The air brake of an engine on the Western Railway failed to act on Tuesday afternoon as the train was en- tering Mont Parnasse station, Paris. The engine dashed through the wall of the station into the street below. A woman who was conversing with her husband in the street was killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The first Saturday match at home which the Warring- ton team as at present constituted have had ,has resulted in a win of seven points to nil for Warrington. Wake- field Trinity, who were the victims,were no match what- ever for Warrington, and were utterly beaten at all points, and the score with a little luck would have been much heavier for Warrington. The game was a fine one from a spectator's point of view,as some very attractive football was witnessed during the afternoon.</p>
        <p>It is a little singular that on this occasion as on the last time that Wakefield Trinity were here, a dropped goal was scored by Warrington. Dropped goals are not of very frequeat occurrence on the Warrington side, but this season they have figured a little too frequently against them. Broady is to be congratulated upon the clever drop he made on Saturday, and it is also pleasant to notice that Barber has at length broken the spell of bad luck which he has gone through, Placed goals ap- pear to be "off "with Warrington, for out of eight tries they have scored this season only one has been converted into a goal, and singular to say that caused a victory to be credited to Warrington.</p>
        <p>In the Saturday match every department of the War- rington team was superior to the Trinity team. The Trinity forwards were heavier than Warrington,but they lacked the energy and smarteess of the latter, among whom Hill, Fairhurst, and Barrett were very conspicu- ous. There is one feature of the game in which the War- rington forwards have yet to meet their superiors, and that is their dribbling. They excel in this, and as it is both pretty to watch and also very dangerous to the I other side,they should stick to this accomplishment.</p>
        <p>The Warrington halves were much more energetic than their vis-h-vis, while three-quarters were miles ahead. Gameson was by far the best of the Wakefield team, but the erstwhile invincible Lockwood, is now too rotund to be of much use. His size was a matter of astonishment to those who saw him a few years ago with Dewsbury at Warrington. He is in fact almost as tall when he is lying down as when he is standing up. Needless to say his running powers were things of the past and in striking contrast to those he displayed on his last visit to Warrington when he scored four tries. In fact, he never was in any way dangerous. Barber,Bur- ton and Broady, were in brilliant form, while Cross was all there when wanted. Although not se brilliant as the othes three, he is a very reliable back. Heesom had not very much to do, but did that little well. His goal kicking was, however, a little " off."</p>
        <p>In consequence of the win on Saturday Warrington moved up two in the league, and but for Batley pre- suming to draw, and Widnes having the impudence po win at Leigh on Saturday, they would have moved up four. However moving up two is better than sticking in the last four, and should Warrington be successful in beating Stockport to-day, they will have a chance of getting into quite a respectable position in the table.</p>
        <p>Some very great surprises resulted from the matches In the Northern League on Saturday. Who for instance expected Widnes to take down Leigh on the latter's ground? Leigh have been the surprise of the League and had actually climbed to the second position, ard then to be beaten by a club which was fifth from the bottom and on their own ground too ! The result of the match was so unpalatable that it has been appealed against by Leigh. The Northern Union will, however,be unwise to begin meddling in matters of this sort,for if they should over-rule a referee they will have no end of appeals. Widnes at the commencement of the match offered to reckon the game as a friendly one, when the referee did not make his appearance, but Leigh refused and they, therefore, had no one to blame but themselves for the result. The match has caused Leigh to drop to the fourth position.</p>
        <p>I commented last week upon the want that Oldham have of a goal kicker, and this was emphasised on Saturday, when by failing to convert a try, they were beaten by Bradford. The " Roughyeds" have made but a moderate show in the competition yet, as they have lost as many matches as they have won, and are only in the middle of the league.</p>
        <p>St. Helene are the most fortunate team in the league. They have won five games out of eight, and drawn one, and yet they have only scored 30 points to 27. Most of their victories have been of the narrowest character but of course sufficient to give them two points on the league table. On Saturday, they only beat Hull by a penalty goal, and it will be remembered that after hav- ing the worst of the game with Warrington they won by a try to nil. They beat Broughton Rangers by two points only, and Runcorn and Rochdale Hornets by a goal. They have thus had all the luck that has been going about in these matches.</p>
        <p>--- Stockport who will be engaged by Warrington to-day, came a cropper at the hands or feet of Liversedge on Saturday, 4 goals 2 tries to a try being the result; while P,tIPV and Runc,r cb.avr. The victors in the other os were Tylrly, Manninghsin, Halifax, Leeds, and Brighouse Rangers while the losers were Broughton Rangers, Wigan, Rochdale Hornets,, Huddersfield, and Hun stet.</p>
        <p>The Northern League table this week is as follows '— Score. Score. Pts. PM. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag'st. Bradford Br'ghouse Ran. ... 6 ... 1 ... 1 ... 51 ... 8 ... 13 Halifax Leigh St. Helens Runcorn 4 . 2 ... 2 ... 72 ... 32 ... 10 Manninghant ... 5 ... 3 ... 0 ... 45 ... 35 ... 10 Leeds Ilversedge Wigan 3 2 3 .27 21 9 Oldham Tyldesley Hull Hunsiet Widnes Batley 2 4 2 .28 .44 Warrington Stockport Hudders6eld ... 2 ... 9 ... 0 ... 32 ... 58 ... Wakefield T ... 2 ... 6 0 22 ... 83 ... Beghen Ran ... 1 ... 6 1 ... 25 ... 48 ... Rochdale H ... 1 ... 6 ... 1 ... 16 ... 96 ...</p>
        <p>The following is the table of the Lancashire and Claeshire'clubs</p>
        <p>Score Score Pt. PI&amp; Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag'st. Runcorn St. Helens 5 4 . 1 0 ... 21 ... 9 ... 8 Leigh Wigan Tyldesley Stock port Widnes Rochdale H 4 ... 1 ... 2 ... 1 ... ... 38 ... 3 Warrington B'g'ht'n Ran 6 ... 0 ... 5 ... 1 ... 34 ... 1 Oldham 1 . 0 . 1 . 0 ... 3 ... 11 ... 0</p>
        <p>The following are the positions of the Yorkshire clubs up to date :</p>
        <p>Score Score Pt s. rid. Won. Lost. Dna. For. Ag'ilt. B'ghouse Ban. 6 ... 4 ... 1 ... 0 30 ... 3 ... Leeds Manninglalm 4 ... 3 ... 1 ... 0 ... 29 ... 9 ... 6 Halifax Batley 4 . 2 2 0 .30 .17 .. 4 Hull 4 2 2 0 9 10 4 Bradford Li versed ge 3 ... 1 ... 2 ... 0 ... 11 ... 11 ... Hunelet Huddersfield 5 ... 1 4 ... 0 ... 16 ... 29 ... Walcefl'd Tri'tv 5 ... 1 ... 4 ... 0 ... 9 ... 60 ...</p>
        <p>Either Warrington or Stockport will break a record to-day. Thus far, Warrington have not won a match away, and Stockport have not won a match at home. It would be a pity to spoil sneh a unique record as that of Stockport, and therefore Warrington ought to strive to inflict another home defeat upon them. Stockport have played four and lost four at home, and to-day's match it is to be hopod will be the fifth. There are only four clubs who have lost every match away and these are Huddersfield, Warrington, Rochdale Hornets, and Wakefield Trinity, while Broughton Rangers have not done much better for they have lost all but one which they drew.</p>
        <p>To try and reduce the number of clubs who h ave failed to win away, the following team has been chosen to represent Warrington : Heesom, full back ; Barber, Cross, Burton, and Broady, threequarters ; Nevins and Houghton, half backs ; Burrows, Saunders, Hill Barrett, Wilcock, Fairhurst, Thornton, and Andrew a forwards.</p>
        <p>The" A "team failed to keep up their reputatien on Saturday, and it is a big drop from a win of 40 points to nil, to a defeat of 11 to nil, yet this was the difference between the results of last Saturday but oiae aad last Saturday. By the way, it will be interesting to note that St. Helens who were beaten by 5 goals 5 tries to nil, by Warriagton A on Saturday week, were beaten on Saturday by Hull A by 8 goals 1 try to nil, and up to the present time they have scored three points and have had 143 scored against them a record surely.</p>
        <p>At the Lancashire v. Cheshire match at Stockport oh Monday, Barber and Nevins did credit to Warrington. Nevins worked hard in the scrimmage and was always to the fore. Barber came off with flying colours, and was a marked contrast to Doherty of St. Helens at the opposite wing. His try fairly electrified the spectators and just showed what he could do had he had half the chances that Doherty had. The try was of the kind which is very popular to the spectators for it was not just a pop over the line, but consisted of catching a ball while at top speed and doubling two opponents during a long run.</p>
        <p>The county match was spoiled by a downpour of rain which lasted durio g the whole of the game. The Cheshire forwards were heavier than Lancashire, but the latter were much smarter in the loose. It was,laowever, at three-quarters that Lancashire excelled, and !three better men than Barber, Sam Lees, and Chapman could not be wished for. Doherty was, as we have said, a failure, but another St. Helens player, Briars, who was in the forwards, on the other hand played an excellent game,as also did Unsworth of Wigan.l</p>
      </div>
