      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>REPORTERS IN JAPAN.</head>
        <p>REPORTERS IN JAPAN.</p>
        <p>The Japanese gentry—the two-sworded Samurai, who I in the old days when they were retainers of the great Daimio gave their attention entirely to fighting and literature—now find a channel for both instincts in join- ing the staffs of the great newspapers. From the year 1875 the Government found it necessary to put some restraint upon the press, and this is not astonishing when we know that one of the new editors thought it no indiscretion to publish the text of a memorial which he had in some way obtained access to, demanding the dis- grace and decapitation of the Prime Minist( r. Still the press has grown, and has found employment for " samu- rai" of all ranks. The compositors were all originally of two-sworded rank, and from the humblrat to the highest the staff was composed of Japanese gentlemen. The manager of the " Nisshin," for example, had been treasurer of a southern clan, and necessarily a person of responsibility in his former " daimio's" household. The chief reporter received a high salary to employ an effici- ent staff, of whom many had been, under the old regime, his armed retainers. Just as these men were bold in war, they became bold with printer's ink, and continu- ally ran the risk of imprisonment for their unguarded utterances. But as soon as one editor was incarcerated, another took his place, and pursued the same tactics. There was a general belief that the real editor-in-chief kept a staff of subordinate editors who contracted to ake their turn in gaol, whilst he, in reality, adminis- ered the affairs of the journal from the shelter of the creen thus afforded him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>MORE NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>The following nominations have been received at the Town Clerk's office since our last return, which was published on Saturday morning : FAIRFIELD WARD.—Mr. R. T. Fairclough (C), corn miller, Mersey House, proposed by Mr. Charles Robert Burton, seconded by Mr. James G. Craik, and assented to by Messrs. William Holbrook, Arthur Moores, Frank Berry, James Sanders, Alfred Mortimer, David Smith, James Henry Japheth Harnpson and Henry Essenhigh. Mr. Duncan (L) is proposed by Mr. Wilkinson Haigh, seconded by Mr. James Ford, and assented to by Messrs. Walter Joynson, George Dickenson, James ARM- son Heckles, Harry Hey, George Stephenson, Thomas Crossley, George Rogers,and Thomas Harding.--Further proposed by Mr. William Jeffery, seconded by Mr. John James Nield, and assented to by Messrs. Benjamin Warburton, George Holbrook, Richard Trigger, William Stratford. Benjamin Farrar, James Massey, George Cay- bury, and Joseph Buckley. ST. JOHN'S WARD. —Mr. William Furness (L) is pro- posed by Mr. William Cannel! Baines, seconded by Mr. James Crank, and supported by Messrs. Peter Wood Naylor, William Price, Joshua William Blair, James Birch, James Naylor, Alfred Bradbury, David Gleave, and Henry Furness.—Messrs. Thomas Mather and James Knowles are respectively the proposer and seconder of Mr. William Furness in another nomination paper. The assentors are Messrs. Henry Siddall, James Johnsonjohn Whitfield, John Scott, Thomas Shaw, John Green, William Crawley, and George Thomas Brown. WHITECRO a WARD.—Mr. Monks is proposed by Mr. James Evans and seconded by Mr. Isaac Hayward,those assenting being Messrs. John Massey, Charles Allen,John Cartwright, William Charnock, Walter Jennings, John Thomas Bradbury, Noah Francis Seymour, and Samuel /duce r.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>FORWATION OF &amp; CHOII&amp;L SOCIETT.—On Saturday evening there was a good attendance at the Church In- stitute, when a meeting was held for the purpose of forming a choral society in the village. Mr. J. M. Muir occupied the chair and explained the objects which such a society would have in view, and the opportunity it would present to those in their midst to cultivate the Ivocal talent they undoubtedly possessed. He further remarked that the possession of a room like the one in which they were then assembled removed the only great lobstanle which had always stood in the way of such an institution, and that the committee of the Institute bad with their usual kindness proffered the use of the room at a nominal rental. The business of the meeting then proceeded, and it WS9 decided with unanimity that a society should be formed, having for its title " The Stockton Heath Choral Society." Mr. Muir, whose musi- cal abilities are too well known to need any explanation, was appointed conductor ; an hon. secretary and trea- surer being found in Mr. F.Welsby. More than 30 names, it is understood, have been received as members, and it is confidently anticipated that this number will in the course of a few weeks be increased to 50. Any of the gentlemen named will be pleased to receive the names of those who desire to become members.</p>
        <p>Fourteen Chinese prisoners, including the assailant of Miss Heatford, were executed at Kucheng for tho mass- acre of missionaries at Fl wasang. _</p>
