      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>HAITON.</head>
        <p>HAITON.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE. —The first entertainment of the session took place on Thursday week. The Rev. C. de P. Gliddon occupied the chair. There was a very large audience ; and an extremely interesting pro- gramme of solos, duets, recitations, Szc., was gone through. Miss Boys acted as accompanist. I good col- lection was taken. A number of new members joined at the close of the meeting. Paaiss Cuuncn.—The Rev. J. P. Cushing, of the Church Parochial Mission Society, who, 4 years ago, conducted a ten days' mission in the parish, has re- visited it this week to hold a five days' mission. The services were very well attended. especially the one for men only, on Sunday afternoon. A special children's service was held on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. Cush- ing also visited the tanneries of Messrs. Reynolds and Sons, Ockleston and Sons, and the Elighfield Tanning Company, and addressed the men during the dinner boor. • WESLEYAN/ CHUROL—In accordance with the custom observed throughout the Wesleyan Connexion, last Sun- day was devoted to special effort on behalf of the chil- dren. In the morning the Rev. W. George preached on the subject of " The whole armour of God." In the afternoon, instead of the usual course of lessions in the Sunday school, the children were taken into the chapel, and addresses were delivered by Messrs. C. Smith, J. Saunders, S. Tomlinson, jun., W. Rowe, and J. Looker. A number of parents were also present. In the evening, Mr. T. Simister preached, and took for his subject "Ruth's Choice." Tan WESLEY GVILD.—At a meeting held on Friday, and presided over by the Rev. C. de P. Gliddon, a braneh of the" Wesley Guild" was formed. Nineteen persons joined as members, and sixteen as companions. Officers were elected, and arrangements made for the Guild" to begin working at an early date. • ___....--.0.-•------</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>tIL ANNUAL MEETING OF -WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>tIL ANNUAL MEETING OF -WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>connection</p>
        <p>On Monday, the annual tea meeting, in _ , with the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society, was held in Wycliffe Hall, Warrington. To the tea there sat down some 400 persons, wlao;were attended to by the following ladies Mrs. F. Womersley, Miss Beswick, Mrs. John Atkinson, Mrs. R. Atkinson, Miss Gray, Mrs. B. Farrar, Mrs. A. Birch, Mrs. G. Brown, Miss Paul, Mrs. T. Wallington, - Miss Pickavance, Miss E. Pickavance, Miss Knowles,Mrs. Lang, Mrs. J. Birch, Miss V. Taylor, Miss Blease, Miss Russell, Mrs. Hickman, Mrs. Marshall, Miss Scholtz,Miss Lingard,Miss Mills,and Miss F. (lough, General satisfac- tion was expressed by everyone at the excellence of the tea, on the completion of which the tables were laid aside and the room cleared for the after proceedings. The meeting and concert were commenced at 8 o'clock, the chair being occupied by Mr. John Fairclough, J.P., who was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. John Yonge (president of the P.S.A.) There was a crowded attendance including Mrs. Fairclough. The CuemmeN said, when his friend Mr. Yonge asked him to come that night he felt that he could please him with very little trouble to himself, for he thought the chairman's duties were generally light. He was sure that that night woeld not be any exception to the rule at P.S.A. meetings as there was an excellent programme to be gone through. He felt he must say a few words about the P.S.A. meetings. Be did not often come to them but he heard that they were very warmly appreciated and increasingly popular. He trusted that those present would continue to support them and do their best to induce others to do the same. If every member would only persuade one other to join, the at- tendance would, of course, increase and he thought they would soon want a larger place for their meetings. ~..... (Hear, hear.) Mr. Yonge helped forward many good ".- Weirs but he must have assistance, and it was eucourag- ing to him to see so many present that night. He (the Chairman) hoped they would go on strengthening Mr. Yonge's efforts, and helping him not only in this but in the other good works he was interested and engaged in. (Applause.) He did not know that he had anything more to say, except to point out that they had a very good watchward in" Brief, Bright, and Brotherly "and he intended to be brief that night. The following prortramme was gone through at inter- vals during the evening. Songs : Miss Booth, "The Children's Home" and "Twilight's Echo "•, Mr. A. Griffiths, " Our Jack " (encored) ; Mr. W. H. Walling- ton, "Come lasses and lads " ; duet, entitled "Flow gently Deva," Messrs. Griffiths and Wallington ; trio, Messrs. Hibbert, Wallington, and Griffiths, " A little farm well tilled," (encored). Miss Bessie Dutton re- cited, "The Italian girl's story " and "Preparing to re- eeive company " (Barrie), with considerable power, the broken English in the former and Gaelic in the latter being admirably interpreted. Mr. li. M. Ashtcn,by re- qnest, delivered a most arnusing skit on "Football." " taking off" the game with mirth provoking results amongst the -1-,ale section of the audience. Addresses of a humorou, natur 3 were also delivered by Mesers.James ' Evans and W. II ckman. Preceding the meeting, Mr. W. H. Payton (who ac- companied the singers later on) gave an excellent organ recitalin the church, thereby providing attraction while , the tea-room was being re-arranged. Mr. George Baows moved" Tnat the best thanks of this meeting be given to the chairman, the speakers,the ladies who had presided at the tables, the young men who ' waited' there, and all and everyone who had contributed to this evening's enjoyment.' With regard to the chairman, Mr. Brown was pleased to find that they had with them a gentleman of Mr. Fairclough's position, and he was only sorry that more of the public men in Warrington did not throw in their lot with them and help a movement which was one of the grandest of the 19th century. Secondly, he thought that all who hail the interests of the town at heart, should throw in their lot with them and the good work. Mr. Brown then referred to the services of those rnenticned in the motion in complimentary terms. Mr. BAYLEY said he had great pleasure in seconding the proposition Mr. Brown had so ably put to the meet- ing. He thought that their entertainment that night was a replica of their meetings on Sunday afternoons. They had in their chairman a gentleman who had cer- tainly shown them that he was sufficiently broad-minded to comprehend movements for the public weal, and they trusted that more public men in the town would take a similar stand. (Hear, hear.) Miss Bessie Dutton's ser- Vices he said,were always at the command of those who Were endeavouring, in any way, to uphold the moral tone of the public. The speaker trusted that this would not be the last time they would see her in their midst, helping them. Melees,. JOHN YONGE said he was personally grateful to Mr. Fairclough for coming among them that night. They knew it was not his first appearance,as he had twice before taken the chair at the usual Sunday afternoon meetings, and they hoped to see him again on some occasion. (Hear, hear.) He wished It had been possible to induce more public men to follow Mr. Fair- dough's example, but somehow or other many of them seemed filled with shyness,and when he approached them they always pleaded their entire inability to.face so large an audience and so trying an ordeal. In their friend, Miss Dutton. they had a very valuable assistant. (Loud applause.) They knew how thoroughly she was heart and soul with them in all works of this kind ; and the contributione which she made to their Sunday afternoon work, were exceedingly acceptable, and they thanked her most heartily for what she did. (Hear, hear.) He also took this opportunity of emphasisine every word of recognition of the labours of all their friends. There wereseveral of them upon whom the joint burden of the P.S.A. business rested, and who had no easy time of it. If tbey only knew the ameunt of detailed work which some of the secretaries had to do, and the constant attendance of the markers, stewards, Choir leadere, singers, and organist, they would be even more ready to render necessary assistance. The work was very considerable upon a considerable number of people and he would like them to appreciate what the workers did and be thankful to them for it all. Mr. Yonge concluded by expressing the wilt: that when another year passed away they might have a similar gathering end a very happy time again. He then put the resolUtion to the meeting and it was carried unanimously. The CHAIRMAN responded to the va rime; eepreesions Of good-will which had been extended to him, and added that he did not know that he should Object te come there some other year. (Hear, hear and applause.) The meeting then conclud with the alleging of the Doxology. _____.------- -----</p>
        <p>- To IldoTnEns.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to *site ; it produces natural quiet sleep. by relieving the child from pain, end the ttle cherub awakes "as bright as a button " Of all heists, Is. I o. per hottle.-4det.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>HUSSAR PRIZE MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual prize meeting of the " C " (Newton) troop of Lancashire Hussars took place at the High- field Moss Range on Saturday. The weather was all that could be de,ired. and the light was suitable for correct shoot nz, rorty-seven memoers com- peted for the prizes which ranged in vaine from 453. to 3s 61., each competitor gaining a pr i ze. Sergeant-Major Strange headed the list with a score of 39 out of a possible 40,the conditions bein, 10 shots and a sighter at 200 yards. The followin..; ar3 the principal winners :—Sergeant-Major Strange, Private Abbott, Corporal Squire, Private T. Bond, Corporal Livesley, Corporal Ogden, Set geant Abbott, Private H. Wheeler, Private StAnley, Corporal W. Bond, Sergeant Turner, Pri- vates Bridge, Ilibbert, Owbridge, and Mather. GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held at the Co-opera- tive Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Bro. J Hatton, C.T., presided. and there was a large atten- dance of members. Five candidates were initiated and several others were proposed. It was resolved to accord a hearty vote of thanks to all those</p>
        <p>friends who gave their services at the entertain- ment, &amp;c., in connection with the anniversary. Bro. W. Wilson was responsible for the programme of the evening, but owing to indisposition he was unable to be present, and Bro. W. Reynolds took his place. After a few opening remarks a programme of songs, recitations, &amp;c., was given by various members of the lodge, and a vote of thanks to Bro. Reynolds closed the proceedings. ALLEGED THEFT OF FOWLS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning at the Earlestown Police Station before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Samuel Moines and Henry Moines, brothers, living in Derby-street, Earlestown, were charged with stealing two fowls, value 45., one the property of Mr. Geoffrey Bennett, Crow-lane, and the other belonging to Mr. G. Nay- lor, Newton Common. On Sunday morning the defendants were seen going along Crow-lane with something wrapped in handkerchiefs. no. Tivney was informed of the fact and followed them. On seeing the officer the bundle was dropped and pri- soners ran away. Subsequently they were appre- hended and charged as above. They were remanded to the Newton Petty Sessions on Saturday. RIINAWAy HORSE AT EARLESTOWN. On Saturday night as Mr. W. Pritchard, of War- grave, Earlestown, was with horse and float deliver- ing milk in Oxford-street, the strap which goes over the saddle of the horse broke and allowed the float to fall, breaking the shafts. The horse becom- ing startled set off at full speed down Grafton-st., and turning down the back of Bridge-st. emerged into Market-st. by Mr. DuncalPs shop. Turning up Market-st. it continued on its career, creating con- sternation amongst the large number of people who were about, until near the . New Market Inn, when it was stopped by two men, having fortu- nately done no further damage than that named. Mr. Pritchard who was in the float was thrown out soon after the horse bolted, but he escaped with- out injury. SONS OF TEMPERANCE,. _ .</p>
        <p>The usual meeting of the Samuel Morley Section of Cadets of Temperance was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Wednesday evening at seven o'clock, Bro. Arthur Smith, W.A., in the chair. Bro. E. An- derton, of St. Helens, paid a visit to the section and addressed the members. The Adult Division met in the same room at eight o'clock. Bro. C. Broster Vir.P., presided. One candidate's name was proposed for membership. The representatives to G.D. at Widnes on Saturday, October 19, Rave a report of the meeting. The financial Scribe gave his quarterly report and the auditors submitted their quarterly reports which were very satisfactory. Having seen all books and documents the various reports were accepted and a pleasant meeting closed at 9.30. LONDON MISSIONARY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The annual special services in connection with Vie above were held in the Congregational Church, Newton-le-Willows, on Sunday. The morning service was conducted by the Rev. J. ll.Riddette,of flaydock,and in the evening by a delegate from the Parent Society. In the afternoon the Rev. James Allatt delivered an address in the schoolroom, and collections were made in aid of the society. EARLESTOWN WESLEY GUILD.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of this Guild was held on Wednesday evening in the Lecture Hall of the Wesleyan chapel. The Rev. J. Bartley presided and there was a good number present. The programme of the evening was "Speaking Bee,", the object being impromptu speeches of five minutes, the sub- jects being unknown to speakers until called in from another room. The subjects chosen were "The uses of great men," and "Which has the most hope- ful future, Earlestown or Newton-le-Willows," and to give reasons for their opinion, prizes being awarded for the best speeches. The following mem- bers took part, the first two receiving the prizes, Messrs. T. Wheatley, and J. H. Profit, Marsh, Speak- man, Barrett, Parr, Misses Newman, Alderson, Mar- row, Ashcroft, and Stringer. MEDICAL AND SURGICAL AID SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the committee was held at the Depot, Queen-street, Earlestown, on Friday after- noon. There were present the Rev. T. R. Matthews, in the chair, Revs. .1. Al'att. E. W. W. Kaye. Dr. Mounsey, Mr. E. T. Evans and Mr. A. Peake (seers- tary). After reading and confirming the minutes of the last meeting, the report was read, showing that the nurse had made, during the year. 2,086 visits to 108 cases, receiving in small donations £1 12s. 6d. ; also that the Society had lent out the appliances on 402 occasions, receiving for hire £2los. After dis- cussion a sub-committee consisting of the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, Dr. Mounsey and the Secretary was appointed to draw up the report for publication, and as the funds of the Society were low,to prepare for collections. The Secretary reperted that he had been waited on by Mr. Houldsworth and Mr. Harrison, representing the Earlestown Orchestral Society, who informed hill] that the Society would hold a concert in November in the Town Hall, and that the members would hand over the proceeds to help the work. The offer was gratefully accepted by the czymmittee. A subscription of £33s. and a donation of the same amount making .£6 6s. was received from Mr. T. Molyneux. After passing the usual accounts for salary, &amp;c., the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>GOOD TEMMAR ANNIVERSARY. – – – – •</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMMAR ANNIVERSARY. - - - - •</p>
        <p>The twenty-third anniversary of the institution of the Hope of Earlestcwn Lodge of Good Templars was celebrated on Saturday evening in the Co- operative Hall by a tea and entertainment. About 140 persons partook of a well served tea, and at the meeting which followed the hall was crowded in every part. Bro. G. E. Warburton presided, and he was supported by Bro. the Rev. J. A. Lee, P.C.T. of Liverpool. and Bro. Q-M. Sergeant Roberts of War- rington, &amp;c. After opening with hymn and prayer, 1 Bro. Barker submitted the annual report of the lodge,which showed that at the preceding anniver- sary the Secretary reported 135 members in good standing. Since then the lodge had steadily pro- gressed, the membership at the present time num- bering 150. A large number of open sessions had been held to which the public were admitted, also a series of public entertainments had been given during the winter months and had been very successful, and it was the intention of the lodge to continue them during the ensuing winter. One proof of the success of the lodge lay in the fact that the lodge held the challenge block and gavel which they had won by having the largest increase ot membership during the year. The financial posi- tion of the lodge was also satisfactory. Referring to the juvenile temple, attached to the lodge, he said it was in good working order, and had a mem- bership of over 70. In conclusion he gave a hearty invitation to all to become associated with them, and help on the work, the mission of the lodge being to rescue the fallen and save others from falling, which they would agree in saying was a very ex- cellent work to carry on.—The Chairman afterwards Rave a short address, and the Rev. J. A. Lae de- livered a stirring and interesting discourse on temperance. During the evening the following programme of songs, &amp;c., was given in an efficient manner :—Piano solo, Mr. W. Willis; song, "The coming of the king," Mr. J. Nichols; reeitation, "The burial of Moses," Mr. J Hopkins; song, "The Holy City," Miss J. Roberts; song, "The dandy coloured coon." Mr. J. Iluddleston; selections on fairy bells," Mr. J. Isherwood; song, "My sweet- hpart when a boy," Mr. J. Nichols; recitation, "Twice at the altar," Miss R. Cordingley ; song, "Allan Water," Miss J. Roberts; song, "It's only silly Tommy," Mr. J. Huddleston; selections on musical glasses, Mr. J. Isherwood.—On Sunday the Rev. J. A. Lee preached special temperance sermons at the Wesleyan chapel morning and evening. In the afternoon he conducted a children's service,this being the day appointed by the Conference for special efforts on behalf of the young people.</p>
        <p>An owl cannot move his eyes, as they are fixed in their sockets. The deficiency is atoned for by great freedom of motion in the muscles of the head and neck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>- , - - .</p>
        <p>faithfully yours,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, OCTOBER 26, 1895.</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN,</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR).</p>
        <p>The ratepayers of Earlestown and Newton are not very enthusiastic over township matters it one may judge from the size of the meeting held in the Newton Town Hall on Monday evening. Perhaps the very inclement weather may have had some- thing to do with it, tor it was really a dreadful night, and it was too much to expect anybody to trudge from Earlestown in such weather. It appears the District Council want their pound of flesh, or in other words, they want all the power vested in them. Hence,having secured the appoint- ment of overseers, they wish to have the appoint- ment of the Assistant Overseer vested in the^a as well. Of course, I don't care who has the appoint- ment, but Mr. Sankey seems loth to pat the thing absolutely and entirely in the hands of the Council, and so the meeting was called to decide which way it should be.</p>
        <p>--- Excluding reporters, there were about half-a- dozen people present,and after if had been explained why they had been brought together they set about discussing the pros and cons. Mr. Sankey thought the overseers should have a voice in the selection of assistant overseer seeing they were responsible for all the finances and other matters connected with the office, and I think if the Council are wise they will fall in with his views. Nobody seemed to have any particular objection to the Council having the power to appoint and revoke, and so with Mr. Sankey's proviso, a resolution was passed agreeing to the Council having the power vested in them.</p>
        <p>Talking of the Council. I wonder what they are thinking about. On Monday toey met in committee at four o'clock, and remained on duty until 8.45,.a sitting of four hours and 45 minutes. But this is early when we consider that not many weeks ago they were in committee until after midnight. With such a painstaking, hard-working set of Councillors we ought to get something done, but I am afraid some of them are qualifying as parliamentary orators, and in the meantime the town's business is ' suffering. But who is going to pay for all the gas which is being burned ? I suppose the ratepayers will gladly see to this, especially when they remem- ber how well they are being looked after. You see we have had the magnificent sum of a penny per thousand feet taken off, so that we need not be- grudge the Council a little extra gas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>RATEPAYERS MEETING AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>RATEPAYERS MEETING AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the ratepayers of Newton-in-Makerfield, e^ ,vend by the overseers, was held in the Town Hall, 'vt ,on Monday evening. for the purpose of taking</p>
        <p>•onsideration the application of the Newton-in': Mikerfield Urban District Council for an order under section 33 ot the Local Government Act of 1894, conterr- log upon them the power to appoint and revoke the ap- pointment of assistant overseers. Mr. James Sankey Presided, and he was supported by his colleague Mr. W. Mills, there being about half-a-dozen ratepayers pre- sent.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he was sorry there was not a larger attendance, but the time bad expired and they might as well get on with their business. He read a communica- tion which had been received from the Local Government Board, as follows:—"I am directed by the Local Govern- ment Board to advert to Mr. Houghton's letter of the 15th Trine with reference to the application of the Newton-in-Makerfield Urban District Council for an order under section 33 of the Local Government Act 1894, conferring upon them the power to appoint, and revoke the appointment of assistant overseers. I am directed to inquire whether a vestry meeting was con- vened to consider the matter ; if not, lam directed to request that that course naav now be taken, and the Board in f ormed:of the views of:the vestry on the subject." The Chairman added that they had convened that meet- ing co get to know from the ratepayers whether it was their wish that the District Council should have that power or not. From the way he read the Act,he thought theyihad that power already, but the Local Government Board wanted to know from the ratepayers whether they should elect the assistant ovPrseer or not. At one of the meetings of the Council they applied for all the powers they could get hold cf, and that was the appoint- ment of overseers and assistant overseers, and the Local Government Board wanted to know whwther the rate- payers were unanimous, or whether it was their wish so tar as that meeting was cencerned,that the power should be placed in the hands of the Council. They had not a , District Councillor present,but if they had had he should have asked him why they wanted the power. So far as the appointment of the Assistant 0 eerseer was concerned he was quite in favour of the Council having the power providing the overseers bad a voice in it. Mr. Barrett asked if the appointment of assistant overseer previously rested in the hands of the overseers absolutely. The Ch airman said Mr. Houghton was elected by vote of the ratepayers, and his appointment was permanent so long as be behaved himself.</p>
        <p>Mr. -Clarke said if they gave the District Council the power to elect, he did not think they could put in a proviso that the overseers must have a word to say in regard to the election. They must either transfer their present privilege to them or keep it where it is. Mr. Mills thought,seeing the overseers were responsible to the Local Government Board for all financial matters and everything ia connection with the duties of assis- tant overseer, they ought to have some voice in the ap- pointment. Mr. Cflarke said the matter simply resolved itself into the question as to whether they as ratepayers were agreeable to the power being granted the Council. Mr. Barrett thought it would be best to take a vote on the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. Speakman considered that on the whole it would be to the best interests of the district to have the power vested in the Council, and with that object in view he proposed that this meeting is of opinion that it is con- ducive to the best interest of the township, that the power be vested in the Council,and providing the power is so vested we think it would be right and good for them to consult with the overseers as to the appoint- ment."</p>
        <p>Mr.Syme seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>KICKING A POLICEMAN.—James Phillips, an Irish labourer, was charged before Dr. Steele and Mr. Rigby, at Frodsham Police Court on Monday, with having been drunk and disorderly at He!shy on the previous Satur- day, and further with assaulting P.C. Price whilst in the execution of his dutv.—Evidence in support of the charge was given by P.C. Price who stated that he was called to the Robin Hood public-house on the night in question, in consequence of the prisoner causing a dis- turbance. He asked him to move away but he refused. He was then taking the prisoner into custody when the latter kicked him very severely about the legs and body Be had to get assistance to bring the prisoner to the police station. The magistrates fined the prisoner the costs, or in default seven day's imprisonment on the first charge, and 108. including costs or 14 day's impri- sonment on the second.</p>
        <p>CHARGE OF STEALING GROCERIES.—On Tuesday morn- ing, before Mr. Thomas and Dr. Steele, a young woman, 19 years of age, named Clara Gough, 4, Little Pountney- st., Wolverhampton, was charged with stealing a quan- tity of groceries, value 2s. 2d., the property of Mrs. Har- rison, lodging house keener and provision dealer, Ship- at., Frodsham, on the 17th inst.—Mrs. Harrison stated that the defendant came to lodge at her house about a fortnight ago, and a few days afterwards she took her into the kitchen as a general servant. On the 17th inst., from what she was told, she went into tne lodging house and found the goods produced which the prisoner ad- mitted having taken out of the shop.—Florence Alice Staples stated that the defendant brought the goods to her and asked her if she wanted something good. She gave information to Mrs. Harrison.—P.C. Price proved apprehending the prismfer. She admitted taking the articles.—The magistrates dealt with her under the First Offenders Act, and bound her over in the sum of £5 to come up for judgment when called upon. They also generously paid her fare home again. THE TECHNICAL CLASSES.—&amp; meeting was held in the Girls' Schoolroom, Frodsham, on Monday evening under the ausp;ces of the Frodsham Technical Instruction Committee. The object of the meeting was the forma- tion of classes in various subjects during the winter sea- son. Mr. Charles E.Linaker,chairman of the Committee, presided ,and there was a fair attendance. It was decided to form a freehand and model drawing class with Miss Bottomley as teacher,a shorthand class conducted by Mr. Sharp, and also a hook-keeping class, for which Mr. Davies was appointed teacher. Miss Ctouent's CONCERT.—In the Town Hall, Frod- sham,on Wednesday evening, there was a large audience on the occasion of Miss Clough's annual concert. Mr. R. Birkett Musgrove, of Runcorn, commenced the pro- ceedings with a pianoforte solo. He also acted as ac- companist. Miss Heatheringten (Runcorn) very sweetly Sang" Island of Dreams," and in response to the loud applause of the audience she bowed her acknowledg- ments. In her next contribut!on she captivated the audience with her singing of" I couldn't could I," and gave as an encore 7" Come lasses and lads." Mr. Joseph Nickson (Runcorn) sang twice, his songs being" Long- shoreman "and " The mighty deep" which he gave in a creditable manner. Mr. J. H. Thompson, and Mr. John Phillips, both of Chester also sang, the latter's humorous songs being greatly appreciated.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mary Coffey, aced one hundred and four years, landed in New York City the other day. She is a native ol County Berry, Ireland, and says she went to Amerl3a to find a husband.</p>
        <p>The Czar is reported to have expressed a stronz desire for a conference with the German and Austrian Em- perors after his coronation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BRICK-STREET MISSION BAZAAR.</head>
        <p>BRICK-STREET MISSION BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>OPENING BY MR. PERCIVAL B. SCOTT.</p>
        <p>A three days' bazaar, the proceeds of which are to be devoted to the building fund of Brick-st. Independent Methodist Mission, Warrington, was opened in the Wes- leyan schoolroom, Bold-st., on Wednesday afternoon by Mr. Percival B. Scott. A brief history of the mission will be interesting and will explain the objects for which the bazaar was held. Six years ago the members of Friars' Green Church decided to form a Sunday school at Brick-st. Five members were deputed to this mission effort, which, from its commencement to the present, has been very successful, proving a great boon and help in spiritual matters to those in the locality. Upon the question whether the work should remain as a mission, or grow to a church permanently established and self- governing according to Independent Methodists' polity and principles, the mission workers reluctantly seceded from their old sphere of labour two and a half years ago. A room was opened in Battersby Lane, and Mr. R. Berry, of Swinton, preached two sermons, the collectims realizing £ll 13s. This room was from the first too small for the requirements of the mission, and as soon as possible the workers took possession of the present room in Crossley-st., which is mueh larger. But even now the work is hindered by insufficient room and facilities. A valuable site has been secured in Brick St., four cot- tages having been purchased by the Trustees of the S-yciety, with 550 square yards of 'land attached. The property cost £350, and the workers have already paid £l3O towards this sum. The present bazaar effort is put forth with the hope of liquidating the debt entirely, and the executive trust that this consummation may be attained. The schoolroom on Wednesday was very tastefully set out, and the stalls contained a large nuin- tier of useful and ornamental articles, the former pre- ponderating. There were two fancy stalls, which were laden with a large assortment of drapery, &amp;c. The first was in charge of Mesdames Massey, Gough, Wright, Shaw, Dutton, and Bennett, and the second in charge or the Misses A. Warburton, E. Shaw, A.Holt,S. A. Roberts, C. Smith, and J.Molyneaux. There were also stalls with hardware and glassware, of which Messrs. S. Gough, B. Warburton, W. Hewitt Dutton, J. Shaw, and W. Barton had charge. The advertisement stall was under the care of Messrs. E. Jolley, J. Thorniley, T. Worsley, and F. H. Jolley. Mr. Roberts, Mr. James Harding,and Miss Harding were respsusible for the Sunday school stall, which bore a large number of toys, Sze. Miss A. Dale, Miss C. Dale, Miss M. A. Bennett, Miss A. Hill, Miss H. Shaw, and Miss C. Hardman had charge of the flower stall, and the refreshment stall was in the hands of Mrs. Dutton' Mrs. Mounfield,'Mrs. Wright,Mrs.Hill, and Mrs. Gough. The stalls were laid out to the best ad- vantage, and the room was plentifully bedecked with flags, and a number of plants, which had been placed in front of the rostrum and in the centre of the room also helped to improve the appearance of the place. At one end of the room was a "fishpond "the keeper of which was Mr. T. Massey, and one advantage of this pond over bthers was that upon paying there was sure to be a " bite." Pro- fessor A. Grounds also gave at intervals during the evening some of his wonderful ventriloquial entertain- ments, and Professor James Betts, of Manchester,under- took to feel the bumps of all and sundry for the modest sum of 2d., a, d private examinations for 6d. Professor T.Acton also gay* number of sleight of hand entertain- ments in which he surprised the spectators with " magical illusions and modern mystery." Persons going in this room were requested not to forget to bring their purses. Miss Barton held a fine art exhibition,and a miniature museum of butterflies, moths, etc., was also kindly lent and exhibited by Mr. William Mounfield. During the evening selections of music, songs, etc. ,were given at intervals, the accompanist being Mr. F. Hick- man, and selections were also given by the school band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WEDNESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance on Wednesday at the opening ceremony, and the following gentlemen accom- panied Mr. Scott to the platform :—Councillor Roberts, and Messrs. J. Mounfield, Mayball (president of the church), S. Gough, and Shaw (secretary). The pro- ceedings were commenced with singing and prayer,after which</p>
        <p>Mr. Manatr,,previous to calliag upon Mr. Scott to de- clare the bazaar open, said he would like to say just a word or two. It was something like seven years since the Mission was commenced, and about two years and five months since they were a separate church. Since they had commenced business oa their own account, they, like a good many people, found that they required furniture and other matters. Towards this, they bad raised £204 7s. 91 The debt they had incurred in get- ting the property they now had was £350. They had in addition to the sum above mentioned,distributed £2O among their poorer brethren. (Applause.) They were, therefore, still in debt to the extent of about £215. Ho hoped that they would be successful in clearing off that debt, and in leaving a little balance which would be de- voted towards the erection of another building. Ano- ther building was urgently required for the room in which they laboured at present was much too small for their purpose. They had a membership of 115, and they had 380 scholars. (Applause.) The building was also in a very undesirable place, and to show this, Mr. May- hall mentioned that a preacher had come to Warring- ton to preach at their Mission and had to go back again because he could not find it. (Laughter.) He, there- fore, hoped that during that and the following days,they would raise sufficient to have a surplus towards 4ettio; a fund towards the removal of the mission to a mor. commodious *room. (Applause.) Mr. P. B. Scorr, who was received with great warmth, said that he had not come there to make a speech, but he had come to thank them for so kindly asking him to come and open the bazaar that day. He believed that one could do more work by coming down from the platform and going into the centre of the room, and by purchasing or by a donation to do something to assist in that good work. (Applause.) Be was glad to see that the articles in that room were all very useful articles, and he trusted that on the last day there would not be an article left—(applause)—and that they would not only wipe off the debt, but to a very large extent be able to defray the expenses of the new building. He would be very pleased, if the debt was not wiped off,to bring some of his lady friends to get up a concert with the assistance of some in Warrington, to raise a sub- stantial amount in further aiding the building fund. (Applause.) He was glad to hear of the good work they had been carrying on during the past six years, and he was sure that they very mush wanted a chapel in that ward. He was sure that there was a large number of worshippers who would join with them, and that the work would be greatly extended if they had a good nlace. He might be speaking to other than Methodists. They were met there on neither political grounds nor en any particularly religious grounds—(hear, hear)—and that bazaar was open to all sects and all creeds. Those friends who might not be of the same opinion, either religiously or politically, could however join in that good work. He was certain that he was only saying what was right when he said that they would assist a worthy course. I He would again say that if at any time he could do anything to assist them he would be only too pleased to do so. He knew many of their faces, as a short time before, he was asking their aid on an- other occasion. He was then unsuccessful, but he hoped that in asking their aid that day, he would be more sue. cessful. (Applause.) He hoped that they would not only bring their purses full and take care to empty them in the bazaar, but refill them and come again. (Laughter and applause.) He was sure that they would get a sub- stantial return for their money, he believed equal to any value they would get if they visited any place in the town. He bad great pleasure in declaring the bazaar open. (Applause.) Mr. GOUGH proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Scott for showing his sympathy, and helping them. He not only helped them rraterially, but by his presence that day, he had caused m fly others to come to the bazaar. The Mission deserved sympathy ss th.y required a much better place than they had at present. 'lna atmosphere in the school room was almost unbearable at times, and a chapel was required as being necessary for the conven- iencs, prosperity, and benefit of the neighbourhood. Be was sure they all felt indebted to Mr. Scott for so kindly coming to help them, and also for promising to assist with his lady friends. Mr. Salm seconded the vote of thanks, which was ac- corded unanimously. Mr. Scorr in reply said that he did not think that any man should take credit for doing a public duty. Anyone who had time and means at his disposal should assist in good work. (Applause.) The bazaar was opened on Thursday by Mrs.Brimelow, wife of the president ef the connexion. There was a fair attendance and after being introduced by Mrs. Shaw, Mrs. Brimelow, in the course of a long address, rrferred to her connection of some 63 years with the Friars' Green Chapel of which they were an offshoot, relating several anecdotes and reminiscences of the past which were listened to with pleasure by the older members of the audience. She thought they needed a good handsome church and Sunday school and hoped they would get</p>
        <p>it. Mrs-Mot/Isms= moved a vote of thanks to Mrs.Brime- low and said they owed her a deep debt of gratitude for coming, considering the state of her health and the weather. She was also pleased to inform them that Mrs. Brimelow had just placed in her hand a donation of £5 to the bazaar. (Loud applause.) Mr. Dtrrrou seconded and the vote was carried un- animously. Mrs. BRIMELOW responded and also moved a vote of thanks to Mrs. Shaw and Mrs. Mounfield. This motion was also passed and the business of buying and selling commenced at once.</p>
        <p>The President of the Board of Agriculture (Ur. W Long),speaking at a conference in Edinburgh on Wednes- day, said the Government proposed to simplify the con- ditions under which agriculturists were called upon to prosecute their calling, and, if possible, to enable them to make thir industry a little more profitable. The British Cycle Manufacturing Company, Liverpool and London, have secured a large space at the forth- coming Stanley Cycle Show, Agricultural Hall,London, where they will exhibit a special display of twenty safeties all of their 1896 design. We understand that they have some important novelties in store, and their exhibit will doubtless attract considerable attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY. PAPER ON BIMETALLISM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY. PAPER ON BIMETALLISM.</p>
        <p>The session 1895-6, in connection with the above Society,was commenced on Monday evening, in the Mu- stum Lecture Room, the President, Mr. William Owen, F.R.1.8.A., in the chair.—Mr. Owen in his opening speech thanked the Society for their appreciation of him in electing hint president. It was with some amount of diffidence that he accepted the office, remembering the literary capabilities of his predecessors, although he felt some confidence in the fact that the members of the society always rallied round the president in connection with a society which had become one of the recognized institutions of the town. (Applause.) But the greatest measure of his confidence lay in the fact that they had such an excellent Secretary—(applause)—and all the president has to do was to sit in the chair during the meetings. The papers which were to be read during the session were very valuable, and the excellent programme which had been prepared was owing to the care and attention of the secretary. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The EON. SECRETARY (Mr. J. Rymer Young) drew the attention of the members to the balance sheet, which was published frr the first time on the notice convening the meeting. Th 3 society was carried on with the mest absolute economy. The number of members was about 180, and remained at about that figure, although eight or ten new names were added each year. The increase was counter-balanced by removals by death, removals out of town, and by the striking off of these members who were in arrears. Mr. Young, in conclusion,referred to the difficulty which was experienced even in getting half-a-dozen papers read during the session ; that did not say much for the intellect and enterprise of their Warrington friends.</p>
        <p>Mr. David Tinnion and the Rev. A. B. Eater were elected members of the society. Mr. Cr EORGE RODGER then read an able and exhaust- tive paper on " Bimetallism," He alluded to the:difference between monometallism (our present sys- tem) of bimetalism, to the ratio that must necessarily exist between gold and silver, after which he proceeded to describe the advantages to be derived from bimetal- lism, also taking a bird's-eye view of the state of trade in this and other gold standard countries. M. de Laveleye had shown that our present system must in- evitably increase protection, and surely enough the tariffs of all other countries had heon increased, whilst even in England a large party had grown up in favour of that system. All these facts corroborated what bi- metallists contended was one of the chief causes of the present unprecedentedly low level of prices, namely, that when the tie between silver and gold was broken ; when one nation after another adopted a gold standard, and demonetised silver ; when the credit of the whole Western world rested upon one metal, gold, instead of on two as before—then gold, having to much to do for the amount of it in circulation in the world, became ap- preciated, and the law acknowledged by all political economists, "that according to the quantity of money in circulation in the world, so will prices of commodities rule," began to act by lowering the prices of commodi- ties (indeed the appreciation of gold and the fall in the gold prices of commodities were synonymous). ' What, then, bad the bimetallists to offer as a panacea for this depressed state of things ? Simply that silver should again be re-monetised and placed in the position towards gold that it occupied before 1873, and that this should be accomplished by an international arrange- ment. Here, then,we arrive at the first advantage to be derived from bimetallism, for by the above-mentioned means we should, to a considerable extent, lower the height: to which gold has appreciated, thus removing what Mr. Arthur Balfour has called " the most deaden- ing and benumbing influence which can touch the springs of enterprise in a nation." As a consequence of this, the prices of all commodities would be raised,bring- ing into action what David Hume describes, "under the influence of rising prices, labour and industry gain a new life, the merchant becomes more enterprising, the manufacturer more diligent, and even the farmer follows his plough with more alacrity and atten- tion." It is true that Sir William Harcourt joins issue with this, deprecating the raising of the prices of com- modities as being inimical to the interests of the great mass of the people, especially the labouring classes. It might be mentioned that as a significant fact,that the United Factory Textile workers have shown their belief in bimetalism, by subscribing £5OO out of their funds to the Bimetallic Leae.ue. The second advantage to be de- rived from bimetallisrn is. that we would have a more stable and uniform standard of value, that is to say, we would secure a money of more constant purchasing power. The third advantage we claim for Bimetallism is that it would give us a stable par of exchange through out the world. That is to say, gold and silver, being tied together at a certain, ratio in the countries of Europe and the United States,remittances of silver from all other parts of the world would be made at an ex- change of the mint price (say in London), less the cost of shipping and 1 aiding charges, freight and insurance. With a fixed ratio between gold and silver the specula- tive element is eliminated from export business with our largest customers which are mainly silver standard countries. Our chief foreign trade is placed on a far sounder basis, and is bound to increase largely by rea- son of lower priees in terms of silver. As an example of the evil arising from the want of a par of exchange be- .•eeen eyold and silver standard countries, let us sup- merchant sends goods, say to Peru (a silver stan- dard country.) To enable him to recover a profit for his trouble, risk, and interest on his capital, he charges 10 per cent, over their cost, landed abroad. This would be the price he would sell at. Within two months of the purchase of the goods, silver falls 12 per cent. (as it did between January and March, 1894), this fall is cabled out, the goods are just arriving, and the merchant finds he has to add 22 per cent., instead of 10, to recoup himself. Everyone was quite aware of the effect of such a rise ; intending pur- chasers will not pay the price until they are absolutely forced to, the goods remaie unsold, no further orders are given to the mannfacturer and business languishes. The evil effects of this instability of exchange do not end here, however. The purchaser at last determines to pay the enchancad price for the goods, but, just then, a small rise in silver is wired out. Perhaps this would encourage him to complete the purchase P Not at all, it has the opposite 'effect ; he thinks silver may rise to its old value, and he again holds off, finding even- tually that the small rise was but a faint backwardation of the pendulum, and that a further fall in silver has set into a lower price than before. Now, this has been go- ing on for the past 23 years, and on an ever accentuat- ing scale. Thus our export business dwindles, and both , manufacturer and exporter find it hard to cover the mere expenses of their business. Bad as this is, being the re- sult of the instability of exchange, it is not the worst that is even now happening. These high prices, in terms of silver, encourage the silver-standard countries to raise factories to make their own goods, thereby, at last, ceas- ing altogether to import their requirements from us,:and this constitutes a fourth argument in favour of bimetal- lism, viz. : that it would do away with the handicap we are at present placing on home producers, and would stop the encouragement we are giving to our competi- tors. Such are the chief advantages that we consider bimetallism would bring in its train. Mr. Rodger, in concluding an able paper, considered some of the argu- ments against bimetallism, gave the answers to them, and resumed his seat amid applause. Captain EDELSTEN, on proposing a vete of thanks to Mr. Rodger, remarked that whatever difference of opinion might exist with regard to the question that gentleman had introduced, there was no doubt that he had given them a most skilful exposition of his own side of the case. He (Mr. Edelsten) differed from his con- clusions, as he belonged to the opposite school. He had , listened in vain to hear why what proved a failure before , —in the case of France—should not prove a failure again. (Hear, hear.) Coming to the question of what benefit would accrue from bimetallism, why did they want it P The whole argument rested on the depreciation in prices of commoditiee—the appreciation of gold which had , resulted in scarcity. But was there any scarcity? They had had no proof of it, and at the present moment the old lady in Threadneedle-street had more bullion than she knew what to do with, and was providing additional , safes in which to keep it. Even if there had been a scarcity it would put itself right, although they had still to prove that depreciation of prices had arisen from the , scarcity. The speaker raised other objections to bime- tallism, and in conclusion, observed that the marriage of the metals was something like the marriage of indiv- , iduals. It was a case of'' for better for worse,for richer , for poor r," and he thought that Britannia cogld not do better than stick to her single blessedness. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. A. H. CROSFIRLD supported, with much pleasure, the vote of thanks which had been proposed by Mr.. , Edelsten to Mr. Rodger. who, he said, had covered the ground so ably that he found himself in some difficulty , because he held views practically the same as his own. , He felt that on the spur of the moment especially it was impossible to add much, if anything, to the force with . which he had presented those views. He (Mr. Crosfield) must confess that on the question of bime- tallism he felt himself a great deal better qualified to be a listener rather than a speaker. He remembered that , some years ago he asked his uncle's opinion upon the question, and his reply was to the effect that the man . who tried to fathom the currency question was only fit for a lunatic asylum. (Laughter.) There was,however, no doubt that the question had been pressed upon them in the last decade or so in a much more forcible manner. He quite admitted that in a large proposed reform like that of bimetallism the burden of proof should rest with the reformers, and it was their duty to answer objec- tions of the kind which had been raised by Mr. Edelsten. The first quktion he raised was the alleged failure nf France to sustain the bimetal:ism quoted by the lecturer as a strong argument in proof of what might be done by more united action. He thought it was suffi- ciently obvious that if for one reason or another the leading nations of Europe re-attempted to adopt the standard, and by diminishing the demand for the white metal, appreciated it, a rich country like France might easily wish that her standard should be in a more valuable metal. At least she would be bound to take steps to protect herself. The next point was the danger of an influx of silver from the East, but as a practical danger be did not</p>
        <p>think they were much threatened in that quarter. There was a large amount of gold in India and China, but such gold as there was in India was,he believed, used in the greater part for the purposes of hoarding, and when it came to the currency of China and Japan the whole mass of the currency was silver. The people were too poor to make use of anything else ; the income of most of the people was something like 30s. a year, and as a large proportion only received about 10s. a year, there was no danger of their attempting to substitute a gold for a silver currency. They (bimetallists) were willing to accept a ratio of 20 to one, if it was desired. Some , of them might imagine that whatever the ultimate ratio might be it would not be 151 or 16 to one, but more likely 20 to ope or more. He should think the general consensus of opinion amongst bimetallists would induce them to accept willingly the ratio proposed by Mr. Bal- four, when he spoke definitely on the subject, when his hands were free—(laughter)--and that ratio was 24 to one. Mr. Crosfield alluded to other phases of the ques- tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY remarked that Mr. Goschen made a profit of over £600,000 on the last recoinage of silver. In buying silver at 23. Md. per ounce, and coining a pound troy, 120 z., into 663., as at present, the Government made a profit of the difference between the cost price 31.6 and 665., viz., 344. 6d., or more than cent, per cent. The silver kings and bimetallist wanted to get that profit for themselves. How were the bimetallists going to bridge over the gap between the real and the nominal ratio of the value of silver coins to gold in this country? The difference between the two was the difference between 31 and 151. They, of course, pro- posed to do it by the action ot'the market, but the loss tp the wage-receiving classes would be no less in the long run. The silver kings and wage-payers would gain, but the wage-receivers would have to work twice as hard for the same silver money._ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Kenyon, Mr. McGregor, and others also spoke, after which the resolution was put to the meeting and heartily agreed to. Mr. RODGER replied to the criticisms at considerable length, and the proceedings then terminated.</p>
        <p>UTTERING OF COUNTERFEIT COIN AT WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SHOPKEEPERS DEFRAUDED.</head>
        <p>SHOPKEEPERS DEFRAUDED.</p>
        <p>A WOMAN IN CUSTODY.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Alderman Sutton and Lieutenant-Colonel Ridg- way, Margaret Wilson, aged 30, was charged with willingly and knowingly uttering a false and counterfeit half-crown to Jessie Booth, on the 23rd inst. and on the same day a false 2s. piece to Clara Tilling and another to Elizabeth Lythgoe and still another to Thomas Ton_ge._ _ _</p>
        <p>M. S. Hunt, chief constable, said the prisoner gave her name as Margaret Wilson, and her age as 30. She was a single woman and came from Bacup to Warring- ton on Wednesday about 6 o'clock. At 7.15 the same evening the prisoner went to the shop of Mr. John Toft, Bridge-street, and asked Miss Jessie Booth, an assistant, to supply her with a 6d. cake, but on noticing some butter she said "I'll have some butter instead, it looks so nice." She enquired the price and was told Is. 4d. a pound and she then said she would take flb. The butter I was given her and the prisoner tendered half-a-crown and received in return from Miss Booth, is. 10d. Miss Booth almost immediately noticed that the half-crown was false and told the prisoner, who at once tendered a florin which was good, but Miss Booth did not accept it. The prisoner then, without any hesitancy left the butter on the counter and walked out of the shop with her is. 10d. change and a bag containing the goods displayed in Court. Mr. Toft was informed and went out of the shop and saw P.C. Newey whom he told of the theft and pointed out the prisoner. P.C. Newey followed the prisoner along Friars' Gate up Friars' Green and overtook her and then brought her to the shop. Here the constable asked her what sort of money she had been trying to pass there. She said" I offered a good two-shilling and did not know the half- crown was bad." When asked for her name and address she said she had none and was thereupon brought to the police station. When searched there, 3s. 6d. in silver was found upon her and also the bag containing confec- tionery. From inquiries which had been made it tran- spired that prisoner had been to Clara Tilling, 113, Sankey-st., on whom she passed a base florin and re- ceived in change is. 9d. She went also to Mr. Lyth- goe's shop in Sankey-st. and for another base florin re- ceived in change is. 9d. Another florin she passed at the shop of Mr. Thomas Tonge, Bridge-street, and ob- tained is. ad. change. Mr. Hunt then handed up one of the coins to the bench and pointed out that almost anyone might tell they were bad. Be further informeri the magistrates that about 12 shops had been entered by the prisoner but he did not yet know whose. He ap- plied for a week's remand to communicate with the Mint authorities.</p>
        <p>Jessie Booth, assistant at Mr. John Toft's shop in Bridge-st., said the prisoner came into the shop at 7.15 on Wednesday night and asked for a cake, putting 2s. 6cl. on the counter. Witness gave prisoner change and put the half-crown in the detector and found that it easily bent. She then ran to the side door and saw the prisoner looking up Bridge-st. as if she was expecting some one. Witness asked her to come back to the shop, , which she did all right, and Mr. Taft asked her if she had any more money like the half-crown. She said she had not and explained the possession of the coin in 1 question by saying that she had just changed a sover- eign. She offered a 2s. piece and asked Mr. Toft to take pay out of that, but he would not and she then threw the butter across the counter and ran out. Mr. Toft then followed and gave her into custody. Clara Tilling said the woman came into their shop about a quarter to seven and asked for two penny cakes putting a Es. piece on the counter. Witness gave her. is. 10d. change and prisoner then went out. About an hour afterwards a policeman came to the shop and asked to see the money which the prisoner brought, and she handed him the florin,which he bent. P.C. Newey proved arresting the prisoner,and repeated the excuse which the prisoner made to him about not knowing the half-crown was bad and offering the 2s. When at the police station he-examined the bag the pri- soner carried and found packets of confectionery, the bags having the names of the sellers on the back. He recovered the three bad coins from different people. . . _ . _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hunt mentioned that before being charged, the prisoner was placed amongst other women and all the witnesses at once pointed her out. The magistrates remanded the case for a week in accordance with Mr. Hunt's request.</p>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE BOWLING ASSOCIA-</p>
        <p>TION.-PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>The eighth annual dinner and distribution of prizes won during the season took place at the Mid- land Hotel, Albert Park, Manchester, on Saturday. The banquet was largely attended by members of the 32 affiliated districts. Mr. J. J. Meakin, presi- dent, was in the chair, supported by the vice- president, Mr. J. W. G. Coombes, Messrs. A. H. Crosfield (Warrington), J. J. Corney (Pendleton), G. Lumb (Chorlton), and other district presidents. After the loyal toasts had been honoured, the chief business of the occasion commenced. Prizes of the total value of £l5O were taken as follows:—Premier competition for the best team in the county, "The Lancashire Championship," won by Withington (Midland), the honour carry- ing with it the president's prize, average perform- ances 192: runners-up in same competition, Wigau4 vice-president's prize, average performances 18.1. In the senior association competition for all , districts, teams distanced in the premier competi- tion, chief prize secared by South Warrington,aver- age 1812; second prize won by North Warrington, average 188. Junior Association competition by teams distanced in the preliminary round, prize won by Stretford (Angel), bowling average for the season 1710. Special prize, offered by the vice-pre- sident for the highest aggregate majority in Lancashire v. Yorkshire,held at Mirfield and Higher Broughton, won by J. Brimelow, North Warrington. Previous winners: J. Harrison (Hindsford) and F. Berry (Warrington Soutb), gold medalists. Half- team tournament: First prize, Timperley (Pelican); second prize, Pendleton (Woolpack). Sixth indiv- idual merit competition prizes: 1, the amateur championship, J. Allison (Warrington North) ; 2, J. W. G. Coombs (Midland, Withington) ; 3, S. Ecker- sall (Cheetham) ; 4. A. J. Jones ( Warrington North); 5, R. Williamson ( Withington) ; 6, W. Wood (War- rington North) ; 7, A. Thompson (Urmston) ; 8. R. Anderton, Warrington South. The averages of performances for the Lancashire Championship in 1894 was 18.10 out of a possible 21.0. This year it is 192, an advance of four paints. The success of Warrington players in the various tour- naments has been the feature of the season just con- cluded, and their share in the valuable prizes offered formed a feature when the presentations took place. Never in the history of the Association has ene district claimed such a monopoly as Warrington, and of the fif- teen prizes offered no fewer than eight were carri-d away by Warringtonians. The prize of the Warrington South (Bull's Head) for gaining honours in the Second Division of the Association Competition took the form of a case of cutlery, whilst the runners up in the same competition, Warrington North (Cemetery Inn), were awarded four silver entros dishes. In the inter-county competition for the best aggregate in the home and home- matches Mr. J. D. Brimelow, of the Warrington North team, succeeded in distancing his opponents,and gaining the gold medal for the 1895 season. In the individual I merit competition for clubs associated with the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Associations, played in August, the Warrington players took a prominent part, and of the eight prizes four were won by Warringtonians, the first prize carrying with it the title of amateur champion,be- ing secured by Mr. J. Allison, of the Warrington North team,his prize taking the form of a gold chronometer and a silver cup. Mr.A. J. Jones was third in the same com- petition, and was awarded a case of cutlery • Mr. W. Woods, fifth, silver flask ; and Mr. R. Andertoll, eighth, also silver flask.</p>
