        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL ELECTION, NOVEMBER IST, 1895. TO THE BURGESSES OF LATCHFORD WARD LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— At the request of many of the Ratepayers, I come for- ward and ask that you will once more place me in the honourable position of your representative. I trust that my actions in the Town Council have secured your approval; I have, at any rate, endeavoured to fulfil honestly and faithfully the duties of the important position to which you elected me four years ago. Should you do me the honour of again returning me for your large and important Ward, I shall do my best to pro- mote its interests and those of the Town generally. Lam, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours faithfully, LINNIEUS GREENING. Warrington, October 21st, 1895.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL ELECTION, NOVEMBER IsT, 1895. TO THE ELECTORS OF THE TOWN HALL WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, Having been requested by an influential body of Electors and Friends in this Ward to allow myself to be nominated as a candidate for the vacancy which occurs on November let, I have consented to do so,feeling that I have your confidence. As most of you are aware I have been a resident and in business In this Ward for upwards of thirty years, and during that time I have had , some experience of your re- quirements and interests. lam in favour, as far as possible, of reducing our very burdensome rates by working on more economical principles, and curtailing the expenses of unnecessary deputations, Asc. If elected it will be my earnest endeavour to assist any scheme which will in any degree tend; to increase our Local Industries, and the trade;of the town general' lam, Ladies and,Gentlemen. Yours truly. GEORGE CLAY. Park Buildinge,lWarrington October 16th,i1895.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGIION. MUNICIPAL ELECTION, NOVEMBER IST, 1895. TO THE ELECTORS OF ORFORD WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— My term of office having expired, and having been requested by numerous ratepayers and others to offer myself for re-election, I have, after mature consideration, consented to place my services at your disposal. Should you do me the honour to again elect me as your representative, it will be my study in the future, as in the past, to do all that I can to promote the trade and prosperity of my native town, and of Orford Ward in particular. I I am well aware that in my three years in the Council I could not have done all I would wish for the Ward, with regard to the paving ar.d lighting of our streets, but I will do all I can to have the streets made and lighted as new houses are built, and also to have a fair wage clause inserted in all Corporation contracts. I am of opinion that the rates, and especially those for gas, are far too high, considering what they are in neighbour- ing towns, and any scheme for reducing them,with due regard to efficiency,shall have my support. I have the honour to be, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient servant, JOSEPH WILKINSON. 60, Longford-street, Warrington, October 23rd, 1895.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL ELECTION, NOVEMBER IST, 1895. TO THE ELECTORS OF ST. JOHN'S WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— Having been invited by a large and influential number of the Electors of this Ward, to offer myself as a Candidate for the vacancy which occurs on the Ist November, I do so feeling that I have the confidence and good wishes of the majority of the electors. I am in favour of making prevision for, and encouraging the laying down and constructing of Tramways through our principal streets, as I am of opinion that this would tend to Improve the general trade of our town. If this were brought about it would have the effect of increasing the number of re- sidents and would give employment to all (fiascos of trades Interested in building and constructive operations. It will be my endeavour,as far as lam able,to bring about the widening of the various streets, so long under considera tion, as early as possible. I believe that the sooner this Improvement is carried out, the less expensive it will be. 4 am also in favour of all work being, when practicable, carried out by local workmen, and full trade prices being paid for the same, I shall at all times do what I can to assist manufacturers and traders to develope their business so that we may have a greater number of works in our midst and, therefore, be in a position to give more employment to all classes of work- people. I am of opinion that the Warrington Town Council has not given that encouragement to local industries which It is in Its power to give. The Sanitary condition oi our town is very much im. I proved but leaves very much to be desired, and I am of opinicn that the amount of sickness that has been prevalent, In this Ward in particular, is caused by the foul vapours that from time to time emanate from our sewers. I shall endea- vour to get some remedy put into practical operation that will remove all these obj ectionable vapours. If you do me the honour to elect me it will be my earnest endeavour to give special attention to everything relating to the interests of this Borough. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, WILLIAM FURNESS, Haydock Street, Warrington. October 11th, 1895.</p>
